/* The heavy half of the pea cards — GENERATED, never hand-edited.
 *
 * abstract / sources / why / quote / image, for the cards whose ids begin `pea-`. None of it is
 * read until a reader REVEALS a card in this collection, so it is fetched then (bundle
 * `cardExtra:pea`) rather than downloaded by every visitor before they can flip one.
 *
 * IT STAGES ONTO A QUEUE rather than assigning, for the reason glossary-extra.js does: app.js
 * snapshots PRISTINE_CARDS at boot, which is BEFORE this file lands, so a plain assignment would
 * leave the editor's revert baseline empty and Revert would DELETE a shipped abstract rather than
 * restore it. cardExtraIngest drains the queue and re-seeds that baseline.
 *
 * An id data.js does not carry is not resurrected — the light half decides what a card is. */
(function () {
  var CARD_EXTRA = {
    "pea-001": {"abstract":"The <b>Tiananmen Massacre</b> was the violent suppression of the 1989 democracy movement, when the People's Liberation Army forced its way into central Beijing on the night of 3–4 June and cleared the square.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The protests had begun in mid-April, after the death of the reform-minded former General Secretary Hu Yaobang gave students a pretext to gather in public mourning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over seven weeks the movement swelled far beyond the campuses, drawing in workers, journalists and ordinary citizens across dozens of cities, while its demands widened from an honest press and an end to official corruption towards political liberalisation itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Martial law was declared on 20 May, and when unarmed crowds blocked the first troop columns the leadership ordered a second and far heavier advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The death toll has never been established, and estimates ranging from several hundred to several thousand are part of why the subject remains among the most tightly censored in China. <br><br> For the politics of the PRC the massacre matters most for what followed it. General Secretary Zhao Ziyang, who had gone to the square and opposed the crackdown, was stripped of his posts and spent the remaining 15 years of his life under house arrest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Economic reform then stalled for roughly three years, until Deng Xiaoping broke the deadlock with his southern tour of 1992 and the party committed itself to building a socialist market economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The episode left a lasting problem of legitimacy, because a ruling party that had just fired on its own citizens could no longer rest its claim on revolutionary virtue alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its answer was the bargain that has shaped Chinese politics ever since: relentless economic growth, paired with a patriotic education campaign that rebuilt loyalty around a century of national humiliation at foreign hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Craig Calhoun, “Revolution and repression in Tiananmen square,” <i>Society</i> 26, no. 6 (1989): 21–38, https://doi.org/10.1007/bf02700237. Free copy at https://researchonline.lse.ac.uk/id/eprint/42467/1/Calhoun_Revolution_repression_Tianmen_1989.pdf. [Open access]","Margaret M. Pearson, Meg Rithmire, and Kellee S. Tsai, “The State and Capitalism in China,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2023), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009356732. [Open access]","William A. Callahan, “National Insecurities: Humiliation, Salvation, and Chinese Nationalism,” <i>Alternatives Global Local Political</i> 29, no. 2 (2004): 199–218, https://doi.org/10.1177/030437540402900204. Free copy at https://ink.library.smu.edu.sg/context/soss_research/article/5592/viewcontent/Callahan_NatInsecurities_China_pv.pdf. [Open access]","Christopher R. Hughes, “Chinese Nationalism in the Global Era,” (2006), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203482001. Free copy at http://eprints.lse.ac.uk/23027/1/Chinese_nationalism_in_the_global_era%28LSERO%29.pdf. [Open access]","Johannes Gerschewski, “Legitimacy in Autocracies: Oxymoron or Essential Feature?,” <i>Perspectives on Politics</i> 16, no. 3 (2018): 652–665, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1537592717002183. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the crackdown freeze economic reform rather than accelerate it?","a":"Because the conservatives who had always distrusted the market used the unrest as proof that liberalisation bred disorder, and they held the initiative for roughly three years until Deng Xiaoping broke the deadlock with his southern tour in 1992."},{"q":"Why could the party no longer rest its claim on revolutionary virtue?","a":"Because it had just fired on the citizens it claimed to represent, which made the old revolutionary story unusable as a reason to obey, so a new basis for loyalty had to be found."},{"q":"Why teach a century of humiliation rather than Marxism?","a":"Because nationalism supplies a loyalty that survives the abandonment of Marxist economics: it casts the party as the force that restored national dignity, and it shifts the test of good rule away from freedoms and towards strength."}]},
    "pea-002": {"abstract":"The <b>Southern Tour</b> was a journey Deng Xiaoping made through southern China in the first weeks of 1992, and it stands among the decisive moments in the country's modern political history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He held no formal leadership post, having surrendered his last office in 1989, so the tour was an exercise in personal authority rather than institutional power. His route ran through the special economic zones of Shenzhen and Zhuhai and on to Guangzhou and Shanghai, the places where market experiments had gone furthest and where their results were most visible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those zones had been created as deliberate experiments, walled off from the planned economy so that a policy could be tried in one place before anyone risked extending it to the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His target was the conservative wing of the leadership, which had used the Tiananmen crackdown to argue that market reform was politically dangerous and had frozen it for some three years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Behind that argument lay a fear shared across the leadership, that China might follow the Soviet Union into stagnation and then collapse, and Deng's answer was that only sustained growth could prevent it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His remarks along the way were blunt, warning that anyone who would not promote reform should be brought down from a position of leadership. Provincial newspapers in the south carried his words before the central press did, and the pressure this generated forced the new General Secretary, Jiang Zemin, to adopt Deng's position as his own. Later that year the 14th Party Congress formally committed the country to building a socialist market economy, and foreign investment into the coastal provinces climbed steeply thereafter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The tour therefore set the course of the following two decades, locking in rapid marketisation while leaving the party's political monopoly entirely untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Margaret M. Pearson, Meg Rithmire, and Kellee S. Tsai, “The State and Capitalism in China,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2023), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009356732. [Open access]","Simon Alder, Lin Shao, and Fabrizio Zilibotti, “Economic reforms and industrial policy in a panel of Chinese cities,” <i>Journal of Economic Growth</i> 21, no. 4 (2016): 305–349, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10887-016-9131-x. [Open access]","Sebastian Heilmann, “Policy Experimentation in China's Economic Rise,” <i>Studies in Comparative International Development</i> 43, no. 1 (2007): 1–26, https://doi.org/10.1007/s12116-007-9014-4. [Open access]","Shaun Breslin and Giuseppe Gabusi, “‘Whatever It Takes’: The Political Economy of the Chinese Communist Party,” <i>ANU Press eBooks</i> (2024): 207–232, https://doi.org/10.22459/ccp.2024.08. [Open access]","Shaun Breslin, “Foreign direct investment in the People's Republic of China: preferences, policies and performance,” <i>Warwick Research Archive Portal</i> (2006), http://wrap.warwick.ac.uk/282/1/WRAP_Breslin_breslinfdifinal.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could Deng dictate policy while holding no office at all?","a":"Because authority in Chinese politics has never mapped neatly onto formal titles: his standing as the surviving architect of reform, and his network among the party elders and the military, gave him a veto that no post conferred."},{"q":"Why did the tour begin in the special economic zones?","a":"Because the zones were where market policy had gone furthest, so their visible prosperity made the case for reform in a form that could be seen rather than argued, which is far harder for an opponent to answer."},{"q":"Why did the Soviet collapse push Deng towards more reform rather than less?","a":"Because he read the Soviet failure as economic rather than political: a party that could not raise living standards would eventually lose its people, so rapid growth became the guarantee of survival rather than a threat to it."}]},
    "pea-003": {"abstract":"<b>Collective leadership</b> is the principle that supreme authority in the Chinese Communist Party belongs to a small committee acting by consensus rather than to a single ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deng Xiaoping pressed it on the party as a direct answer to the Mao era, when one man's authority had been beyond challenge and the resulting campaigns had cost millions of lives. In practice the Politburo Standing Committee, a body of between five and nine members, took the major decisions together, and the General Secretary was treated as first among equals rather than as a commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Around that core grew a set of supporting conventions, most importantly a mandatory retirement age and the two-term limit on the state chairmanship written into the constitution in 1982.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Out of these came a clear expectation of an orderly generational handover roughly every ten years. <br><br> Such arrangements are unusual in an authoritarian state, where the succession is normally the moment of greatest danger, and many observers credited them with much of China's long-term stability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Jiang Zemin's transfer of power to Hu Jintao in 2002 and 2003 was widely regarded as a genuine achievement, and Hu's own handover a decade later seemed to confirm that the norm had taken hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The system carried real costs all the same, since consensus rule made decisive action difficult and let factions, entrenched interests and corruption spread through the party and the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Xi Jinping has dismantled it since 2012 through a dramatic centralisation of authority, the growth of a personality cult and the abolition of the term limit in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What remains unsettled is the very question the norm existed to answer, because a system without a rule for succession has no agreed way to replace a leader who fails.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Shiping Zheng, “Leadership change, legitimacy, and party transition in China,” <i>Journal of Chinese Political Science</i> 8, no. 1–2 (2003): 47–63, https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02876949. Free copy at https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF02876949.pdf. [Open access]","Margaret M. Pearson, Meg Rithmire, and Kellee S. Tsai, “The State and Capitalism in China,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2023), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009356732. [Open access]","Johannes Gerschewski, “Legitimacy in Autocracies: Oxymoron or Essential Feature?,” <i>Perspectives on Politics</i> 16, no. 3 (2018): 652–665, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1537592717002183. [Open access]","Amy H. Liu, “Pronoun Usage as a Measure of Power Personalization: A General Theory with Evidence from the Chinese-Speaking World,” <i>British Journal of Political Science</i> 52, no. 3 (2021): 1258–1275, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0007123421000181. [Open access]","Shaun Breslin and Giuseppe Gabusi, “‘Whatever It Takes’: The Political Economy of the Chinese Communist Party,” <i>ANU Press eBooks</i> (2024): 207–232, https://doi.org/10.22459/ccp.2024.08. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a succession rule so valuable to an authoritarian regime?","a":"Because without one the death or decline of a leader invites a struggle that can split the ruling group and bring the regime down, so a predictable handover turns the most dangerous moment in autocratic politics into a routine one."},{"q":"Why did rule by consensus make corruption so hard to tackle?","a":"Because decisions needed agreement among leaders who each protected their own patronage networks, so any measure threatening one faction could be blocked by it, and the reforms that survived tended to be those that hurt nobody."},{"q":"Why was abolishing the term limit read as more than a technical change?","a":"Because that limit was the most visible guarantee that no individual could ever repeat Mao, so removing it signalled that the restraints Deng built after the Mao era were being deliberately undone rather than merely adjusted."}]},
    "pea-004": {"abstract":"The <b>nomenklatura</b> system is the mechanism through which the Chinese Communist Party controls who holds every position of consequence in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word is Russian and the practice was borrowed from the Soviet Union, where it meant a list of posts together with the list of vetted people eligible to fill them. In China it reaches far beyond the government itself, covering the leadership of state enterprises, banks, universities, hospitals, newspapers and officially sanctioned social organisations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Central Organisation Department administers the most senior of these appointments, and equivalent departments operate at provincial, municipal and county level for the posts within their reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The scale is enormous, since the party-state employs tens of millions of people whose careers run through this machinery rather than through any professional hierarchy of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Because promotion depends on party superiors, the incentive of every ambitious official is to satisfy them rather than the public or the institution being run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Performance targets have been used to sharpen that incentive, so that a local leader's prospects turn on measurable results such as growth, investment and, more recently, pollution and social stability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The arrangement gives the leadership real reach into a vast country, and it is one reason policy can be turned quickly once the centre has decided on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It also produces the pathologies familiar from every communist system, above all the distortion of information as subordinates report what their superiors wish to hear. Deng Xiaoping's reforms tried to temper this with a professional civil service recruited by examination alongside the party appointees, but Xi Jinping's insistence that the party leads everything has pushed the balance firmly back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Pierre F. Landry, “The Political Management of Mayors in Post-Deng China,” <i>The Copenhagen Journal of Asian Studies</i> 17 (2003): 31–58, https://doi.org/10.22439/cjas.v17i0.12. [Open access]","Yuen Yuen Ang, “Counting Cadres: A Comparative View of the Size of China's Public Employment,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 211 (2012): 676–696, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741012000884. [Open access]","Christopher Heurlin, “Responsive Authoritarianism in China,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2016), https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9781316443019. [Open access]","Margaret M. Pearson, Meg Rithmire, and Kellee S. Tsai, “The State and Capitalism in China,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2023), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009356732. [Open access]","Shaun Breslin and Giuseppe Gabusi, “‘Whatever It Takes’: The Political Economy of the Chinese Communist Party,” <i>ANU Press eBooks</i> (2024): 207–232, https://doi.org/10.22459/ccp.2024.08. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the party appoint university and newspaper heads at all?","a":"Because control of personnel is how a Leninist party rules without taking every decision itself: whoever appoints an institution's leadership sets its limits, so the party can steer hospitals, banks and editors without issuing instructions to any of them."},{"q":"Why do performance targets distort as much as they motivate?","a":"Because officials are judged on what can be measured, so effort flows towards the indicator rather than the outcome: growth figures and investment totals get inflated, or are pursued at the expense of everything nobody is counting."},{"q":"Why did professionalising the bureaucracy not fix the problem?","a":"Because the party kept the appointments that mattered, so a merit-recruited civil servant still answered to a party appointee above them, and the reform added expertise without moving the authority it was meant to check."}]},
    "pea-005": {"abstract":"The <b>People's Liberation Army</b> is the armed force of the People's Republic of China and, with roughly two million active personnel, the largest standing military in the world. Its defining feature for the study of Chinese politics is that it belongs to the party rather than to the state, a relationship captured in Mao Zedong's maxim that the party commands the gun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Command runs formally through two Central Military Commissions, one answering to the Central Committee of the party and one to the Standing Committee of the National People's Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two bodies have effectively identical membership and the same chairman, so the state commission is best understood as a constitutional formality laid over genuine party control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Political commissars are embedded throughout the command structure, paralleling the chain of officers and charged with the army's ideological loyalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The arrangement is built against a dilemma every authoritarian ruler faces, since an army strong enough to defend the state is also strong enough to overthrow it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It explains why the chairmanship of the Central Military Commission is treated as the most important of the three offices a paramount leader holds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The army has intervened in domestic politics at moments of crisis, most notoriously in clearing central Beijing in June 1989, an order it carried out only after real internal resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under Xi Jinping it has undergone its most sweeping reorganisation in decades, the old military regions giving way to five theatre commands and new services being raised for rocket and support forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same years have seen senior officers repeatedly removed for corruption, which serves both to clean up a notoriously venal promotion market and to secure the loyalty of those who remain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Daniel Mattingly, “How the Party Commands the Gun: The Foreign–Domestic Threat Dilemma in China,” <i>American Journal of Political Science</i> 68, no. 1 (2022): 227–242, https://doi.org/10.1111/ajps.12739. [Open access]","David Shambaugh, “Civil-Military Relations in China: Party-Army or National Military?,” <i>The Copenhagen Journal of Asian Studies</i> 16 (2002): 10–29, https://doi.org/10.22439/cjas.v16i0.3. Free copy at https://rauli.cbs.dk/index.php/cjas/article/download/3/3. [Open access]","James Char and Richard A. Bitzinger, “A New Direction in the People's Liberation Army's Emergent Strategic Thinking, Roles and Missions,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 232 (2017): 841–865, https://doi.org/10.1017/s030574101700128x. [Open access]","Craig Calhoun, “Revolution and repression in Tiananmen square,” <i>Society</i> 26, no. 6 (1989): 21–38, https://doi.org/10.1007/bf02700237. Free copy at https://researchonline.lse.ac.uk/id/eprint/42467/1/Calhoun_Revolution_repression_Tianmen_1989.pdf. [Open access]","Frank N. Pieke and Bert Hofman, “CPC Futures: The New Era of Socialism with Chinese Characteristics,” <i>East Asian Institute, NUS eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.56159/eai.52060. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the army answer to the party rather than the state?","a":"Because the party built the army before it held the state, and keeping that line of command means the armed forces are loyal to an organisation rather than to a constitution, which removes the option of an army defending the country against the party."},{"q":"Why is the military chairmanship the office that really matters?","a":"Because it carries command of the only instrument that can settle a dispute by force, which is why Deng Xiaoping ruled through it while holding nothing else, and why a predecessor who keeps it leaves his successor visibly incomplete."},{"q":"Why purge generals and claim to be strengthening the army?","a":"Because a promotion market in which commands were bought produced officers chosen for their payments rather than their competence, so removing them can genuinely improve the force while also replacing anyone whose loyalty is uncertain."}]},
    "pea-006": {"abstract":"<b>Xi Jinping</b> has led China since 2012 and has reshaped its politics more thoroughly than any leader since Deng Xiaoping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is a princeling, son of the revolutionary veteran Xi Zhongxun, and he built a conventional provincial career as governor of Fujian and then party chief of Zhejiang before entering the Politburo Standing Committee in 2007. Designated Hu Jintao's successor in 2008, he was widely expected to be another cautious consensus manager, and was quickly shown to be nothing of the kind. His central project has been the concentration of authority in his own hands, through commissions and leading groups that he chairs personally and that bypass the ordinary machinery of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shift is visible even in the language of official politics, where the personal register of a single leader has displaced the collective voice of an institution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A sweeping anti-corruption campaign has disciplined millions of officials and removed a series of powerful rivals, serving at once as genuine housekeeping and as political consolidation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Alongside it has come a reassertion of the party over business, universities, civil society, religion and the flow of information across the country's borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The 19th Party Congress of October 2017 proclaimed a New Era and wrote his name into the party constitution, an honour Mao Zedong received in his lifetime and Deng Xiaoping never did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 2018 the two-term limit on the state chairmanship was removed, ending the orderly succession norm that had underpinned three decades of stability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That leaves unanswered the very question the norm existed to settle, since no successor has been groomed and no rule now governs what happens when he goes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Dimitar D. Gueorguiev, “Dictator's Shadow,” <i>China Perspectives</i> 2018, no. 1–2 (2018): 17–26, https://doi.org/10.4000/chinaperspectives.7569. [Open access]","Frank N. Pieke and Bert Hofman, “CPC Futures: The New Era of Socialism with Chinese Characteristics,” <i>East Asian Institute, NUS eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.56159/eai.52060. [Open access]","Amy H. Liu, “Pronoun Usage as a Measure of Power Personalization: A General Theory with Evidence from the Chinese-Speaking World,” <i>British Journal of Political Science</i> 52, no. 3 (2021): 1258–1275, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0007123421000181. [Open access]","Ting Chen and James Kai-sing Kung, “Busting the ‘Princelings’: The Campaign Against Corruption in China's Primary Land Market,” <i>The Quarterly Journal of Economics</i> 134, no. 1 (2018): 185–226, https://doi.org/10.1093/qje/qjy027. [Open access]","Shiping Zheng, “Leadership change, legitimacy, and party transition in China,” <i>Journal of Chinese Political Science</i> 8, no. 1–2 (2003): 47–63, https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02876949. Free copy at https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF02876949.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did observers expect Xi to be a consensus manager?","a":"Because his provincial career had been unremarkable and deliberately inoffensive, and because the two leaders before him had both governed by balancing factions, so the system appeared to select for caution rather than for ambition."},{"q":"Why do leading groups matter more than ministries under Xi?","a":"Because a commission he chairs personally reports to him rather than to the State Council, so policy is made in bodies he controls directly and the premier is left implementing decisions rather than shaping them."},{"q":"Why can an anti-corruption drive be honest and self-serving at once?","a":"Because corruption was genuinely widespread, so almost any official could be investigated truthfully, which means the choice of whom to pursue is itself an exercise of power even when every charge is sound."}]},
    "pea-007": {"abstract":"<b>Patriotic Education</b> is the long-running ideological campaign through which the Chinese Communist Party grounded its right to rule in nationalism rather than in Marxist doctrine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was launched in the early 1990s in answer to a double shock: the loss of moral authority after the Tiananmen crackdown, and the collapse of communist rule across Eastern Europe and the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The leadership concluded that young Chinese had been left ideologically adrift and dangerously receptive to Western liberal ideas, and that a new basis for loyalty was urgently needed. The campaign was formalised in 1994 and reached into textbooks, museums, war memorials, films and a network of designated patriotic education bases across the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its organising narrative is the century of humiliation running from the Opium Wars to 1949, during which a weak China was carved up by foreign powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The political work that narrative performs is to recast the party not as the bearer of a failed utopian ideology but as the force that restored national dignity and unity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also shifts the standard by which citizens judge their rulers away from freedoms and towards strength, sovereignty and the recovery of lost territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Maps do much of the teaching, since a cartography of lost lands makes the injury visible and fixes in the mind a national body felt to be incomplete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The strategy carries a real risk for the leadership, because a public taught to feel humiliated can demand a harder line abroad than the government wishes to take, and any perceived weakness then becomes a vulnerability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It has been extended and toughened under Xi Jinping, for whom national rejuvenation is the organising promise of the New Era.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["William A. Callahan, “National Insecurities: Humiliation, Salvation, and Chinese Nationalism,” <i>Alternatives Global Local Political</i> 29, no. 2 (2004): 199–218, https://doi.org/10.1177/030437540402900204. Free copy at https://ink.library.smu.edu.sg/context/soss_research/article/5592/viewcontent/Callahan_NatInsecurities_China_pv.pdf. [Open access]","William A. Callahan, “The Cartography of National Humiliation and the Emergence of China's Geobody,” <i>Public Culture</i> 21, no. 1 (2009): 141–173, https://doi.org/10.1215/08992363-2008-024. [Open access]","Christopher R. Hughes, “Chinese Nationalism in the Global Era,” (2006), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203482001. Free copy at http://eprints.lse.ac.uk/23027/1/Chinese_nationalism_in_the_global_era%28LSERO%29.pdf. [Open access]","Johannes Gerschewski, “Legitimacy in Autocracies: Oxymoron or Essential Feature?,” <i>Perspectives on Politics</i> 16, no. 3 (2018): 652–665, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1537592717002183. [Open access]","Frank N. Pieke and Bert Hofman, “CPC Futures: The New Era of Socialism with Chinese Characteristics,” <i>East Asian Institute, NUS eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.56159/eai.52060. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the party turn to nationalism rather than repairing Marxism?","a":"Because the economy it was building was visibly a market one, so Marxist doctrine could no longer describe what the party was actually doing, while nationalism explains and justifies the same policies without contradiction."},{"q":"Why does teaching humiliation constrain the government that teaches it?","a":"Because a public trained to read foreign slights as national injuries will judge its own leaders by how firmly they answer them, so a compromise abroad can be attacked at home as a betrayal."},{"q":"Why do maps appear so often in this teaching?","a":"Because a map of territory once lost turns an abstract grievance into something the eye can take in at once, and it defines the nation as a shape that is not yet whole, which makes recovery feel owed."}]},
    "pea-008": {"abstract":"<b>Shenzhen</b> lies in the south of Guangdong province on the border with Hong Kong, and it is the place where the reform era is most visibly written on the landscape. It was designated one of the first special economic zones in 1980, on a stretch of the Pearl River Delta chosen precisely because it adjoined Hong Kong and could draw on that city's capital, management and access to world markets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The zone offered foreign investors tax concessions, simplified customs and freedom from much of the planned economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its real purpose was to serve as a laboratory, a place where market practices could be tried at a manageable scale before anyone risked extending them to the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Growth was astonishing, turning a county of a few hundred thousand people into a metropolis of roughly 17.5 million with a technology industry that now includes Huawei, Tencent and BYD.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Its political significance is inseparable from that economic record, because the zone made the case for reform in a form that could be seen rather than argued. That is why Deng Xiaoping chose it as the first stop of his southern tour in 1992, using the visible prosperity of the place as a rebuke to the conservatives who had frozen reform after 1989.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The experiment was also a political device in a second sense, since a policy confined to one city could be abandoned quietly if it failed and generalised loudly if it worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The relationship with its neighbour has since inverted, as Shenzhen has overtaken Hong Kong in economic output and in technological weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The geography is worth locating precisely, because the position of the Pearl River Delta explains much of why reform began where it did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Shaun Breslin, “Foreign direct investment in the People's Republic of China: preferences, policies and performance,” <i>Warwick Research Archive Portal</i> (2006), http://wrap.warwick.ac.uk/282/1/WRAP_Breslin_breslinfdifinal.pdf. [Open access]","Margaret M. Pearson, Meg Rithmire, and Kellee S. Tsai, “The State and Capitalism in China,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2023), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009356732. [Open access]","Sebastian Heilmann, “Policy Experimentation in China's Economic Rise,” <i>Studies in Comparative International Development</i> 43, no. 1 (2007): 1–26, https://doi.org/10.1007/s12116-007-9014-4. [Open access]","Simon Alder, Lin Shao, and Fabrizio Zilibotti, “Economic reforms and industrial policy in a panel of Chinese cities,” <i>Journal of Economic Growth</i> 21, no. 4 (2016): 305–349, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10887-016-9131-x. [Open access]","Shaun Breslin and Giuseppe Gabusi, “‘Whatever It Takes’: The Political Economy of the Chinese Communist Party,” <i>ANU Press eBooks</i> (2024): 207–232, https://doi.org/10.22459/ccp.2024.08. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why put the first zone on the Hong Kong border rather than inland?","a":"Because the zone needed what China then lacked: investors, managers who understood export markets and buyers abroad. Hong Kong supplied all three within an hour of the site, so the experiment could borrow a working economy rather than build one."},{"q":"Why did confining reform to one city make it politically safer?","a":"Because a policy tried in a single place can be dropped quietly if it fails, so the leadership risked no national commitment, while a success becomes a visible argument that opponents must answer on the evidence."},{"q":"Why did Deng open his 1992 tour here rather than in Beijing?","a":"Because the case he needed to make was about results rather than doctrine, and in Shenzhen the results were standing in front of him, which is a harder thing for a conservative opponent to argue against than a theory."}]},
    "pea-009": {"abstract":"The <b>Central Military Commission</b> exercises supreme command over the Chinese armed forces, and its curious double existence is one of the clearest illustrations of how the party-state actually works.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One commission sits under the party's Central Committee and the other under the Standing Committee of the National People's Congress, yet they are staffed by the same people and chaired by the same man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state version was created in 1982 to give constitutional respectability to an arrangement in which the army answered to a political party rather than to the nation. Nothing of substance flows from the state body, and the party commission is where authority genuinely lies, which is why the party chairmanship is always acquired first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its chairman holds direct command, assisted by two uniformed vice-chairmen and members drawn from the services and the senior departments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The chairmanship is the single most important office in the Chinese system, since whoever holds it commands the ultimate instrument of coercion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Deng Xiaoping demonstrated the point by retaining it until 1989 while holding no other leading position, and by using it to direct the response to the Tiananmen protests. Jiang Zemin kept it for two years after surrendering the presidency, a manoeuvre that left Hu Jintao's authority incomplete and is remembered as a cautionary tale about a half-finished succession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hu avoided repeating it by transferring every office to Xi Jinping at once, which gave his successor an unusually strong start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under Xi the commission has been substantially restructured, its general departments broken up into fifteen smaller organs reporting to him directly, concentrating military authority more tightly than at any time since Mao.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["David Shambaugh, “Civil-Military Relations in China: Party-Army or National Military?,” <i>The Copenhagen Journal of Asian Studies</i> 16 (2002): 10–29, https://doi.org/10.22439/cjas.v16i0.3. Free copy at https://rauli.cbs.dk/index.php/cjas/article/download/3/3. [Open access]","Daniel Mattingly, “How the Party Commands the Gun: The Foreign–Domestic Threat Dilemma in China,” <i>American Journal of Political Science</i> 68, no. 1 (2022): 227–242, https://doi.org/10.1111/ajps.12739. [Open access]","James Char and Richard A. Bitzinger, “A New Direction in the People's Liberation Army's Emergent Strategic Thinking, Roles and Missions,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 232 (2017): 841–865, https://doi.org/10.1017/s030574101700128x. [Open access]","Shiping Zheng, “Leadership change, legitimacy, and party transition in China,” <i>Journal of Chinese Political Science</i> 8, no. 1–2 (2003): 47–63, https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02876949. Free copy at https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF02876949.pdf. [Open access]","Dimitar D. Gueorguiev, “Dictator's Shadow,” <i>China Perspectives</i> 2018, no. 1–2 (2018): 17–26, https://doi.org/10.4000/chinaperspectives.7569. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why create a state commission that decides nothing?","a":"Because a constitution that never mentions the army looks like an admission that the state does not control it, so the state body supplies the appearance of national command while the party body keeps the substance of it."},{"q":"Why acquire the party chairmanship before the state one?","a":"Because the party commission is where command actually sits, so taking it first means holding real authority from the outset, while the state appointment that follows is a formality the legislature performs afterwards."},{"q":"Why did keeping this office weaken the next leader rather than the last?","a":"Because a successor who holds the party and the state but not the army commands everything except the thing that settles disputes by force, so officials hedge, knowing the retired man can still overrule him."}]},
    "pea-010": {"abstract":"The <b>Politburo Standing Committee</b> is the small group at the apex of Chinese politics and is best understood as the country's real cabinet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its size has varied between five and nine and currently stands at seven, each member holding a portfolio covering a major area such as the legislature, the government, ideology or discipline inspection. It is chaired by the General Secretary and is believed to meet roughly every two weeks, with discussion open among members and decisions reached by consensus rather than by formal vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost everything of consequence is settled here before being presented to the wider Politburo of around two dozen members for endorsement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Selection is formally the business of the Central Committee, but the membership is negotiated among the outgoing leadership and the retired elders, and the Central Committee ratifies what it is handed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Secrecy is near total, since no minutes are published, no dissent is ever disclosed and the body presents a face of complete unity to the outside world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Analysts therefore fall back on indirect evidence such as the order in which members are listed, the portfolios they receive and their ages at appointment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Personal ties matter a great deal more than the meritocratic account allows, since shared hometown, school and workplace connections to a patron measurably improve an official's chances of rising this far.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No woman has ever sat on it in the party's entire history, and after the 20th Congress of 2022 there was for the first time since the 1990s no woman even in the full Politburo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its character has changed markedly under Xi Jinping, shifting from a council of relative equals towards a body of his own allies whose collective voice has given way to a personal one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Shiping Zheng, “Leadership change, legitimacy, and party transition in China,” <i>Journal of Chinese Political Science</i> 8, no. 1–2 (2003): 47–63, https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02876949. Free copy at https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF02876949.pdf. [Open access]","Raymond Fisman et al., “Social Ties and the Selection of China's Political Elite,” <i>American Economic Review</i> 110, no. 6 (2020): 1752–1781, https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20180841. [Open access]","Dimitar D. Gueorguiev, “Dictator's Shadow,” <i>China Perspectives</i> 2018, no. 1–2 (2018): 17–26, https://doi.org/10.4000/chinaperspectives.7569. [Open access]","Frank N. Pieke and Bert Hofman, “CPC Futures: The New Era of Socialism with Chinese Characteristics,” <i>East Asian Institute, NUS eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.56159/eai.52060. [Open access]","Amy H. Liu, “Pronoun Usage as a Measure of Power Personalization: A General Theory with Evidence from the Chinese-Speaking World,” <i>British Journal of Political Science</i> 52, no. 3 (2021): 1258–1275, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0007123421000181. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why watch the order in which members are listed?","a":"Because the ranking is one of the very few facts the party publishes about a body that discloses nothing else, and it encodes seniority and portfolio, so a change in the order signals a change in standing that no announcement will confirm."},{"q":"Why does the Central Committee elect a slate it did not choose?","a":"Because the bargaining happens beforehand among the people leaving office and the elders who still carry weight, so the vote confers legitimacy on a settlement already reached rather than deciding anything itself."},{"q":"Why do personal ties undercut the meritocracy argument?","a":"Because if shared hometown, school or workplace connections to a patron predict promotion, then advancement is measuring loyalty and network as much as competence, which is precisely what a meritocratic account denies."}]},
    "pea-011": {"abstract":"The <b>anti-corruption campaign</b> has been the signature domestic instrument of Xi Jinping's rule, begun almost immediately after he took office and sustained far longer than anyone expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its slogan promised to catch tigers and flies alike, meaning that senior leaders would be pursued as relentlessly as petty local officials, which broke the old pattern of protecting the very top. The scale has been extraordinary, with millions of officials investigated and punished across the party, the state bureaucracy, the state enterprises and the armed forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The most senior target was Zhou Yongkang, a former member of the Politburo Standing Committee who had overseen the security apparatus and was jailed for life in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two former vice-chairmen of the Central Military Commission were brought down as well, along with the ambitious Chongqing leader Bo Xilai, whose fall removed the one rival with a public following of his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The campaign addresses a genuine problem, since corruption had grown severe enough by the end of the Hu Jintao decade to worry the leadership about the party's own survival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Studies of the land market find real effects, with the advantage once enjoyed by politically connected buyers measurably reduced where the campaign reached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is nonetheless inseparable from the consolidation of personal power, because in a system where nearly every official is vulnerable the choice of whom to investigate is itself the exercise of power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those purged have included a striking number of figures tied to rival factions, while allies have largely been spared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its methods remain deeply troubling, since detainees can be held incommunicado for months outside the ordinary legal system, without a lawyer and under heavy pressure to confess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Ting Chen and James Kai-sing Kung, “Busting the ‘Princelings’: The Campaign Against Corruption in China's Primary Land Market,” <i>The Quarterly Journal of Economics</i> 134, no. 1 (2018): 185–226, https://doi.org/10.1093/qje/qjy027. [Open access]","Dimitar D. Gueorguiev, “Dictator's Shadow,” <i>China Perspectives</i> 2018, no. 1–2 (2018): 17–26, https://doi.org/10.4000/chinaperspectives.7569. [Open access]","Frank N. Pieke and Bert Hofman, “CPC Futures: The New Era of Socialism with Chinese Characteristics,” <i>East Asian Institute, NUS eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.56159/eai.52060. [Open access]","Daniel Mattingly, “How the Party Commands the Gun: The Foreign–Domestic Threat Dilemma in China,” <i>American Journal of Political Science</i> 68, no. 1 (2022): 227–242, https://doi.org/10.1111/ajps.12739. [Open access]","Pierre F. Landry, “The Political Management of Mayors in Post-Deng China,” <i>The Copenhagen Journal of Asian Studies</i> 17 (2003): 31–58, https://doi.org/10.22439/cjas.v17i0.12. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a genuine campaign still serve a factional purpose?","a":"Because corruption was widespread enough that almost any official could be investigated truthfully, so the discretion over whom to pursue becomes a weapon even when every individual charge is sound and every conviction deserved."},{"q":"Why did jailing Zhou Yongkang matter beyond the man himself?","a":"Because an unwritten rule had held that serving or retired members of the Standing Committee were beyond prosecution, so breaking it removed the guarantee of safety in retirement that had made peaceful handovers possible."},{"q":"Why do the campaign's methods worry lawyers as much as its targets?","a":"Because party investigators can hold a detainee for months outside the ordinary legal system, without a lawyer and under pressure to confess, so guilt is effectively settled before any court sees the case."}]},
    "pea-012": {"abstract":"The <b>Belt and Road Initiative</b> is China's global infrastructure and investment programme, announced by Xi Jinping in 2013 and since joined in some form by roughly 150 countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name combines an overland belt of road, rail and pipeline routes across Central Asia towards Europe with a maritime road of ports linking the South China Sea to the Indian Ocean and East Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The branding deliberately invokes the ancient Silk Road, presenting Chinese expansion abroad as the revival of an old and peaceful commercial tradition rather than as anything new.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The economic logic at home was straightforward, since Chinese construction, steel and cement industries carried enormous surplus capacity after years of domestic building, and foreign projects gave that capacity somewhere to go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Financing has come largely as loans from Chinese policy banks, usually tied to contracts for Chinese firms and Chinese labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The political purposes are equally clear, building influence across the developing world and securing the energy and shipping routes that would otherwise be vulnerable to interruption.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Critics have accused China of debt-trap diplomacy, pointing to Sri Lanka's handover of the Hambantota port on a 99-year lease after it could not service its obligations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A careful assessment of the lending finds a narrower problem than the slogan implies, with serious debt risk concentrated in a handful of borrowing countries rather than spread across the programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Other criticisms are harder to dismiss, including opaque contracts, corruption, environmental damage and terms that deepen a borrower's dependence rather than transforming its economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The initiative is also a claim about world order, offering an alternative to the Western-led institutions and a different account of how development ought to be financed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Peter Ferdinand, “Westward ho — the China dream and ‘one belt, one road’: Chinese foreign policy under Xi Jinping,” <i>International Affairs</i> 92, no. 4 (2016): 941–957, https://doi.org/10.1111/1468-2346.12660. [Open access]","John Hurley, Scott Morris, and Gailyn Portelance, “Examining the debt implications of the Belt and Road Initiative from a policy perspective,” <i>Journal of Infrastructure Policy and Development</i> 3, no. 1 (2019): 139, https://doi.org/10.24294/jipd.v3i1.1123. [Open access]","Lauren Johnston, “The Belt and Road Initiative: What is in it for China?,” <i>Asia & the Pacific Policy Studies</i> 6, no. 1 (2018): 40–58, https://doi.org/10.1002/app5.265. [Open access]","Pádraig Carmody, Ian Taylor, and Tim Zajontz, “China's spatial fix and ‘debt diplomacy’ in Africa: constraining belt or road to economic transformation?,” <i>Canadian Journal of African Studies</i> 56, no. 1 (2021): 57–77, https://doi.org/10.1080/00083968.2020.1868014. [Open access]","Astrid Nordin and Mikael Weissmann, “Will Trump make China great again? The belt and road initiative and international order,” <i>International Affairs</i> 94, no. 2 (2018): 231–249, https://doi.org/10.1093/ia/iix242. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does surplus industrial capacity push a country to lend abroad?","a":"Because the alternative to finding new projects is idle factories and unemployment at home, so lending a foreign government the money to hire Chinese builders keeps the domestic industry working while the cost appears as a loan rather than a subsidy."},{"q":"Why is debt-trap diplomacy a contested description rather than a finding?","a":"Because it claims an intention, and the evidence mostly shows poor appraisal and optimistic forecasting on both sides. Serious debt distress turns out to be concentrated in a few borrowers rather than being the general pattern."},{"q":"Why invoke the Silk Road rather than name the programme plainly?","a":"Because the image carries a history of exchange between equals and makes a new projection of power sound like the return of something ancient and familiar, which is a far easier idea for a partner government to accept."}]},
    "pea-013": {"abstract":"The <b>National People's Congress</b> is the national legislature of the People's Republic of China and, on paper, the highest organ of state power in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly 3,000 deputies are elected for five-year terms through a tiered system of indirect elections, in which lower congresses choose the delegates above them, so ordinary citizens vote directly only at county level and below. The full body convenes for about ten days each March, approving the budget, passing legislation, hearing the premier's work report and electing the State Chairman and the Premier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because it sits so briefly, the continuous work falls to its Standing Committee of roughly 175 members, which meets every two months and issues most legislation and interpretations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its reputation as a rubber stamp is well earned, since bills are settled within the party before they arrive and are then approved by overwhelming margins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It is nonetheless a mistake to dismiss the institution entirely, because it performs functions that matter to the regime even without any legislative independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Deputies are measurably more active than the rubber-stamp image suggests, raising proposals and questions that carry information upward which the leadership would otherwise struggle to obtain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The congress also confers legal form on decisions already taken, and its committees have built real technical expertise in drafting commercial, environmental and civil law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its composition serves a representative purpose, deliberately including workers, farmers, soldiers and minorities, though the presence of so many wealthy business figures has drawn considerable comment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A seat is itself a prize, since for a private entrepreneur it buys political access and a measure of protection that are hard to obtain in any other way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Felix Wiebrecht, “Explaining Activity in Authoritarian Assemblies: Evidence from China,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 22, no. 3 (2022): 435–455, https://doi.org/10.1017/jea.2022.19. [Open access]","Xin Sun, Jiangnan Zhu, and Yiping Wu, “Organizational Clientelism: An Analysis of Private Entrepreneur Delegates in Chinese Local Legislatures,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> (2016), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800009565. [Open access]","Keith J. Hand, “Resolving Constitutional Disputes in Contemporary China,” <i>East Asia Law Review</i> 7, no. 1 (2011): 51, https://scholarship.law.upenn.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1056&context=ealr. [Open access]","Johannes Gerschewski, “Legitimacy in Autocracies: Oxymoron or Essential Feature?,” <i>Perspectives on Politics</i> 16, no. 3 (2018): 652–665, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1537592717002183. [Open access]","Christopher Heurlin, “Responsive Authoritarianism in China,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2016), https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9781316443019. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why keep a legislature that never rejects anything?","a":"Because it does work the party cannot do otherwise: it turns decisions into law that officials and courts can apply, it gathers information from every province, and it puts a representative face on rule that would otherwise look purely imposed."},{"q":"Why do indirect elections matter to how the body behaves?","a":"Because a deputy is chosen by the congress below rather than by voters, so accountability runs upward through the tiers instead of outward to a constituency, and no deputy has an electorate that could punish loyalty to the leadership."},{"q":"Why would a businessman want a seat in a rubber-stamp assembly?","a":"Because the seat is not about voting. It confers standing, access to officials who decide licences and land, and a degree of protection against arbitrary treatment, which in a system without reliable courts is worth a great deal."}]},
    "pea-014": {"abstract":"A <b>civilizational state</b> is a country understood as the political expression of an entire civilisation rather than as one nation among many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The idea is deployed against the assumption that the nation-state, which emerged in early modern Europe and spread through colonialism, is the natural form of political organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zhang Weiwei popularised it for China in his 2012 book <i>The China Wave</i>, and it has since become a staple of official and semi-official argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four claims usually carry it: an exceptionally long continuous history, a population no European state has ever governed, a vast and varied territory, and a cultural tradition emphasising order and the collective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The political conclusion drawn is that a strong central state is a requirement of governing such a place rather than a deviation from some proper path.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The idea is genuinely useful as a corrective, since comparative politics has often treated one Western trajectory as the standard against which every other country is found wanting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has also travelled well beyond China, taken up by thinkers and governments elsewhere who wish to justify their own departures from liberal norms by appeal to civilisational difference rather than by argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The objections are serious nonetheless, beginning with the fact that civilisations are neither unified nor unchanging, so claims about what Chinese civilisation requires are selective readings made for present purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second objection is that the argument conveniently insulates one particular government from criticism, treating opposition to the ruling party as opposition to the civilisation itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A third objection is simply empirical, since Taiwan, Japan and South Korea share much of the same cultural inheritance and have nonetheless built durable democracies within it.","sources":["William A. Callahan, “Sino-speak: Chinese Exceptionalism and the Politics of History,” <i>The Journal of Asian Studies</i> 71, no. 1 (2012): 33–55, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0021911811002919. [Open access]","Jinghan Zeng, “The Debate on Regime Legitimacy in China: bridging the wide gulf between Western and Chinese scholarship,” <i>Journal of Contemporary China</i> 23, no. 88 (2014): 612–635, https://doi.org/10.1080/10670564.2013.861141. [Open access]","Michael Gow, “The Core Socialist Values of the Chinese Dream: towards a Chinese integral state,” <i>Critical Asian Studies</i> 49, no. 1 (2016): 92–116, https://doi.org/10.1080/14672715.2016.1263803. [Open access]","William A. Callahan, “Identity and Security in China: The Negative Soft Power of the China Dream,” <i>Politics</i> 35, no. 3–4 (2015): 216–229, https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-9256.12088. [Open access]","Johannes Gerschewski, “Legitimacy in Autocracies: Oxymoron or Essential Feature?,” <i>Perspectives on Politics</i> 16, no. 3 (2018): 652–665, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1537592717002183. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is calling a country a civilisation a political move rather than a description?","a":"Because a civilisation has no government to criticise, so redescribing the state as its expression transfers the respect owed to a culture onto the party that currently rules, and turns political disagreement into cultural disloyalty."},{"q":"Why do Taiwan and South Korea weaken the argument so badly?","a":"Because they share the Confucian inheritance, the written language and much of the history that the argument says makes democracy unworkable, yet both built functioning democracies, so the cultural premise cannot be doing the work claimed for it."},{"q":"Why has the idea spread to India, Russia and Turkey?","a":"Because it offers any government a principled-sounding reason to reject outside criticism of its institutions: the objection is recast as cultural imperialism rather than answered on its merits."}]},
    "pea-015": {"abstract":"<b>Zhang Weiwei</b> is a professor of international relations at Fudan University in Shanghai and the best-known intellectual defender of the Chinese government both at home and abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He worked in the 1980s as an English interpreter for the Chinese leadership, an experience that lends his account of the reform era a certain authority. His argument, set out in his 2012 book <i>The China Wave</i>, is that China is not a nation-state of the ordinary European kind but a civilisation of enormous scale that has taken the form of a modern country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From this he concludes that models developed for small and homogeneous European societies do not transfer, and that liberal democracy is unsuitable for a country of China's size and history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He points out that China did attempt a Western-style republic after 1912 and that the experiment produced warlordism, fragmentation and decades of misery. <br><br> The second half of his case is an attack on the condition of the democracies themselves, which he describes as gripped by populism, short-termism, the excessive influence of money and the elevation of showmanship over competence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Against this he sets what he presents as an elaborate meritocracy, in which leaders are tested through demanding provincial postings before reaching the top, and he treats sustained growth as proof that the system works.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His case is not easily dismissed, since the developmental record is real and the criticisms of democratic dysfunction are widely shared by Western commentators too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has nonetheless been overtaken in one embarrassing respect, because in 2012 he cited the strict term limits on Chinese leaders as evidence of the system's superiority, and those limits were abolished six years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Critics add that a defence resting on performance offers no answer to the question of what legitimates the system once growth slows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Jinghan Zeng, “The Debate on Regime Legitimacy in China: bridging the wide gulf between Western and Chinese scholarship,” <i>Journal of Contemporary China</i> 23, no. 88 (2014): 612–635, https://doi.org/10.1080/10670564.2013.861141. [Open access]","William A. Callahan, “Sino-speak: Chinese Exceptionalism and the Politics of History,” <i>The Journal of Asian Studies</i> 71, no. 1 (2012): 33–55, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0021911811002919. [Open access]","Dimitar D. Gueorguiev, “Dictator's Shadow,” <i>China Perspectives</i> 2018, no. 1–2 (2018): 17–26, https://doi.org/10.4000/chinaperspectives.7569. [Open access]","Johannes Gerschewski, “Legitimacy in Autocracies: Oxymoron or Essential Feature?,” <i>Perspectives on Politics</i> 16, no. 3 (2018): 652–665, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1537592717002183. [Open access]","Michael Gow, “The Core Socialist Values of the Chinese Dream: towards a Chinese integral state,” <i>Critical Asian Studies</i> 49, no. 1 (2016): 92–116, https://doi.org/10.1080/14672715.2016.1263803. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a defence built on economic performance carry a risk?","a":"Because performance can fall. A claim to rule that rests on delivering growth is only as strong as the next decade of growth, which is why slowing figures are a political problem in China rather than merely an economic one."},{"q":"Why is the 1912 republic such a useful example for this argument?","a":"Because it lets the case be made from Chinese history rather than from theory: the country did try the Western model once, and what followed was warlordism and collapse, which makes caution sound like experience rather than excuse."},{"q":"Why did the 2018 change embarrass this defence particularly?","a":"Because term limits had been offered as the proof that the system disciplined its own leaders. Removing them took away the very feature that had been cited to show the model was institutional rather than personal."}]},
    "pea-016": {"abstract":"<b>Xu Zhangrun</b> is a Chinese legal scholar who taught constitutional law at Tsinghua University and became the most outspoken establishment critic of the direction the country has taken since 2012.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His essay of July 2018, written months after the abolition of term limits, set out with remarkable directness what he regarded as a descent back towards personal dictatorship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He named the return of totalitarian politics, the revival of a personality cult, the smothering of intellectual life and the wasteful pursuit of prestige abroad, and he called for the restoration of term limits and a reckoning with the events of 1989. In February 2020 he published a second essay, written during the first weeks of the coronavirus outbreak, arguing that a system built on censorship had made the epidemic worse by silencing the doctors who first raised the alarm. Both circulated widely on the Chinese internet and through overseas platforms before censors could suppress them. <br><br> What distinguishes him from many other critics is his decision to remain in China and to publish under his own name, accepting the consequences rather than seeking safety abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was suspended, placed under investigation and barred from teaching, then detained in July 2020, held for six days and dismissed from the university.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His case is instructive because he was not a marginal figure but a respected professor at one of the country's most prestigious universities, writing in the classical register of a scholar remonstrating with an emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Set beside the regime intellectuals who defend the Chinese model, he represents the opposite pole of thinking about the country's political future, and the sharply different fates of the two illustrate the limits of permissible debate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The wider pattern is a narrowing of what a Chinese university can host, which reaches well beyond the few scholars who are punished by name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Tim Pringle and Sophia Woodman, “Between a rock and a hard place: academic freedom in globalising Chinese universities,” <i>The International Journal of Human Rights</i> 26, no. 10 (2022): 1782–1802, https://doi.org/10.1080/13642987.2022.2074979. [Open access]","Sarah Biddulph, Elisa Nesossi, and Susan Trevaskes, “Criminal Justice Reform in the Xi Jinping Era,” <i>China Law and Society Review</i> 2, no. 1 (2017): 63–128, https://doi.org/10.1163/25427466-00201002. Free copy at http://hdl.handle.net/11343/252821. [Open access]","Dimitar D. Gueorguiev, “Dictator's Shadow,” <i>China Perspectives</i> 2018, no. 1–2 (2018): 17–26, https://doi.org/10.4000/chinaperspectives.7569. [Open access]","Jinghan Zeng, “The Debate on Regime Legitimacy in China: bridging the wide gulf between Western and Chinese scholarship,” <i>Journal of Contemporary China</i> 23, no. 88 (2014): 612–635, https://doi.org/10.1080/10670564.2013.861141. [Open access]","William A. Callahan, “Sino-speak: Chinese Exceptionalism and the Politics of History,” <i>The Journal of Asian Studies</i> 71, no. 1 (2012): 33–55, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0021911811002919. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does staying in China make a critic harder to dismiss?","a":"Because a critic abroad can be described as speaking for foreign interests, which is the standard official answer. Someone who stays, publishes under his own name and accepts the consequences cannot be answered that way."},{"q":"Why write in a classical literary register rather than plainly?","a":"Because it places the author in the old tradition of the scholar who remonstrates with a ruler out of loyalty rather than hostility, which makes the criticism a duty performed rather than an attack, and is harder to call subversion."},{"q":"Why punish one professor rather than simply delete the essay?","a":"Because deletion answers the text while the example answers everyone else: a colleague who watches a respected scholar lose his post learns the boundary without ever being told it, which is cheaper than censoring each new essay."}]},
    "pea-017": {"abstract":"<b>Xi Jinping Thought</b> is the body of doctrine attributed to the current leader and enshrined as the party's guiding ideology at the 19th Party Congress in October 2017.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its full title, Xi Jinping Thought on Socialism with Chinese Characteristics for a New Era, matters a great deal in a system where every word of such a formula is weighed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crucial element is the word thought, which in the party's strict hierarchy of ideological labels ranks above theory and had previously been reserved for Mao Zedong alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Deng Xiaoping, for all his influence, was credited only with a theory, and both Jiang Zemin and Hu Jintao received humbler formulations that did not carry their names at all. Xi received the higher honour while still in office, a break with precedent that told every official in the country where power now lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The declaration of a New Era carries its own message, dividing party history into three stages in which Mao made the nation stand up, Deng made it prosperous and Xi will make it strong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The content is broad rather than sharply defined, gathering fourteen principles that range across party leadership over all work, national security, ecological civilisation and the reunification of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of it restates the core socialist values already promoted under Hu Jintao, which is part of how a doctrine presented as new can also feel entirely familiar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Practical consequences followed quickly, since study of the doctrine was made compulsory in schools and universities, embedded in the civil service examinations and pushed through a widely used mobile application.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Elevating a living leader to this rank places his judgement beyond ordinary criticism, because disagreement with a policy becomes disagreement with the party's own constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["John Garrick and Yan Chang Bennett, “‘Xi Jinping Thought’,” <i>China Perspectives</i> 2018, no. 1–2 (2018): 99–105, https://doi.org/10.4000/chinaperspectives.7872. [Open access]","Frank N. Pieke and Bert Hofman, “CPC Futures: The New Era of Socialism with Chinese Characteristics,” <i>East Asian Institute, NUS eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.56159/eai.52060. [Open access]","Michael Gow, “The Core Socialist Values of the Chinese Dream: towards a Chinese integral state,” <i>Critical Asian Studies</i> 49, no. 1 (2016): 92–116, https://doi.org/10.1080/14672715.2016.1263803. [Open access]","Dimitar D. Gueorguiev, “Dictator's Shadow,” <i>China Perspectives</i> 2018, no. 1–2 (2018): 17–26, https://doi.org/10.4000/chinaperspectives.7569. [Open access]","Amy H. Liu, “Pronoun Usage as a Measure of Power Personalization: A General Theory with Evidence from the Chinese-Speaking World,” <i>British Journal of Political Science</i> 52, no. 3 (2021): 1258–1275, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0007123421000181. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the difference between thought and theory matter so much?","a":"Because the labels are ranked, and everyone in the system knows the ranking. Awarding the higher one to a living leader is a public statement that he stands with the founder rather than with his immediate predecessors."},{"q":"Why write a doctrine into the constitution rather than simply announce it?","a":"Because a constitutional formula converts a policy preference into the party's own definition of itself, so opposing the policy afterwards is not a disagreement to be argued but a breach of the rules that bind every member."},{"q":"Why does vagueness help rather than weaken such a doctrine?","a":"Because a broad formula can absorb almost any policy that follows, so the leader is never caught contradicting his own thought, and officials must interpret rather than measure him against a fixed standard."}]},
    "pea-018": {"abstract":"<b>Hu Jintao</b> led China for a decade as the fourth generation of communist leadership and is the figure most associated with the mature form of collective leadership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A hydraulic engineer by training, he rose through the Communist Youth League and through postings in poor inland provinces, and had been marked out as Jiang Zemin's eventual successor long before he took office. His governing style was deliberately self-effacing, treating the general secretaryship as a first among equals and working as mediator among the factions represented on the Politburo Standing Committee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He inherited a country growing spectacularly but tearing at the seams, with a widening divide between coast and interior and tens of thousands of protests each year over land seizures and unpaid wages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His answer was a shift of emphasis away from raw growth and towards balance, expressed in the slogans of the harmonious socialist society and the scientific outlook on development.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Concrete measures followed, including the abolition of the agricultural tax, an extension of rural health insurance and heavy investment in the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The social-policy record is in fact better than the lost-decade verdict allows, since spending on health, education and pensions rose substantially and reached groups that had previously been left out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Room also opened for social organisations to deliver services, always on conditions the party set and could withdraw at will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The disappointments were real all the same, as inequality stayed high, corruption grew worse, and harmony in practice also meant tighter policing of dissidents, lawyers and the restive western regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His clearest success was procedural, since he observed the retirement norms and handed every office to Xi Jinping on schedule, which also delivered unencumbered power to a successor who would dismantle those very norms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jude Howell and Jane Duckett, “Reassessing the Hu–Wen Era: A Golden Age or Lost Decade for Social Policy in China?,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 237 (2018): 1–14, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741018001200. [Open access]","Qin Gao, Sui Yang, and Fuhua Zhai, “Social Policy and Income Inequality during the Hu–Wen Era: A Progressive Legacy?,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 237 (2018): 82–107, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741018001248. [Open access]","Jude Howell, “NGOs and Civil Society: The Politics of Crafting a Civic Welfare Infrastructure in the Hu–Wen Period,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 237 (2018): 58–81, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741018001236. [Open access]","Heike Holbig and Bruce Gilley, “Reclaiming Legitimacy in China,” <i>Politics & Policy</i> 38, no. 3 (2010): 395–422, https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1747-1346.2010.00241.x. [Open access]","Shiping Zheng, “Leadership change, legitimacy, and party transition in China,” <i>Journal of Chinese Political Science</i> 8, no. 1–2 (2003): 47–63, https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02876949. Free copy at https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF02876949.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the lost-decade verdict on these years contested?","a":"Because it measures the decade by structural economic reform, which largely stalled, while social spending on health, education and pensions rose sharply and reached people the earlier boom had passed over entirely."},{"q":"Why would a leader who mediated between factions struggle to reform?","a":"Because consensus gives every major interest a veto, and structural reform by definition imposes losses on somebody, so the measures that survive the bargaining are the ones that cost no faction anything."},{"q":"Why did an orderly handover end up weakening the norms it honoured?","a":"Because transferring all three offices at once gave the successor complete authority from his first day, and nothing in the arrangement obliged him to pass it on the same way when his own turn came."}]},
    "pea-019": {"abstract":"The <b>Harmonious Socialist Society</b> was the defining slogan of the Hu Jintao decade and the party's admission that two decades of headlong growth had produced dangerous social strain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its diagnosis was that inequality, rural neglect, environmental ruin and official corruption were together generating unrest on a scale that threatened stability itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The remedy proposed was a rebalancing of development towards the interior, the countryside and the poor, rather than the single-minded pursuit of the highest possible growth rate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Policy substance did follow the rhetoric, including the abolition of the centuries-old agricultural tax in 2006, the extension of a rural cooperative medical scheme and the phased removal of school fees in the countryside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The vocabulary was also a deliberate borrowing from the Confucian tradition, reaching past Marxist language for an older and more familiar idea of social order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Harmony carried a second and harder meaning, since a society is made harmonious not only by redistribution but by the removal of discord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In practice the same slogan justified intensified surveillance and suppression of petitioners, rights lawyers, religious groups and independent journalists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It framed the heavy security responses to the Tibetan unrest of 2008 and the Urumqi violence of 2009, where minority grievances were treated above all as threats to stability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Chinese internet users caught the irony quickly and turned the word itself into slang, saying that a deleted post had been harmonised, a joke that in due course became a censored term of its own. The slogan is now generally judged to have named the country's problems accurately while failing to solve them, and that frustration helped legitimise the far more forceful approach adopted after 2012.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Ai Guo Han, “Building a Harmonious Society and Achieving Individual Harmony,” <i>Journal of Chinese Political Science</i> 13, no. 2 (2008): 143–164, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11366-008-9021-y. [Open access]","Jude Howell and Jane Duckett, “Reassessing the Hu–Wen Era: A Golden Age or Lost Decade for Social Policy in China?,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 237 (2018): 1–14, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741018001200. [Open access]","Qin Gao, Sui Yang, and Fuhua Zhai, “Social Policy and Income Inequality during the Hu–Wen Era: A Progressive Legacy?,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 237 (2018): 82–107, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741018001248. [Open access]","Heike Holbig and Bruce Gilley, “Reclaiming Legitimacy in China,” <i>Politics & Policy</i> 38, no. 3 (2010): 395–422, https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1747-1346.2010.00241.x. [Open access]","Johannes Gerschewski, “Legitimacy in Autocracies: Oxymoron or Essential Feature?,” <i>Perspectives on Politics</i> 16, no. 3 (2018): 652–665, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1537592717002183. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why reach for Confucian language in a Marxist party?","a":"Because Marxism could no longer describe an economy the party was deliberately marketising, while an older vocabulary of order and social duty was familiar to everyone and carried no awkward implications about class struggle."},{"q":"Why does harmony end up justifying repression as well as welfare?","a":"Because a harmonious society can be reached from either direction: by removing the grievance or by removing the person voicing it, and the second is quicker, cheaper and needs no agreement from anyone with something to lose."},{"q":"Why did naming the problems accurately not lead to solving them?","a":"Because the measures that would have worked cut into the interests of officials, state firms and local governments whose consent consensus rule required, so the diagnosis could be published while the cure stayed blocked."}]},
    "pea-020": {"abstract":"The <b>Soviet collapse</b> of 1991 is the single event that has most shaped how the Chinese Communist Party thinks about its own survival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came at the worst possible moment for the Chinese leadership, barely two years after the Tiananmen crackdown, and seemed to demonstrate that a ruling communist party could simply cease to exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lessons drawn were not the ones a Western observer might expect, since the leadership concluded that the Soviet Union fell because it reformed politically while failing economically, rather than because it was too repressive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Deng Xiaoping's response was the southern tour, driven by the conviction that only rapid growth and rising living standards could secure the party's position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The patriotic education campaign came from the same anxiety, an attempt to build a nationalist basis for loyalty once Marxist belief had evidently ceased to command it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The communist regimes that survived 1991 divided between those that froze their doctrine and those that adapted it, and China is the clearest case of adaptation rather than retreat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The preoccupation has if anything intensified under Xi Jinping, who used an early speech to ask why the Soviet party had disintegrated and answered that its ideals had wavered and nobody had stood up to resist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Party schools teach the subject systematically, and the specific diagnoses guide present policy in ways a reader can trace directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ideological laxity, the loss of party control over the army, historical nihilism about the party's own past and the corrosive effect of official corruption are each treated as a fatal error to be avoided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Understanding this fear is essential to making sense of Chinese politics, because a great deal of what looks like confidence is better read as a calculated defence against a remembered catastrophe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Dukalskis and Johannes Gerschewski, “Adapting or Freezing? Ideological Reactions of Communist Regimes to a Post-Communist World,” <i>Government and Opposition</i> 55, no. 3 (2018): 511–532, https://doi.org/10.1017/gov.2018.40. [Open access]","Margaret M. Pearson, Meg Rithmire, and Kellee S. Tsai, “The State and Capitalism in China,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2023), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009356732. [Open access]","Frank N. Pieke and Bert Hofman, “CPC Futures: The New Era of Socialism with Chinese Characteristics,” <i>East Asian Institute, NUS eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.56159/eai.52060. [Open access]","Heike Holbig and Bruce Gilley, “Reclaiming Legitimacy in China,” <i>Politics & Policy</i> 38, no. 3 (2010): 395–422, https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1747-1346.2010.00241.x. [Open access]","Christopher R. Hughes, “Chinese Nationalism in the Global Era,” (2006), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203482001. Free copy at http://eprints.lse.ac.uk/23027/1/Chinese_nationalism_in_the_global_era%28LSERO%29.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why read the Soviet collapse as an economic failure rather than a political one?","a":"Because that reading licenses the course the party already wanted: keep the political monopoly, deliver growth. Reading it as a failure of repression would have implied liberalising, which is the one conclusion no leadership draws about itself."},{"q":"Why does the party insist the army answer to it rather than the state?","a":"Because one lesson taken from 1991 is that Soviet forces would not act to save the party, so a military loyal to the state rather than the party is treated as a danger to be designed out rather than a normal arrangement."},{"q":"Why is historical nihilism treated as a threat to the state?","a":"Because the party concluded that once Soviet citizens were free to condemn their own past, the regime lost the story that justified it. Protecting the official version of history therefore counts as protecting the regime itself."}]},
    "pea-021": {"abstract":"The <b>National Party Congress</b> is formally the highest organ of the Chinese Communist Party, assembling roughly 2,300 delegates in Beijing once every five years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its powers on paper are considerable, since it elects the Central Committee, the Central Military Commission and the discipline inspection commission, and it alone may amend the party constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In practice it functions as a ratifying assembly rather than a deliberative one, approving in a few days what has been settled through months of bargaining among the senior leadership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> This does not make it unimportant, because the Congress is the moment at which those bargains become public and the leadership for the next five years is revealed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Analysts therefore read its outcomes closely, counting which groups have gained seats and which figures have been promoted, retired or quietly dropped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The constitutional amendments it passes are equally revealing, since each leader seeks to have a formulation inserted and the wording signals that leader's standing precisely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 19th Congress of 2017 wrote Xi Jinping Thought into the constitution by name, and the 20th of 2022 delivered him a third term with a Standing Committee composed almost entirely of his allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That Congress also produced the episode in which a visibly frail Hu Jintao was escorted from the hall in front of the cameras, an image that circulated worldwide and was scrubbed from the Chinese internet. A persistent source of confusion is that the party's Congress and the state's National People's Congress are entirely different institutions. The party body meets every five years and outranks the legislature in real authority, since the party sets the policy that the state apparatus then carries out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Frank N. Pieke and Bert Hofman, “CPC Futures: The New Era of Socialism with Chinese Characteristics,” <i>East Asian Institute, NUS eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.56159/eai.52060. [Open access]","Shiping Zheng, “Leadership change, legitimacy, and party transition in China,” <i>Journal of Chinese Political Science</i> 8, no. 1–2 (2003): 47–63, https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02876949. Free copy at https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF02876949.pdf. [Open access]","Dimitar D. Gueorguiev, “Dictator's Shadow,” <i>China Perspectives</i> 2018, no. 1–2 (2018): 17–26, https://doi.org/10.4000/chinaperspectives.7569. [Open access]","Raymond Fisman et al., “Social Ties and the Selection of China's Political Elite,” <i>American Economic Review</i> 110, no. 6 (2020): 1752–1781, https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20180841. [Open access]","Chenggang Xu, “The Fundamental Institutions of China's Reforms and Development,” <i>Journal of Economic Literature</i> 49, no. 4 (2011): 1076–1151, https://doi.org/10.1257/jel.49.4.1076. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why hold a congress that decides nothing it has not already agreed?","a":"Because the bargaining needs an endpoint that everyone can see. The Congress converts a private settlement into a public fact, so an official who disputes it afterwards is defying the party rather than disagreeing with colleagues."},{"q":"Why do analysts read the constitutional wording so carefully?","a":"Because the formula is one of the few precise signals the party emits: whether a leader's name appears, and whether his contribution is called a thought or a theory, fixes his rank against his predecessors without any announcement."},{"q":"Why is confusing the two congresses more than a naming problem?","a":"Because it confuses where power sits. Treating the state legislature as the senior body implies decisions are made in the government and ratified by the party, when the actual sequence runs the other way round."}]},
    "pea-022": {"abstract":"The <b>Central Committee</b> is responsible for party work during the long intervals when the National Party Congress is not in session, which in practice means almost all of the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is elected by the Congress for a five-year term and comprises roughly 200 full members with voting rights and about 170 alternates without them. Formally it elects the General Secretary, the Politburo and the Secretariat, which makes it the body from which the entire senior leadership derives its authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It gathers in plenary sessions, usually once a year, and these plenums are the occasions on which major policy turns are announced, the third plenum of a new term traditionally being reserved for economic reform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its membership doubles as the personnel pool for the major posts in the state administration, so that provincial governors, ministers and senior generals are drawn largely from its ranks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its real political weight is best understood through composition rather than through formal powers that are rarely exercised independently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because seats are allocated across provinces, ministries, the military and state enterprises, the balance among these groups at each Congress indicates where influence is shifting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The proportion of members with backgrounds in finance, technology or internal security likewise signals the leadership's priorities for the coming years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Calling it a miniature parliament captures something real, since it represents the party's broad elite, but the analogy should not be pressed, because it neither debates openly nor rejects what the leadership proposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its independence has been tested only rarely, and the last occasion on which it decisively altered events was the third plenum of 1978, which confirmed Deng Xiaoping's ascendancy and launched the reform era.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Chenggang Xu, “The Fundamental Institutions of China's Reforms and Development,” <i>Journal of Economic Literature</i> 49, no. 4 (2011): 1076–1151, https://doi.org/10.1257/jel.49.4.1076. [Open access]","Shiping Zheng, “Leadership change, legitimacy, and party transition in China,” <i>Journal of Chinese Political Science</i> 8, no. 1–2 (2003): 47–63, https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02876949. Free copy at https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF02876949.pdf. [Open access]","Weijie Luo and Shikun Qin, “China's Local Political Turnover in the Twenty-First Century,” <i>Journal of Chinese Political Science</i> 26, no. 4 (2021): 651–674, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11366-021-09739-2. [Open access]","Raymond Fisman et al., “Social Ties and the Selection of China's Political Elite,” <i>American Economic Review</i> 110, no. 6 (2020): 1752–1781, https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20180841. [Open access]","Frank N. Pieke and Bert Hofman, “CPC Futures: The New Era of Socialism with Chinese Characteristics,” <i>East Asian Institute, NUS eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.56159/eai.52060. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the composition tell you more than the powers?","a":"Because the powers are exercised as the leadership directs, while the seats are allocated in advance among provinces, ministries, the army and state firms, so the share each receives is a published measure of its standing."},{"q":"Why does a third plenum traditionally carry economic reform?","a":"Because a new leadership needs its first year to settle appointments and establish authority, so the meeting after that is the earliest point at which it can commit the party to a programme and expect it to be implemented."},{"q":"Why did 1978 turn out to be the exception rather than the rule?","a":"Because the leadership was genuinely divided after Mao, so the body had a real choice to make. When the top is united beforehand, a plenum has nothing left to decide and ratifies what it is given."}]},
    "pea-023": {"abstract":"<b>Paramount Leader</b> is the informal term for the person who genuinely holds supreme power in the People's Republic of China, as distinct from whoever occupies any particular constitutional office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The term exists because formal titles in China are an unreliable guide to real authority, a point Deng Xiaoping proved by dominating the country through the 1980s while holding no top post at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Since Jiang Zemin the status has normally been anchored in three positions held simultaneously: General Secretary of the party, State Chairman, and Chairman of the Central Military Commission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first gives command of the party, which sets policy and controls appointments, while the third gives command of armed forces that belong to the party rather than to the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state chairmanship, usually rendered in English as the presidency, is largely ceremonial on its own and matters chiefly as the title through which the leader represents China abroad. <br><br> The order in which the three are acquired and surrendered is politically revealing, since an outgoing leader has sometimes clung to the military chairmanship after giving up the others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jiang Zemin did exactly this, keeping the Central Military Commission until 2005, which left Hu Jintao's authority visibly incomplete at the start of his term.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hu in turn handed all three offices to Xi Jinping promptly in 2012 and 2013, a clean transfer that strengthened his successor from the outset. The concentration of the three roles in one pair of hands is what allows a single figure to direct party, state and army together without any formal separation of powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Under Xi Jinping the accumulation has gone further still, through a proliferation of commissions and leading groups that he chairs personally,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and through a public language that has become markedly more personal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Shiping Zheng, “Leadership change, legitimacy, and party transition in China,” <i>Journal of Chinese Political Science</i> 8, no. 1–2 (2003): 47–63, https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02876949. Free copy at https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF02876949.pdf. [Open access]","David Shambaugh, “Civil-Military Relations in China: Party-Army or National Military?,” <i>The Copenhagen Journal of Asian Studies</i> 16 (2002): 10–29, https://doi.org/10.22439/cjas.v16i0.3. Free copy at https://rauli.cbs.dk/index.php/cjas/article/download/3/3. [Open access]","Amy H. Liu, “Pronoun Usage as a Measure of Power Personalization: A General Theory with Evidence from the Chinese-Speaking World,” <i>British Journal of Political Science</i> 52, no. 3 (2021): 1258–1275, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0007123421000181. [Open access]","Dimitar D. Gueorguiev, “Dictator's Shadow,” <i>China Perspectives</i> 2018, no. 1–2 (2018): 17–26, https://doi.org/10.4000/chinaperspectives.7569. [Open access]","Frank N. Pieke and Bert Hofman, “CPC Futures: The New Era of Socialism with Chinese Characteristics,” <i>East Asian Institute, NUS eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.56159/eai.52060. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does China need an informal term for its own leader at all?","a":"Because the constitution describes offices rather than authority, and the person who matters has at times held none of the senior ones. A vocabulary of titles cannot name what everyone in the system can see."},{"q":"Why is the state chairmanship the least important of the three?","a":"Because it carries no command over either the party that makes policy or the army that guarantees it. Its value is representational, which is why removing its term limit mattered as a signal rather than as a transfer of power."},{"q":"Why watch the sequence in which the offices change hands?","a":"Because a handover that leaves one office behind is a handover that has not finished. The gap tells you the retiring leader still expects to be consulted, and officials adjust their loyalties to match."}]},
    "pea-024": {"abstract":"<b>Jiang Zemin</b> led the Chinese Communist Party out of the crisis of 1989 and is counted as the third generation of its leadership, after Mao Zedong and Deng Xiaoping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was an unexpected choice, brought in from the Shanghai party organisation after the purge of Zhao Ziyang precisely because he had handled protests in his own city without bloodshed. He arrived with a conservative reputation, yet once Deng forced the pace with the southern tour of 1992 he adopted the reform programme wholeheartedly and made it his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 1993 he held the general secretaryship, the state chairmanship and the military chairmanship together, and this triple grip became the template for what a paramount leader looks like.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With Premier Zhu Rongji he oversaw growth averaging around 8 percent a year, the painful restructuring of loss-making state industry and, in 2001, entry into the World Trade Organization.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The record has a darker side that the growth figures conceal, since inequality widened sharply, a gulf opened between the prosperous coastal cities and the rural interior, and corruption and factional interests multiplied throughout the party and state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Aware that Marxism no longer commanded belief, his government launched a campaign of patriotic education to rebuild the party's claim on its citizens through nationalism instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Governance in these years came increasingly to be understood as the management of an economy rather than as politics, an approach his successor judged inadequate to the dislocation it produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His greatest institutional achievement was simply to leave, since the scheduled transfer of power to Hu Jintao was a genuine rarity in the communist world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He nonetheless held on to the military chairmanship for a further two years, which qualified the achievement and left his successor visibly short of full authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Shiping Zheng, “Leadership change, legitimacy, and party transition in China,” <i>Journal of Chinese Political Science</i> 8, no. 1–2 (2003): 47–63, https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02876949. Free copy at https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF02876949.pdf. [Open access]","Jude Howell and Jane Duckett, “Reassessing the Hu–Wen Era: A Golden Age or Lost Decade for Social Policy in China?,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 237 (2018): 1–14, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741018001200. [Open access]","Margaret M. Pearson, Meg Rithmire, and Kellee S. Tsai, “The State and Capitalism in China,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2023), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009356732. [Open access]","Christopher R. Hughes, “Chinese Nationalism in the Global Era,” (2006), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203482001. Free copy at http://eprints.lse.ac.uk/23027/1/Chinese_nationalism_in_the_global_era%28LSERO%29.pdf. [Open access]","Heike Holbig and Bruce Gilley, “Reclaiming Legitimacy in China,” <i>Politics & Policy</i> 38, no. 3 (2010): 395–422, https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1747-1346.2010.00241.x. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a conservative chosen to lead a party committed to reform?","a":"Because the immediate problem in 1989 was not economic direction but survival, and he had contained protests in Shanghai without shooting anyone, which made him acceptable to both wings at a moment when neither could impose its own candidate."},{"q":"Why does leaving office count as an achievement for a leader?","a":"Because communist regimes have mostly changed leaders through death or purge, so a handover that happens on schedule establishes that the office can outlast the person, which is the foundation every later succession rested on."},{"q":"Why did treating government as economic management store up trouble?","a":"Because the problems that grew fastest were distributional and social rather than technical, and a leadership that measured itself by output had no vocabulary for inequality, land seizures or unpaid wages until the protests forced one."}]},
    "pea-025": {"abstract":"The <b>princelings</b> are the children and grandchildren of senior Chinese Communist Party figures, especially of the revolutionary generation that founded the People's Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Chinese term carries an edge of mockery, borrowing the imperial vocabulary of a crown prince faction to describe a supposedly classless party that has produced a hereditary elite. Their advantages are both political and commercial, since family standing opens doors to leadership tracks, to the officer corps and to the most lucrative corners of a partly marketised economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ordinary Chinese have long resented this, and princeling status is a recurring theme in popular anger about corruption and unearned privilege.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The label is nonetheless double-edged, because a candidate too obviously marked as one can face resistance from colleagues who rose without such help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Xi Jinping is the outstanding example, as the son of Xi Zhongxun, a guerrilla commander and vice-premier who was purged under Mao and later, as party chief of Guangdong, helped create the special economic zone at Shenzhen. That inheritance cut both ways in his own life, since his father's disgrace sent him as a teenager to spend seven years labouring in a village in Shaanxi, an experience he has made central to his public story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Princeling identity supplies a particular claim to legitimacy, the argument that those whose families won the country have a special duty and right to preserve it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their prominence sits awkwardly with the party's meritocratic self-image, which is why the anti-corruption campaign made a point of pursuing them, and studies of the land market find their commercial advantage measurably reduced where it reached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It remains one of the sharper objections to any account presenting Chinese politics as a pure system of selection by merit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Ting Chen and James Kai-sing Kung, “Busting the ‘Princelings’: The Campaign Against Corruption in China's Primary Land Market,” <i>The Quarterly Journal of Economics</i> 134, no. 1 (2018): 185–226, https://doi.org/10.1093/qje/qjy027. [Open access]","Raymond Fisman et al., “Social Ties and the Selection of China's Political Elite,” <i>American Economic Review</i> 110, no. 6 (2020): 1752–1781, https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20180841. [Open access]","Dimitar D. Gueorguiev, “Dictator's Shadow,” <i>China Perspectives</i> 2018, no. 1–2 (2018): 17–26, https://doi.org/10.4000/chinaperspectives.7569. [Open access]","Weijie Luo and Shikun Qin, “China's Local Political Turnover in the Twenty-First Century,” <i>Journal of Chinese Political Science</i> 26, no. 4 (2021): 651–674, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11366-021-09739-2. [Open access]","Frank N. Pieke and Bert Hofman, “CPC Futures: The New Era of Socialism with Chinese Characteristics,” <i>East Asian Institute, NUS eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.56159/eai.52060. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why would princeling status ever count against a candidate?","a":"Because colleagues who climbed without family help resent the shortcut, and a leadership anxious about looking hereditary may prefer someone whose rise it can present as earned, so the advantage has to be used quietly."},{"q":"Why does a purged father strengthen rather than weaken the inheritance?","a":"Because it supplies a story of hardship that answers the charge of privilege: years of rural labour can be offered as evidence that the leader knows ordinary life, which a straightforwardly comfortable upbringing could not."},{"q":"Why do princelings complicate the meritocracy argument specifically?","a":"Because that argument says the system selects the capable through demanding provincial service. If family background independently predicts who reaches the top and which firms win land, then something other than merit is doing part of the selecting."}]},
    "pea-026": {"abstract":"The <b>Premier</b> of the People's Republic of China is the head of government and the leading figure in the State Council, the body that corresponds to a cabinet in other systems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The distinction from the State Chairman is the one students most often blur: the chairman is head of state and represents the country, while the premier actually runs the machinery of administration. Responsibility for the economy has traditionally fallen squarely on the premier, covering the budget, industrial policy, employment and the annual growth target announced to the legislature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The office has produced some of the most consequential figures in modern Chinese politics, notably Zhu Rongji, who pushed through painful reform of loss-making state industry and negotiated entry into the World Trade Organization.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wen Jiabao cultivated a public persona of concern for ordinary people, while Li Keqiang was a trained economist who occasionally offered unwelcome truths about how many Chinese still lived on very little.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The standing of the post has diminished considerably under Xi Jinping, in a shift that illustrates the wider recentralisation of power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Economic policymaking has migrated from the State Council towards party commissions that Xi chairs himself, leaving the premier to implement decisions rather than to shape them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Li Keqiang was visibly marginalised during his final years and left office in 2023, dying suddenly a few months later. His successor Li Qiang came to the job as a long-standing associate of Xi from his Zhejiang years and, unusually, without any prior experience as a vice-premier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That break with the customary apprenticeship was widely read as confirmation that loyalty now outweighs technical preparation in appointments to even the most senior administrative office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Chenggang Xu, “The Fundamental Institutions of China's Reforms and Development,” <i>Journal of Economic Literature</i> 49, no. 4 (2011): 1076–1151, https://doi.org/10.1257/jel.49.4.1076. [Open access]","Margaret M. Pearson, Meg Rithmire, and Kellee S. Tsai, “The State and Capitalism in China,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2023), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009356732. [Open access]","Dimitar D. Gueorguiev, “Dictator's Shadow,” <i>China Perspectives</i> 2018, no. 1–2 (2018): 17–26, https://doi.org/10.4000/chinaperspectives.7569. [Open access]","Frank N. Pieke and Bert Hofman, “CPC Futures: The New Era of Socialism with Chinese Characteristics,” <i>East Asian Institute, NUS eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.56159/eai.52060. [Open access]","Qin Gao, Sui Yang, and Fuhua Zhai, “Social Policy and Income Inequality during the Hu–Wen Era: A Progressive Legacy?,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 237 (2018): 82–107, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741018001248. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the difference between chairman and premier worth getting right?","a":"Because it identifies who answers for what. Blaming the head of state for an economic failure misreads the system, since the budget, industrial policy and employment have traditionally been the premier's brief rather than his."},{"q":"Why did moving policy into party commissions weaken the office?","a":"Because a commission the leader chairs reports to him rather than to the State Council, so the decisions arrive already made and the premier becomes the official who executes them instead of the one who argues about them."},{"q":"Why does appointing a premier with no vice-premier experience matter?","a":"Because the apprenticeship was how the system produced administrators who knew the machinery before running it, so skipping it signals that proximity to the leader now counts for more than the preparation the job was thought to require."}]},
    "pea-027": {"abstract":"The <b>Central Commission for Discipline Inspection</b> enforces party rules and investigates the misconduct of party members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is elected by the National Party Congress alongside the Central Committee, and it operates a network of inspection teams and resident disciplinary units reaching into every ministry, province and state enterprise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its significance lies in the fact that it works outside the state legal system, since a party member accused of corruption is dealt with by the party first and handed to the prosecutors only afterwards, if at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For decades its principal tool was a form of extralegal detention under which an official was required to report at a designated time and place and could then be held incommunicado for months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Detainees had no access to a lawyer, no contact with their families and no recourse to a court, and confessions extracted in these conditions have repeatedly been questioned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The commission became the engine of the anti-corruption campaign after 2012, and the scale of its reach into the provinces is what made that campaign possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its powers were formalised and extended in 2018 with the creation of a state supervisory commission that shares its offices and leadership but covers everyone exercising public authority, including the many officials who are not party members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That reform replaced the old detention practice with a statutory measure, which regularised it without restoring ordinary legal protections.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The institution captures a central feature of the Chinese system, that the party disciplines itself rather than submitting to scrutiny by courts, press or voters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its vast discretion over whom to investigate is precisely what makes it so effective as a political weapon as well as a genuine check on official greed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Sarah Biddulph, Elisa Nesossi, and Susan Trevaskes, “Criminal Justice Reform in the Xi Jinping Era,” <i>China Law and Society Review</i> 2, no. 1 (2017): 63–128, https://doi.org/10.1163/25427466-00201002. Free copy at http://hdl.handle.net/11343/252821. [Open access]","Ting Chen and James Kai-sing Kung, “Busting the ‘Princelings’: The Campaign Against Corruption in China's Primary Land Market,” <i>The Quarterly Journal of Economics</i> 134, no. 1 (2018): 185–226, https://doi.org/10.1093/qje/qjy027. [Open access]","Chenggang Xu, “The Fundamental Institutions of China's Reforms and Development,” <i>Journal of Economic Literature</i> 49, no. 4 (2011): 1076–1151, https://doi.org/10.1257/jel.49.4.1076. [Open access]","Pierre F. Landry, “The Political Management of Mayors in Post-Deng China,” <i>The Copenhagen Journal of Asian Studies</i> 17 (2003): 31–58, https://doi.org/10.22439/cjas.v17i0.12. [Open access]","Frank N. Pieke and Bert Hofman, “CPC Futures: The New Era of Socialism with Chinese Characteristics,” <i>East Asian Institute, NUS eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.56159/eai.52060. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why investigate party members outside the courts at all?","a":"Because handing a member straight to prosecutors would put the party's internal affairs before a state institution, which would imply the courts sit above it. Disciplining its own first keeps the hierarchy the other way round."},{"q":"Why did creating a state supervisory body in 2018 matter?","a":"Because it extended the same machinery to officials who are not party members, so the reach no longer stopped at the membership card, and a practice that had been internal party discipline became something closer to a parallel legal system."},{"q":"Why does regularising the detention not answer the objection to it?","a":"Because the complaint was never that the practice lacked a statute. Putting it in law left the detainee without a lawyer, without a court and without contact with family, so the protections that make a confession trustworthy are still absent."}]},
    "pea-028": {"abstract":"The <b>abolition of term limits</b> in March 2018 removed the clause in the Chinese state constitution barring the State Chairman and Vice-Chairman from serving more than two consecutive five-year terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That clause had been inserted in 1982 on Deng Xiaoping's insistence, as part of a deliberate effort to ensure that no individual could ever again accumulate the unchecked lifetime power Mao Zedong had wielded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over the following decades it hardened into the most visible guarantee of an orderly handover every ten years, and foreign observers routinely cited it as evidence that Chinese authoritarianism had become genuinely institutionalised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The amendment passed the National People's Congress by 2,958 votes to two, with three abstentions, a margin that says more about that body than about the measure. Because the general secretaryship and the military chairmanship had never carried formal limits, the change removed the last obstacle to one man remaining in power indefinitely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Reaction inside China was muted in public and sharply critical in private, and censors worked hard to suppress oblique jokes about emperors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It confirmed what the 19th Party Congress had already suggested, since that Congress had conspicuously failed to elevate any younger figure who might serve as an heir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The strongest argument against the move is the one Deng himself made, that a system without a rule for succession invites instability at the top and leaves no mechanism for correcting a leader's mistakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Defenders reply that continuity of leadership is needed for long-term national goals and that the party retains other means of internal accountability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Xi duly secured a third term in October 2022 and the question of what follows him is now entirely unanswered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Dimitar D. Gueorguiev, “Dictator's Shadow,” <i>China Perspectives</i> 2018, no. 1–2 (2018): 17–26, https://doi.org/10.4000/chinaperspectives.7569. [Open access]","Shiping Zheng, “Leadership change, legitimacy, and party transition in China,” <i>Journal of Chinese Political Science</i> 8, no. 1–2 (2003): 47–63, https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02876949. Free copy at https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF02876949.pdf. [Open access]","Frank N. Pieke and Bert Hofman, “CPC Futures: The New Era of Socialism with Chinese Characteristics,” <i>East Asian Institute, NUS eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.56159/eai.52060. [Open access]","Amy H. Liu, “Pronoun Usage as a Measure of Power Personalization: A General Theory with Evidence from the Chinese-Speaking World,” <i>British Journal of Political Science</i> 52, no. 3 (2021): 1258–1275, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0007123421000181. [Open access]","Johannes Gerschewski, “Legitimacy in Autocracies: Oxymoron or Essential Feature?,” <i>Perspectives on Politics</i> 16, no. 3 (2018): 652–665, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1537592717002183. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did removing a limit on a ceremonial office matter so much?","a":"Because the clause was never doing legal work; the offices that carry real power were always unlimited. Its value was as a signal that the system bound its leaders, so striking it out announced that it no longer does."},{"q":"Why is a 2,958 to two vote evidence about the legislature rather than the measure?","a":"Because a proposal of that consequence would divide any body able to divide. A margin that lopsided shows the assembly registering a decision taken elsewhere rather than reaching one of its own."},{"q":"Why does the absence of an heir compound the problem?","a":"Because a designated successor is how a system without elections rehearses its own continuity. Without one, the eventual transfer has no agreed procedure, and every faction has reason to prepare for a contest instead."}]},
    "pea-029": {"abstract":"The <b>principal–agent problem</b> arises whenever one party must rely on another to act on its behalf while being unable to observe fully what that other party actually does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Chinese politics the principal is the party leadership, which sets policy and controls appointments, and the agents are the officials who must carry that policy out across an enormous country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because promotion depends entirely on the judgement of party superiors, every agent has a powerful incentive to report success and to conceal failure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The result is a systematic upward distortion of information, in which targets are reported as met, problems are minimised and bad news is filtered out at each level it passes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The leadership is therefore governing partly blind, working from figures it cannot fully trust about the country it rules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What makes this so severe in a one-party state is the absence of the mechanisms that correct such distortions elsewhere, since there are no competitive elections, no independent press and no courts able to rule against the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The historical consequences have been catastrophic, most terribly during the Great Leap Forward, when wildly inflated harvest reports encouraged grain requisitions that helped produce a famine costing tens of millions of lives. More recent examples include the initial concealment of the SARS outbreak in 2003 and the local suppression of early warnings in Wuhan in 2019. The system has developed partial answers, including rotating officials between posts, sending inspection teams from the centre and measuring performance against targets that are harder to fake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of these removes the underlying incentive, and tightening party control while raising the political cost of unwelcome news arguably sharpens it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Pierre F. Landry, “The Political Management of Mayors in Post-Deng China,” <i>The Copenhagen Journal of Asian Studies</i> 17 (2003): 31–58, https://doi.org/10.22439/cjas.v17i0.12. [Open access]","Yuen Yuen Ang, “Counting Cadres: A Comparative View of the Size of China's Public Employment,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 211 (2012): 676–696, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741012000884. [Open access]","Chenggang Xu, “The Fundamental Institutions of China's Reforms and Development,” <i>Journal of Economic Literature</i> 49, no. 4 (2011): 1076–1151, https://doi.org/10.1257/jel.49.4.1076. [Open access]","Weijie Luo and Shikun Qin, “China's Local Political Turnover in the Twenty-First Century,” <i>Journal of Chinese Political Science</i> 26, no. 4 (2021): 651–674, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11366-021-09739-2. [Open access]","Christopher Heurlin, “Responsive Authoritarianism in China,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2016), https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9781316443019. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the problem bite harder without a free press?","a":"Because journalists and courts are how a ruler learns what subordinates are hiding. Remove them and the only channel left runs through the very officials with the strongest reason to shade what they send upward."},{"q":"Why does rotating officials between posts help at all?","a":"Because an official who expects to leave in three years has less time to build the local alliances that conceal a problem, and a successor arriving fresh has every reason to report what his predecessor buried."},{"q":"Why might tighter control make the distortion worse rather than better?","a":"Because raising the punishment for failure raises the payoff from hiding it. If delivering bad news ends a career, the rational official sends good news, and the centre receives a cleaner picture that is less true."}]},
    "pea-030": {"abstract":"The <b>party-state</b> is the standard description of a system in which the ruling party and the governing apparatus are so interwoven that the two cannot usefully be separated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A party committee and a party leading group sit inside every governmental body at national, provincial and county level, so that policy and appointments run through the party while administration runs through the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the Mao era the two were almost completely fused, which weakened the state bureaucracy and its specialists, since promotion rewarded loyalty rather than competence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The arrangement produced an information problem familiar in every communist system, because officials had every reason to tell party superiors what those superiors wished to hear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also left no democratic check capable of correcting the picture from outside, since there was no press, court or election able to contradict what the hierarchy reported.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Deng Xiaoping's reforms of the 1980s tried to loosen the relationship, delegating more responsibility for economic policy to the government bureaucracy and abolishing provincial party departments that merely duplicated government ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Civil service reform created a dual structure, with officials recruited by examination and professional criteria working alongside administrators appointed by the party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The intended result was a more independent bureaucracy with a genuine incentive to be efficient, which the party itself stood to gain from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those reforms were fiercely resisted by conservatives and never went as far as their advocates had hoped, and the party remained the senior partner throughout, setting general policy and ideology. The relationship tightened again under Jiang Zemin, and under Xi Jinping the insistence that the party leads everything has brought party-state relations closer to their Mao-era form than at any point since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Chenggang Xu, “The Fundamental Institutions of China's Reforms and Development,” <i>Journal of Economic Literature</i> 49, no. 4 (2011): 1076–1151, https://doi.org/10.1257/jel.49.4.1076. [Open access]","Pierre F. Landry, “The Political Management of Mayors in Post-Deng China,” <i>The Copenhagen Journal of Asian Studies</i> 17 (2003): 31–58, https://doi.org/10.22439/cjas.v17i0.12. [Open access]","Yuen Yuen Ang, “Counting Cadres: A Comparative View of the Size of China's Public Employment,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 211 (2012): 676–696, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741012000884. [Open access]","Weijie Luo and Shikun Qin, “China's Local Political Turnover in the Twenty-First Century,” <i>Journal of Chinese Political Science</i> 26, no. 4 (2021): 651–674, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11366-021-09739-2. [Open access]","Christopher Heurlin, “Responsive Authoritarianism in China,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2016), https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9781316443019. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why put a party committee inside every government office?","a":"Because it lets the party direct what the state does without having to administer anything itself: the committee decides the line and the appointments, and the ministry carries out the work under its own name."},{"q":"Why would the party benefit from a more independent bureaucracy?","a":"Because officials chosen for loyalty rather than competence run things badly and report dishonestly, so a professional civil service gives the leadership better administration and better information, which is worth some loss of direct control."},{"q":"Why did conservatives resist separating party from state?","a":"Because the party committees were where their own authority sat. Delegating economic policy to technically qualified officials would have transferred influence to people who had not risen through the party and did not owe them anything."}]},
    "pea-031": {"abstract":"<b>Article 9</b> is the clause of the Japanese constitution in which the people forever renounce war as a sovereign right of the nation and the threat or use of force as a means of settling international disputes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It goes further than any comparable provision, adding that land, sea and air forces, as well as other war potential, will never be maintained, and that the right of belligerency of the state will not be recognised. Taken literally the words forbid the armed forces Japan has had since 1954, which is why the article has generated more constitutional argument than any other sentence in Japanese public life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The orthodox reading holds that a state retains an inherent right of self-defence, so forces maintained strictly for defence do not count as war potential.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Amending the text is extremely difficult, requiring two-thirds of both houses of the Diet and then a majority in a national referendum, and no amendment has ever been made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Because formal revision has been out of reach, successive governments have changed the article's meaning by reinterpretation instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The most consequential came in July 2014, when the cabinet decided that Japan could exercise collective self-defence and come to the aid of an ally under attack, a reading its own previous governments had rejected for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Security legislation followed in 2015 amid the largest protests in a generation, and constitutional scholars objected that amending a constitution by cabinet decision hollows out the document itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Defenders answer that the alternative was a constitution frozen against a changing region, and that reinterpretation at least proceeded through an elected government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The practical result is a country with the world's third-largest defence budget and a constitution that still says it maintains no forces at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Christopher W. Hughes, “Japan's Strategic Trajectory and Collective Self-Defense: Essential Continuity or Radical Shift?,” <i>Journal of Japanese Studies</i> 43, no. 1 (2017): 93–126, https://doi.org/10.1353/jjs.2017.0005. [Open access]","Christopher W. Hughes, “Japan as a Global Military Power,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108975025. [Open access]","Jonathan Boyd and Richard J. Samuels, “Nine Lives?: The Politics of Constitutional Reform in Japan,” <i>East-West Center Policy Studies</i> (2005), https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/bitstreams/c1e9a383-a58e-4a2a-9999-57a51898b129/download. [Open access]","Craig Martin, “The Legitimacy of Informal Constitutional Amendment and the ‘Reinterpretation’ of Japan's War Powers,” <i>Fordham International Law Journal</i> 40, no. 2 (2016): 427, https://ir.lawnet.fordham.edu/ilj/vol40/iss2/3. [Open access]","Benedict S. B. Chan, “Utilitarian Contingent Pacifism and Article 9 of the Japanese Constitution,” <i>Philosophia</i> 51, no. 2 (2022): 635–657, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11406-022-00566-0. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why has the article never been amended despite decades of pressure?","a":"Because amendment needs two-thirds of both houses and then a referendum, and for most of the postwar period the opposition held just over a third of the lower house, which was enough to block it on its own."},{"q":"Why do constitutional scholars object to reinterpretation so strongly?","a":"Because if a cabinet can change what the constitution means by deciding that it means something else, the document no longer constrains the government, and the amendment procedure it contains becomes optional rather than binding."},{"q":"Why keep a clause the country has visibly outgrown?","a":"Because it still does work: it anchors a public expectation that Japan does not fight foreign wars, reassures neighbours who remember the empire, and gives governments a reason to decline commitments they would rather avoid."}]},
    "pea-032": {"abstract":"The <b>1947 Constitution</b> is the founding document of postwar Japan and replaced the Meiji order, in which sovereignty had belonged to the emperor, with one in which it belongs to the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is usually summarised by three pillars: popular sovereignty and democratic government, the renunciation of war, and an extensive list of rights and duties. It was drafted under the supervision of the occupation authorities, and the celebrated speed of the American drafting team has given opponents an enduring argument that the document was imposed rather than chosen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That charge is only partly fair, since Japanese liberal and pacifist politicians contributed substantially, the Diet debated and amended the draft, and later opinion polling has repeatedly found majorities content with the result.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rights chapter went well beyond American practice of the time, guaranteeing equality of the sexes, academic freedom and the right to a minimum standard of living.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Its most distinctive feature is how hard it is to change, since an amendment needs two-thirds of both houses of the Diet and then a majority in a national referendum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No amendment has ever been made, which makes it among the oldest unamended constitutions in the world and, by that measure, one of the most rigid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The reason is arithmetic rather than affection, because for most of the postwar period the opposition held just over a third of the lower house, which was enough to block revision on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Governments wanting change therefore turned to reinterpretation, which has altered the practical meaning of the war-renouncing clause while leaving its words untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Revision remains a live ambition of the conservative right and a live fear of the left, which is why it recurs in every Japanese election.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jonathan Boyd and Richard J. Samuels, “Nine Lives?: The Politics of Constitutional Reform in Japan,” <i>East-West Center Policy Studies</i> (2005), https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/bitstreams/c1e9a383-a58e-4a2a-9999-57a51898b129/download. [Open access]","Ayako Kusunoki, “US Policy for the Occupation of Japan and Changes to It,” (2023), https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-19-9593-4_9. [Open access]","Craig Martin, “The Legitimacy of Informal Constitutional Amendment and the ‘Reinterpretation’ of Japan's War Powers,” <i>Fordham International Law Journal</i> 40, no. 2 (2016): 427, https://ir.lawnet.fordham.edu/ilj/vol40/iss2/3. [Open access]","Oktay Kurtulus, “Japan Under Occupation, 1945–1952: Structure of the Occupation, Reform Policies, and the Reverse Course,” <i>Chuo University Repository</i> 45 (2016), https://chuo-u.repo.nii.ac.jp/record/11767/files/1345-2401_45_035.pdf. [Open access]","Christopher W. Hughes, “Japan's Strategic Trajectory and Collective Self-Defense: Essential Continuity or Radical Shift?,” <i>Journal of Japanese Studies</i> 43, no. 1 (2017): 93–126, https://doi.org/10.1353/jjs.2017.0005. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the imposed-constitution charge only partly fair?","a":"Because although American officials wrote the draft, Japanese politicians shaped and amended it in the Diet, several of its most distinctive provisions had Japanese advocates, and the public has repeatedly declined to vote it away since."},{"q":"Why does a one-third minority give the opposition so much power?","a":"Because amendment needs two-thirds of both houses, so holding a third of either chamber is a veto. That arithmetic, rather than any settled national consensus, is what has kept the text unchanged for nearly eighty years."},{"q":"Why does rigidity encourage reinterpretation rather than stability?","a":"Because pressure to adapt does not disappear when the formal route is blocked. If the words cannot be changed, a government that wants a different policy changes what the words are said to mean instead."}]},
    "pea-033": {"abstract":"<b>SCAP</b> stands for the Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers, the authority that governed Japan from the surrender in 1945 until sovereignty was restored in 1952.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Although the occupation was nominally Allied, it was overwhelmingly American, and the title is used both for General Douglas MacArthur personally and for the headquarters staff who did the work. Its early programme was a thorough remaking of Japanese society, carried out through a Japanese government that remained in place and issued the orders as its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That first phase dissolved the armed forces, put wartime leaders on trial, gave women the vote, legalised trade unions, broke up the landlord system through a sweeping land reform and moved against the zaibatsu conglomerates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also supervised the drafting of the new constitution, whose American authorship remains the most contested part of its legacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> From about 1947 the priorities changed in what is known as the reverse course, as the deepening Cold War made a stable anti-communist Japan matter more than a thoroughly democratised one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The dissolution of the conglomerates was halted well short of its targets, and purged wartime figures were rehabilitated while communists and union activists were removed from public life instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> This shift explains a great deal about the country that emerged, since the same business groups and bureaucratic networks carried over into the postwar order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The occupation therefore left behind two legacies that sit awkwardly together: a genuinely liberal constitution on the one hand, and on the other a conservative establishment that had survived largely intact. Both were handed to an independent Japan in 1952, and the tension between them runs through the whole of its postwar politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Ayako Kusunoki, “US Policy for the Occupation of Japan and Changes to It,” (2023), https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-19-9593-4_9. [Open access]","Oktay Kurtulus, “Japan Under Occupation, 1945–1952: Structure of the Occupation, Reform Policies, and the Reverse Course,” <i>Chuo University Repository</i> 45 (2016), https://chuo-u.repo.nii.ac.jp/record/11767/files/1345-2401_45_035.pdf. [Open access]","Takeo Kikkawa, “History of Innovative Entrepreneurs in Japan,” <i>Springer Nature</i> (2023), https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-19-9454-8. [Open access]","Jonathan Boyd and Richard J. Samuels, “Nine Lives?: The Politics of Constitutional Reform in Japan,” <i>East-West Center Policy Studies</i> (2005), https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/bitstreams/c1e9a383-a58e-4a2a-9999-57a51898b129/download. [Open access]","H. M. Kramer, “Just Who Reversed the Course? The Red Purge in Higher Education during the Occupation of Japan,” <i>Social Science Japan Journal</i> 8, no. 1 (2004): 1–18, https://doi.org/10.1093/ssjj/jyi011. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why rule through the Japanese government rather than directly?","a":"Because the occupiers were few and did not speak the language, while the existing bureaucracy could reach every town. Orders issued as Japanese law were also obeyed more readily than orders visibly issued by foreigners."},{"q":"Why did the reverse course change who was purged?","a":"Because the threat had been redefined. Once the danger was communism rather than militarism, the useful people were the conservatives and businessmen who had run wartime Japan, and the dangerous ones were the unions and the left."},{"q":"Why do the occupation's two legacies still pull against each other?","a":"Because the constitution written in the first phase commits Japan to pacifism and popular sovereignty, while the establishment preserved in the second has spent decades trying to revise exactly those commitments."}]},
    "pea-034": {"abstract":"<b>Anpo</b> is the Japanese shorthand for the security treaty with the United States, signed in 1951 on the same day as the peace treaty that ended the occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The original bargain was lopsided: the Americans gained unrestricted use of bases across the archipelago and kept full control of Okinawa, while Japan gained a security guarantee and severe limits on its own military activity abroad. That asymmetry was in some ways deliberate, since it let Japan rebuild its economy under American protection without rearming, an arrangement later named after the prime minister who signed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A revised treaty in 1960 made the commitment more mutual and required consultation over the use of bases, but it was forced through the Diet in a manner that provoked the largest protests in postwar Japanese history. The prime minister who pushed it through resigned immediately afterwards, and the episode fixed the alliance as the central dividing line of Japanese politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Roughly 50,000 American personnel remain in Japan, and the arrangement still shapes what Japanese governments can and cannot do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It has underwritten the nuclear umbrella, which is a large part of why Japan has remained a non-nuclear state despite possessing every technical means to change that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It has also given Washington a standing voice in Japanese security debates, and successive reinterpretations of the pacifist clause have been driven partly by American expectations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The costs fall very unevenly, since Okinawa hosts the great majority of the base area while holding a small fraction of the national population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Every government since has had to manage the same tension between an alliance most voters accept and a base presence many of them resent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Christopher W. Hughes, “Japan's Strategic Trajectory and Collective Self-Defense: Essential Continuity or Radical Shift?,” <i>Journal of Japanese Studies</i> 43, no. 1 (2017): 93–126, https://doi.org/10.1353/jjs.2017.0005. [Open access]","Carmina Yu Untalan, “Beyond Empire: Okinawa and the politics of American Military bases in Japan,” <i>Political Anthropological Research on International Social Sciences</i> 5, no. 1 (2024): 62–80, https://doi.org/10.1163/25903276-bja10056. [Open access]","Adam P. Liff, “Japan's Security Policy in the Abe Era: Radical Transformation or Evolutionary Shift?,” <i>Texas ScholarWorks</i> (2018), https://doi.org/10.15781/t29s1m35c. [Open access]","Mike Mochizuki, “Japan Tests the Nuclear Taboo,” <i>The Nonproliferation Review</i> 14, no. 2 (2007): 303–328, https://doi.org/10.1080/10736700701379393. [Open access]","Erik French, “The US-Japan Alliance and China's Rise: Alliance Strategy and Reassurance, Deterrence, and Compellence,” <i>Syracuse University Libraries</i> (2018), https://surface.syr.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1918&context=etd. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why would Japan accept such a one-sided treaty in 1951?","a":"Because it had no army, no money and no alternative protector, and because accepting American defence meant it could put its resources into rebuilding the economy rather than into rearmament, which is what its leadership wanted."},{"q":"Why did a treaty that reduced the asymmetry cause the bigger protests?","a":"Because the objection was never only to the terms. Renewing the alliance committed Japan to the American side of the Cold War for another generation, and forcing the vote through confirmed the fear that the public had no say in it."},{"q":"Why does the alliance make Japanese rearmament less likely rather than more?","a":"Because the American guarantee, including the nuclear umbrella, removes the strongest argument for acquiring independent capabilities. A Japan that doubted the guarantee would face a very different debate about its own deterrent."}]},
    "pea-035": {"abstract":"<b>Okinawa</b> is the southernmost prefecture of Japan, a chain of subtropical islands lying roughly 1,500 kilometres (930 miles) from Tokyo and closer to Taipei than to its own capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its position explains almost everything about its politics, since it sits at the hinge between the Japanese archipelago, Taiwan and the East China Sea. The islands were an independent kingdom until Japan annexed them in the 19th century, and that separate history still shapes how many islanders see their relationship with the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1945 they were the site of the only major land battle fought on Japanese soil, in which roughly a quarter of the civilian population died. The islands then remained under direct American administration until 1972, twenty years longer than the rest of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That history left a base presence out of all proportion to the prefecture's size, since Okinawa holds a small fraction of Japan's land and population but the great majority of the territory used exclusively by American forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Local opposition is sustained and well organised, driven by aircraft noise, environmental damage, accidents and a series of crimes by American servicemen that have repeatedly brought tens of thousands onto the streets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plan to close the Futenma air station and move its functions elsewhere on the island has been agreed, abandoned and re-agreed since 1996, and it destroyed the credibility of the Democratic Party government that promised to move it off Okinawa entirely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tokyo has continued construction against the declared wishes of the prefecture, including a referendum, which raises an uncomfortable question about how a democracy treats a minority region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The strategic case has only strengthened as tensions over Taiwan have grown, which makes the tension between national security and local consent harder rather than easier to resolve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Carmina Yu Untalan, “Beyond Empire: Okinawa and the politics of American Military bases in Japan,” <i>Political Anthropological Research on International Social Sciences</i> 5, no. 1 (2024): 62–80, https://doi.org/10.1163/25903276-bja10056. [Open access]","Christopher W. Hughes, “Japan as a Global Military Power,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108975025. [Open access]","Adam P. Liff, “Japan's Security Policy in the Abe Era: Radical Transformation or Evolutionary Shift?,” <i>Texas ScholarWorks</i> (2018), https://doi.org/10.15781/t29s1m35c. [Open access]","Erik French, “The US-Japan Alliance and China's Rise: Alliance Strategy and Reassurance, Deterrence, and Compellence,” <i>Syracuse University Libraries</i> (2018), https://surface.syr.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1918&context=etd. [Open access]","Christopher W. Hughes, “Japan’s Strategic Trajectory and Collective Self-Defense: Essential Continuity or Radical Shift?,” <i>Journal of Japanese Studies</i> 43, no. 1 (2017): 93–126, https://doi.org/10.1353/jjs.2017.0005. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the base concentration here treated as a political problem?","a":"Because the burden and the benefit are separated: the whole country receives the security, while one poor prefecture with a separate history absorbs the noise, the land loss and the accidents, and has voted repeatedly against doing so."},{"q":"Why has moving Futenma proved impossible for thirty years?","a":"Because every workable alternative site is somewhere, and no other prefecture will accept it. Keeping the burden on Okinawa is the only option that costs a national government nothing politically outside Okinawa."},{"q":"Why does the geography matter more now than it did in 1972?","a":"Because the islands sit directly between the Chinese coast and the Pacific and close to Taiwan, so a crisis there would run through them, which strengthens the military case exactly as local consent has been most clearly withheld."}]},
    "pea-036": {"abstract":"The <b>Japan Self-Defence Forces</b> are the country's armed forces, founded in 1954 despite a constitution stating that land, sea and air forces will never be maintained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They comprise a Ground, a Maritime and an Air Self-Defence Force, and the careful naming is itself part of the constitutional argument rather than an accident of translation. The orthodox legal defence holds that a state retains an inherent right of self-defence, so forces kept strictly for that purpose do not amount to the war potential the text forbids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their equipment and budget nonetheless place them among the best-funded militaries in the world, which is hard to reconcile with the plain words of the constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Successive governments have therefore maintained an elaborate set of self-imposed limits, covering weapons that might be called offensive, arms exports and any use of force abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Those limits have been loosened steadily over the past two decades, beginning with deployments to peacekeeping operations and disaster relief and extending to anti-piracy patrols off Somalia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The decisive change came with the 2014 cabinet decision on collective self-defence and the security legislation that followed, which allows Japanese forces to act in support of an ally under attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> More recently the government has committed to roughly doubling defence spending and to acquiring counterstrike capabilities, a shift accomplished with far less controversy than earlier and smaller steps had provoked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Public attitudes have moved alongside the policy, helped by the forces' visible role in the response to the 2011 earthquake and tsunami, which did more for their standing than any argument could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What has not changed is the constitutional position, so Japan continues to field a large modern military under a clause declaring that it maintains none at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Christopher W. Hughes, “Japan as a Global Military Power,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108975025. [Open access]","Christopher W. Hughes, “Japan’s Strategic Trajectory and Collective Self-Defense: Essential Continuity or Radical Shift?,” <i>Journal of Japanese Studies</i> 43, no. 1 (2017): 93–126, https://doi.org/10.1353/jjs.2017.0005. [Open access]","Adam P. Liff, “Japan's Security Policy in the Abe Era: Radical Transformation or Evolutionary Shift?,” <i>Texas ScholarWorks</i> (2018), https://doi.org/10.15781/t29s1m35c. [Open access]","Erik French, “The US-Japan Alliance and China's Rise: Alliance Strategy and Reassurance, Deterrence, and Compellence,” <i>Syracuse University Libraries</i> (2018), https://surface.syr.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1918&context=etd. [Open access]","Craig Martin, “The Legitimacy of Informal Constitutional Amendment and the ‘Reinterpretation’ of Japan's War Powers,” <i>Fordham International Law Journal</i> 40, no. 2 (2016): 427, https://ir.lawnet.fordham.edu/ilj/vol40/iss2/3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the name of each branch matter constitutionally?","a":"Because calling them self-defence forces rather than an army, navy and air force is the claim being made in miniature: they are presented as something other than the forces the constitution forbids, and the vocabulary carries that argument everywhere."},{"q":"Why did doubling the budget provoke less outcry than earlier smaller steps?","a":"Because the security environment had changed public opinion first. North Korean missiles and Chinese pressure near the southern islands made the case in a way no government argument had, so the politics followed the perceived threat."},{"q":"Why did disaster relief do more for the forces than any argument?","a":"Because it showed them doing something nobody disputes. A public uneasy about a military it was told should not exist met it instead as the organisation digging out its own towns after the tsunami."}]},
    "pea-037": {"abstract":"The <b>National Diet</b> is the legislature of Japan and, under the 1947 constitution, the highest organ of state power and the sole law-making authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It has two chambers whose different rhythms shape almost everything about Japanese politics. The House of Representatives has 465 seats and four-year terms that are almost never completed, because the prime minister may ask for the chamber to be dissolved at any moment of political advantage, and only the election of 1976 followed a full term.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The House of Councillors has 248 seats and six-year terms, with half the chamber renewed every three years, and it cannot be dissolved at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The lower house holds primacy over the budget, over treaties and over the designation of the prime minister, and can override the upper chamber on ordinary legislation by a two-thirds majority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> It alone may pass a resolution of no confidence in the cabinet, which obliges the government either to resign or to dissolve the chamber and fight an election.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The upper house is far from powerless, though, and a government that loses control of it faces the condition Japanese commentators call a twisted Diet, in which legislation stalls and prime ministers fall in quick succession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because the chambers are elected on different cycles and different rules, split control is a recurring feature of the system rather than an accident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The electoral reform of 1994 changed how the lower house is filled and, with it, what candidates campaign on, shifting attention from local favours towards national policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The building itself, in the Nagatachō district of Tokyo, has lent its name to Japanese political life much as Westminster or Capitol Hill have elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Kenneth Mori McElwain, “The Nationalization of Japanese Elections,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 12, no. 3 (2012): 323–350, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800008055. [Open access]","Amy Catalinac, “Positioning under Alternative Electoral Systems: Evidence from Japanese Candidate Election Manifestos,” <i>American Political Science Review</i> 112, no. 1 (2017): 31–48, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0003055417000399. [Open access]","Adam P. Liff and Ko Maeda, “Electoral incentives, policy compromise, and coalition durability: Japan's LDP–Komeito Government in a mixed electoral system,” <i>Japanese Journal of Political Science</i> 20, no. 1 (2018): 53–73, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1468109918000415. [Open access]","Nathan Beaulieu Batto et al., “Mixed-Member Electoral Systems in Constitutional Context,” <i>University of Michigan Press eBooks</i> (2016), https://doi.org/10.3998/mpub.8084028. [Open access]","Steven R. Reed, “Japanese Electoral Politics,” (2004), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203458501. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a four-year term almost never served out?","a":"Because dissolution is the prime minister's to time, so an election is called when polling is favourable rather than when the clock runs down. Waiting for the full term means fighting at the moment of least advantage."},{"q":"Why does an upper house that cannot be dissolved cause governments to fall?","a":"Because a prime minister cannot resolve a deadlock there by appealing to voters, as he can with the lower house. Legislation stalls, the government looks impotent, and his own party replaces him to break the impasse."},{"q":"Why do different election cycles make split control likely?","a":"Because the two chambers sample opinion at different moments and under different rules, so a party can hold a majority won in one political climate while facing a chamber elected in another."}]},
    "pea-038": {"abstract":"<b>Sekihairitsu</b>, the narrow-loss ratio, is the mechanism that joins the two halves of Japan's mixed electoral system together, and it explains an oddity that puzzles most newcomers to Japanese politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lower house is filled partly from single-member districts decided by first past the post and partly by proportional representation in eleven regional blocs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Parties are permitted to enter the same person both as a district candidate and on their regional list, a practice known as dual candidacy. When several dual candidates are placed at the identical rank on that list, the tie is broken by the ratio of each one's district vote to the winning candidate's, expressed as a percentage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A politician who lost by a hair therefore takes precedence over one beaten heavily, and may enter the Diet despite having lost, which Japanese political slang calls being revived as a zombie. <br><br> The arrangement has a defensible logic, since it lets a party retain able politicians who happen to sit in districts dominated by an opponent, and it softens the harshness of first past the post.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It also gives losing candidates a reason to campaign hard in hopeless seats, because the margin of defeat determines their own survival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The wider 1994 reform that created it was meant to end the money politics of the old multi-member districts, where candidates of the same party had competed against one another for the same voters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Evidence from candidate manifestos suggests it worked in one respect at least, since campaigning shifted measurably away from local favours and towards national policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Critics reply that reviving a defeated candidate undermines the whole point of a district contest, because voters can reject someone at the ballot box and find that same person in parliament a week later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Nathan Beaulieu Batto et al., “Mixed-Member Electoral Systems in Constitutional Context,” <i>University of Michigan Press eBooks</i> (2016), https://doi.org/10.3998/mpub.8084028. [Open access]","Amy Catalinac, “Positioning under Alternative Electoral Systems: Evidence from Japanese Candidate Election Manifestos,” <i>American Political Science Review</i> 112, no. 1 (2017): 31–48, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0003055417000399. [Open access]","Kenneth Mori McElwain, “The Nationalization of Japanese Elections,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 12, no. 3 (2012): 323–350, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800008055. [Open access]","Steven R. Reed, “Japanese Electoral Politics,” (2004), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203458501. [Open access]","Adam P. Liff and Ko Maeda, “Electoral incentives, policy compromise, and coalition durability: Japan's LDP–Komeito Government in a mixed electoral system,” <i>Japanese Journal of Political Science</i> 20, no. 1 (2018): 53–73, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1468109918000415. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why let a party enter the same candidate twice?","a":"Because a strong politician may sit in a district the party cannot win, and losing them wastes talent the party needs. Dual candidacy keeps them available while still making them fight for the seat."},{"q":"Why rank the revived candidates by how narrowly they lost?","a":"Because it rewards effort in the districts rather than standing with the party leadership. A candidate who closed the gap has demonstrably done the work, which a fixed list position would not measure at all."},{"q":"Why did the 1994 reform change what candidates campaign on?","a":"Because under the old multi-member districts, rivals from the same party could not differ on party policy, so they competed on local favours instead. One candidate per party per district makes national policy the thing to run on."}]},
    "pea-039": {"abstract":"The <b>Emperor</b> of Japan occupies a position the 1947 constitution defines with unusual precision, as the symbol of the state and of the unity of the people, deriving that position from the will of the people in whom sovereign power resides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> This was a deliberate demotion from the prewar order, in which the emperor had been sovereign and sacred and in whose name the war had been fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He performs a list of ceremonial acts set out in the constitution, including promulgating laws, convoking the Diet, dissolving the lower house and attesting appointments, and every one of them requires the advice and approval of the cabinet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He has no powers related to government and, unlike many constitutional monarchs, not even a formal advisory role. Japanese eras are still counted by reign, so dates are commonly given in the Heisei or Reiwa calendar as well as the Western one. <br><br> The modern institution has been shaped above all by how its occupants have chosen to interpret a role with no formal content.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Akihito, who reigned from 1989, used the symbolic function to travel to former battlefields, to express remorse for the war and to visit the victims of earthquakes and tsunamis, giving the throne a quietly pacifist and consolatory character.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 2016 he signalled in a televised address that age was making his duties difficult, and the Diet passed a one-off law permitting him to abdicate, which he did in 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was the first Japanese abdication in more than two hundred years, and the special legislation deliberately avoided creating any general right to step down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The succession rules remain contested, since only males in the male line may inherit and a shrinking imperial family has brought the question of a reigning empress close to practical necessity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Noah Berlin, “Constitutional Conflict with the Japanese Imperial Role: Accession, Yasukuni Shrine, and Obligatory Reformation,” <i>Journal of Constitutional Law</i> 1, no. 2 (1998): 383, https://scholarship.law.upenn.edu/jcl/vol1/iss2/8. [Open access]","Ben-Ami Shillony and Anthony Best, “Japanese Monarchy: Past and Present,” <i>LSE Research Online</i> (2006), http://eprints.lse.ac.uk/6874/1/Japanese_Monarchy_Past_and_Present.pdf. [Open access]","Karol Żakowski and Sylwia Łagnowska, “Symbols Matter: Imperial Diplomacy in Postwar Japan,” <i>Toruńskie Studia Międzynarodowe</i> 1, no. 15 (2022), https://doi.org/10.12775/tis.2022.005. [Open access]","Lianyou Guo, “Research Trends in China on the Japanese Emperor System,” <i>Institutional Repositories DataBase</i> (2022): 49–59, https://doi.org/10.15055/00007952. [Open access]","John M. Breen, “Abdication, Succession and Japan's Imperial Future: An Emperor's Dilemma,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 17, no. 2 (2019), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466019015286. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why strip the throne of power rather than abolish it in 1947?","a":"Because the occupation judged that keeping the emperor made the surrender and the reforms acceptable to a population that might otherwise have resisted, while removing his sovereignty prevented the institution being used as it had been before."},{"q":"Why did the abdication need its own special law?","a":"Because the Imperial House Law makes no provision for one, and writing a general right into it would have raised the question of whether an emperor could be pressured to leave, which the government preferred not to open."},{"q":"Why is a role with no formal content still politically significant?","a":"Because what the occupant chooses to do with it sets a tone the government cannot easily contradict. An emperor who visits battlefields and expresses remorse makes a public statement about the war without exercising any power at all."}]},
    "pea-040": {"abstract":"The <b>1955 System</b> is the name given to the pattern of Japanese party politics that held from that year until 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was created by two mergers in quick succession: the left and right wings of the socialist movement reunited, and the two conservative parties responded by combining into the Liberal Democratic Party. What looked like the birth of a two-party system turned out to be nothing of the kind, because one of the two never came close to governing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Liberal Democrats governed without interruption for 38 years while the Japan Socialist Party, the permanent opposition, never held more than about a third of the seats in the lower house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Commentators therefore describe the arrangement as a one-and-a-half party system, a label also applied to postwar Italy and Sweden. <br><br> The socialist third of the chamber was nonetheless decisive, because constitutional revision requires two-thirds of both houses, and that arithmetic is the reason Article 9 was never amended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Real political competition migrated inside the ruling party, where factions functioned almost as parties in their own right, trading the premiership among themselves and distributing posts and funds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The electoral system encouraged exactly this, since the old multi-member districts forced candidates of the same party to compete against one another on anything except party policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Continuous economic growth meanwhile persuaded most voters that the governing formula worked, and the Cold War made a Marxist alternative look risky as well as unappealing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The system ended in 1993 when the Liberal Democrats split and an opposition coalition took office, but its central feature has largely survived, since the party has been out of power for only about four years since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Kenneth Mori McElwain, “The Nationalization of Japanese Elections,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 12, no. 3 (2012): 323–350, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800008055. [Open access]","Steven R. Reed, “Japanese Electoral Politics,” (2004), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203458501. [Open access]","Amy Catalinac, “Positioning under Alternative Electoral Systems: Evidence from Japanese Candidate Election Manifestos,” <i>American Political Science Review</i> 112, no. 1 (2017): 31–48, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0003055417000399. [Open access]","Nathan Beaulieu Batto et al., “Mixed-Member Electoral Systems in Constitutional Context,” <i>University of Michigan Press eBooks</i> (2016), https://doi.org/10.3998/mpub.8084028. [Open access]","Adam P. Liff and Ko Maeda, “Electoral incentives, policy compromise, and coalition durability: Japan's LDP–Komeito Government in a mixed electoral system,” <i>Japanese Journal of Political Science</i> 20, no. 1 (2018): 53–73, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1468109918000415. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a permanently losing opposition still matter?","a":"Because holding just over a third of the lower house was enough to block constitutional revision, which needs two-thirds. The socialists could never govern, but they could and did prevent the one change the conservatives most wanted."},{"q":"Why did competition move inside the governing party?","a":"Because that was where power actually changed hands. With the opposition unable to win, the contest that decided who became prime minister happened among the ruling party's factions rather than between parties at an election."},{"q":"Why did the old districts push candidates towards local favours?","a":"Because several members were elected per district, so candidates of the same party ran against each other. They could not differ on party policy, leaving personal networks and constituency spending as the way to stand out."}]},
    "pea-041": {"abstract":"The <b>Liberal Democratic Party</b> has governed Japan for almost the whole postwar period and is among the most electorally successful parties in any democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was formed in 1955 by the merger of two conservative parties, assembled in large part to keep the reunified socialists out of office. Despite the name it is neither liberal nor especially democratic in the European sense, being a broad conservative coalition held together by the distribution of office rather than by doctrine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its internal divisions are real and durable, running between mainstream and anti-mainstream conservatives with a more progressive wing that has never quite disappeared, and the sharpest recurring quarrel concerns relations with the United States and with Japan's Asian neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its power base has always lain in the countryside, whose votes the electoral system weights generously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What sustains it is less ideology than an apparatus, built on dense connections to big business and the ministries and on constituency organisations that deliver public works to loyal districts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its defenders argue that this stability produced the postwar economic miracle and a reliable supply of leaders experienced enough to govern from their first day in office. Its critics point to the same arrangements as a description of systemic corruption, in which politicians, officials and firms are too closely intertwined for any of them to be properly accountable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The party's real secret, though, is flexibility, since it has repeatedly absorbed the positions of its opponents, from pollution control to welfare expansion, leaving them with nothing distinctive to offer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The cost of such dominance is that opposition views are almost never represented in government and that structural problems can be deferred indefinitely rather than confronted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Steven R. Reed, “Japanese Electoral Politics,” (2004), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203458501. [Open access]","Adam P. Liff and Ko Maeda, “Electoral incentives, policy compromise, and coalition durability: Japan's LDP–Komeito Government in a mixed electoral system,” <i>Japanese Journal of Political Science</i> 20, no. 1 (2018): 53–73, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1468109918000415. [Open access]","Kenneth Mori McElwain, “The Nationalization of Japanese Elections,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 12, no. 3 (2012): 323–350, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800008055. [Open access]","Phillip Y. Lipscy and Ethan Scheiner, “Japan Under the DPJ: The Paradox of Political Change Without Policy Change,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 12, no. 3 (2012): 311–322, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800008043. [Open access]","Amy Catalinac, “Positioning under Alternative Electoral Systems: Evidence from Japanese Candidate Election Manifestos,” <i>American Political Science Review</i> 112, no. 1 (2017): 31–48, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0003055417000399. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does absorbing opponents' policies protect a governing party?","a":"Because it removes the reason to vote against it. If the government adopts the popular parts of the opposition programme, a voter who likes those policies no longer has to change the government to get them."},{"q":"Why does a rural base matter more than its share of the population?","a":"Because district boundaries have lagged behind decades of migration to the cities, so a rural vote carries more weight than an urban one, and a party strong in the countryside wins more seats than its national vote alone would give."},{"q":"Why can long dominance defer structural problems rather than solve them?","a":"Because reform means imposing losses on the business, bureaucratic and constituency interests the party depends on, and a government facing no credible replacement has little reason to pay that price."}]},
    "pea-042": {"abstract":"The <b>Democratic Party of Japan</b> was the strongest opposition party to emerge after the collapse of the 1955 System and the only one since 1955 to win a genuine mandate to govern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was assembled in 1998 from several existing parties and carried members whose politics ranged from conservative through liberal to social democratic, which gave it breadth but very little coherence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its programme was generally moderate, promising support directed at households rather than at construction projects, and its principal appeal lay in offering a different style of leadership. Its landslide in August 2009, fought on the simple slogan of a change of government, produced the first genuine alternation of governing party in more than half a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The central promise was constitutional in spirit rather than in law: to shift power from career officials to elected politicians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Prime Minister Hatoyama Yukio proposed to put ministries under genuine political control, to abolish the vice-ministerial meetings through which senior bureaucrats coordinated policy before cabinet saw it, and to bar officials from taking political decisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also sought to rebalance the security relationship with the United States, in particular over the bases on Okinawa, and to reset relations within Asia. The attempt failed comprehensively, and the reasons are instructive: the party's appointees had almost no policy-making experience, the bureaucratic networks proved immovable, and his successor had reversed much of the programme by 2011.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Scholars summarise the episode as political change without policy change, since alternation in office altered far less than either supporters or opponents had expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party never recovered from the impression of incompetence, and after splitting repeatedly it dissolved in 2016, leaving two successor parties behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Phillip Y. Lipscy and Ethan Scheiner, “Japan Under the DPJ: The Paradox of Political Change Without Policy Change,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 12, no. 3 (2012): 311–322, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800008043. [Open access]","Giulio Pugliese, “Kantei diplomacy? Japan's hybrid leadership in foreign and security policy,” <i>The Pacific Review</i> 30, no. 2 (2016): 152–168, https://doi.org/10.1080/09512748.2016.1201131. [Open access]","Tina Burrett, “Explaining Japan's Revolving Door Premiership: Applying the Leadership Capital Index,” <i>Politics and Governance</i> 4, no. 2 (2016): 36–53, https://doi.org/10.17645/pag.v4i2.575. [Open access]","Steven R. Reed, “Japanese Electoral Politics,” (2004), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203458501. [Open access]","Kenneth Mori McElwain, “The Nationalization of Japanese Elections,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 12, no. 3 (2012): 323–350, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800008055. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did political appointees struggle to control the ministries?","a":"Because the officials held the expertise, drafted the legislation and controlled the information the ministers saw. A politician arriving without policy experience depends on the very people he is trying to direct."},{"q":"Why is political change without policy change a paradox worth explaining?","a":"Because alternation in office is supposed to be how democracies change direction. When a new party wins a landslide and governs much as the old one did, the mechanism voters rely on has visibly failed to deliver."},{"q":"Why did breadth of membership become a weakness in government?","a":"Because a coalition assembled to defeat the LDP had no agreed programme for what to do afterwards. In opposition disagreement costs nothing; in office every decision exposes it, and the party split along those lines."}]},
    "pea-043": {"abstract":"The <b>Iron Triangle</b> is the standard shorthand for the relationship between Japan's governing politicians, its career bureaucrats and its large corporations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each side supplies something the others need: business provides political funding and organised votes, politicians provide favourable legislation and public contracts, and the ministries provide the regulatory decisions and licences on which firms depend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bureaucracy is the unusual element by comparative standards, because Japanese ministries have historically drafted most legislation themselves and were staffed by graduates who expected to guide national development rather than merely to administer it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The relationship is sealed by the practice known as amakudari, literally descent from heaven, in which senior officials retire into well-paid positions at the companies they formerly regulated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of the national press is tied into the same arrangement through the exclusive press club system. <br><br> Defenders of the model argue that it produced the postwar economic miracle, letting long-term industrial strategy be pursued without the disruption of alternating governments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The criticisms bear directly on the country's recent difficulties, since accountability is weak when none of the three partners has any interest in exposing the others, and a regulator expecting a job in the industry is unlikely to be severe with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The official inquiry into the Fukushima accident made exactly this argument, finding that collusion between the regulator, the utility and the government had allowed known risks to go unaddressed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The arrangement also entrenches stagnation, because the structural reforms the economy has needed for three decades would impose losses on precisely the interests the triangle exists to protect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Breaking it was the central promise of the government elected in 2009, and the failure of that attempt is the clearest measure of how durable it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Iida Tetsunari and Andrew DeWit, “The ‘Power Elite’ and Environmental-Energy Policy in Japan,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 9, no. 4 (2011): 1–23, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466011010801. [Open access]","Phillip Y. Lipscy and Ethan Scheiner, “Japan Under the DPJ: The Paradox of Political Change Without Policy Change,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 12, no. 3 (2012): 311–322, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800008043. [Open access]","Hiroshi Ohta, “The Analysis of Japan's Energy and Climate Policy from the Aspect of Anticipatory Governance,” <i>Energies</i> 13, no. 19 (2020): 5153, https://doi.org/10.3390/en13195153. [Open access]","Steven R. Reed, “Japanese Electoral Politics,” (2004), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203458501. [Open access]","Tina Burrett, “Explaining Japan's Revolving Door Premiership: Applying the Leadership Capital Index,” <i>Politics and Governance</i> 4, no. 2 (2016): 36–53, https://doi.org/10.17645/pag.v4i2.575. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the prospect of a later job weaken a regulator?","a":"Because the official deciding today whether to enforce a rule may be applying next year for a post at the firm affected. Nothing needs to be said for that expectation to shape how severely the rule is applied."},{"q":"Why can the same arrangement explain both the miracle and the stagnation?","a":"Because coordination between state and industry is an advantage when the task is catching up along a known path, and a liability when the task is changing direction, since the same tight relationships now protect what exists."},{"q":"Why was Fukushima treated as a governance failure rather than a natural disaster?","a":"Because the tsunami was foreseeable and the risk had been raised. The inquiry concluded that the regulator, the utility and the government were too close for those warnings to be acted on, which is a failure of institutions rather than of engineering."}]},
    "pea-044": {"abstract":"The <b>Fukushima disaster</b> began on 11 March 2011, when a magnitude nine earthquake off the coast of Tōhoku sent a tsunami over the sea wall of the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear plant and destroyed the power supply that cooled its reactors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Three of the six reactors suffered meltdowns and hydrogen explosions tore apart their buildings over the following days. Well over a hundred thousand people were evacuated, many of them permanently, while the earthquake and tsunami together killed close to twenty thousand along the north-eastern coast. The nuclear accident itself caused very few deaths from radiation, but the displacement, the loss of livelihoods and the long strain on evacuated communities were severe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was the gravest nuclear accident since Chernobyl in 1986 and remains the defining domestic crisis of modern Japan, still shaping energy and safety policy today. <br><br> Politically the episode was devastating for the governing Democratic Party of Japan, which had been in office barely eighteen months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The response was widely judged chaotic, information reached the public late and incompletely, and the relationship between the prime minister's office and the plant's operator broke down in public view.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An official inquiry commissioned by the Diet went considerably further, concluding that the accident was a profoundly man-made disaster rooted in collusion between the regulator, the industry and the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is the iron triangle of Japanese politics applied to nuclear power, and it turned a natural catastrophe into an indictment of how the country is governed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The accident also reshaped energy politics for a decade, shutting the entire reactor fleet for a time and making opposition to nuclear power a defining position for the parties of the centre-left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Iida Tetsunari and Andrew DeWit, “The ‘Power Elite’ and Environmental-Energy Policy in Japan,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 9, no. 4 (2011): 1–23, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466011010801. [Open access]","Phillip Y. Lipscy and Ethan Scheiner, “Japan Under the DPJ: The Paradox of Political Change Without Policy Change,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 12, no. 3 (2012): 311–322, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800008043. [Open access]","Hiroshi Ohta, “The Analysis of Japan's Energy and Climate Policy from the Aspect of Anticipatory Governance,” <i>Energies</i> 13, no. 19 (2020): 5153, https://doi.org/10.3390/en13195153. [Open access]","Aleh Cherp et al., “Integrating techno-economic, socio-technical and political perspectives on national energy transitions: A meta-theoretical framework,” <i>Energy Research & Social Science</i> 37 (2018): 175–190, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.erss.2017.09.015. [Open access]","Tina Burrett, “Explaining Japan's Revolving Door Premiership: Applying the Leadership Capital Index,” <i>Politics and Governance</i> 4, no. 2 (2016): 36–53, https://doi.org/10.17645/pag.v4i2.575. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why call a disaster triggered by a tsunami man-made?","a":"Because the size of possible waves had been studied and warnings had been raised, so the failure lay in the decision not to act on them. The earthquake was unavoidable; the unprotected sea wall was a choice."},{"q":"Why did the accident damage the DPJ rather than the industry alone?","a":"Because a government is judged on how it handles a crisis, and this one looked improvised and evasive on television for weeks. The party had promised competent political control and was seen to lack it when it mattered most."},{"q":"Why did one accident reorder Japanese energy politics for a decade?","a":"Because it made nuclear power a question of trust in institutions rather than of engineering risk. Voters were asked to believe assurances from the same regulator and utility the inquiry had just described as too close to each other."}]},
    "pea-045": {"abstract":"<b>Abenomics</b> was the attempt to pull Japan out of two decades of stagnation and deflation, presented from the start as three arrows fired together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first was monetary, an unprecedented programme of quantitative easing aimed at a two percent inflation target and at driving down the value of the yen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The second was fiscal, a series of large stimulus packages directed at public works and disaster reconstruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The third was structural reform, meaning the deregulation of protected sectors such as agriculture, energy and labour, together with measures intended to bring more women and older workers into the workforce. The design was coherent enough on paper, since the first two arrows were meant to buy the political time in which the painful third could be fired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The results were mixed in a way that is revealing about Japanese political economy more generally, since each arrow met a different kind of obstacle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The monetary arrow worked in its own terms, weakening the yen, lifting corporate profits and share prices sharply and ending outright deflation, while unemployment fell to very low levels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The inflation target was nonetheless missed for years, wages barely moved in real terms, and household consumption stayed weak, so the gains were felt far more by firms and shareholders than by ordinary families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The third arrow was the one that mattered most and was largely never fired, because structural reform meant confronting exactly the entrenched interests that sustain the governing party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Critics add that the programme deepened inequality and left an enormous public debt with the central bank holding much of it, so its lasting significance is as evidence of how hard genuine structural change proves to be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Joshua K. Hausman and Johannes Wieland, “Overcoming the Lost Decades? Abenomics after Three Years,” <i>Brookings Papers on Economic Activity</i> 2015, no. 2 (2015): 385–431, https://doi.org/10.1353/eca.2015.0008. [Open access]","Ayako Saiki and Jon Frost, “Does unconventional monetary policy affect inequality? Evidence from Japan,” <i>Applied Economics</i> 46, no. 36 (2014): 4445–4454, https://doi.org/10.1080/00036846.2014.962229. [Open access]","Mahito Uchida, “Towards the end of deflation in Japan? Monetary policy under Abenomics and the role of the central bank,” <i>Revue de l'OFCE</i> (2014), https://doi.org/10.3917/reof.135.0243. [Open access]","Elif C. Arbatli Saxegaard et al., “Policy uncertainty in Japan,” <i>Journal of the Japanese and International Economies</i> 64 (2022): 101192, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jjie.2022.101192. [Open access]","Lars E. O. Svensson, “Escaping from a Liquidity Trap and Deflation: The Foolproof Way and Others,” <i>The Journal of Economic Perspectives</i> 17, no. 4 (2003): 145–166, https://doi.org/10.1257/089533003772034934. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the first two arrows supposed to come before the third?","a":"Because deregulation imposes immediate losses on protected industries and delivers gains only later. Easy money and stimulus were meant to create the growth and the political capital that would make those losses survivable."},{"q":"Why did share prices rise while wages did not?","a":"Because a weaker yen lifts the reported profits of exporters without obliging them to pay more. Firms banked the gain as retained earnings and dividends, and nothing in the programme compelled them to pass it to employees."},{"q":"Why is the unfired third arrow the most revealing part?","a":"Because it shows the constraint is political rather than economic. The analysis of what Japan needed was widely shared, but no government could impose the costs on the farmers, small firms and incumbents its own coalition rests on."}]},
    "pea-046": {"abstract":"<b>Abe Shinzō</b> dominated Japanese politics for a decade and left office in 2020 as the longest-serving prime minister in the country's history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He came from a political dynasty, his grandfather Kishi Nobusuke having been a wartime minister who later signed the revised security treaty as prime minister, and that inheritance shaped his ambitions. His first term in 2006 and 2007 ended after a year in illness and scandal, and his return in 2012 was widely underestimated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The central goal of his career was revision of the constitution, and specifically of Article 9, to give the Self-Defence Forces explicit legal standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He never secured it, settling instead for a cabinet reinterpretation in 2014 permitting collective self-defence, followed by security legislation in 2015 that passed amid large public protests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His other lasting change was institutional rather than constitutional, since he strengthened the prime minister's own office and took control of senior bureaucratic appointments in 2014, which gave the post a grip on the ministries no predecessor had managed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Assessments divide predictably. Supporters credit him with moving the country beyond endless self-criticism about the war, with bold multilateral trade agreements, and with a genuinely strategic foreign policy that raised Japan's international standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Opponents charge that he downplayed Japan's wartime record, needlessly alienated China and South Korea with nationalist gestures, and presided over worsening cronyism in a series of favouritism scandals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The judgement most commonly offered is that his real talent was pragmatism, an ability to advance nationalist aims by increments while avoiding the confrontations that had destroyed his first premiership, and his murder in 2022 exposed his party's long entanglement with the Unification Church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Craig Martin, “The Legitimacy of Informal Constitutional Amendment and the ‘Reinterpretation’ of Japan's War Powers,” <i>Fordham International Law Journal</i> 40, no. 2 (2016): 427, https://ir.lawnet.fordham.edu/ilj/vol40/iss2/3. [Open access]","Christopher W. Hughes, “Japan’s Strategic Trajectory and Collective Self-Defense: Essential Continuity or Radical Shift?,” <i>Journal of Japanese Studies</i> 43, no. 1 (2017): 93–126, https://doi.org/10.1353/jjs.2017.0005. [Open access]","Giulio Pugliese, “Kantei diplomacy? Japan's hybrid leadership in foreign and security policy,” <i>The Pacific Review</i> 30, no. 2 (2016): 152–168, https://doi.org/10.1080/09512748.2016.1201131. [Open access]","Tina Burrett, “Explaining Japan's Revolving Door Premiership: Applying the Leadership Capital Index,” <i>Politics and Governance</i> 4, no. 2 (2016): 36–53, https://doi.org/10.17645/pag.v4i2.575. [Open access]","Adam P. Liff, “Japan's Security Policy in the Abe Era: Radical Transformation or Evolutionary Shift?,” <i>Texas ScholarWorks</i> (2018), https://doi.org/10.15781/t29s1m35c. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why settle for reinterpretation rather than keep pushing for revision?","a":"Because revision needed two-thirds of both houses and a referendum, and even at the height of his majority the public was unwilling. Reinterpretation delivered most of the practical effect using only a cabinet decision."},{"q":"Why did controlling senior appointments matter more than it sounds?","a":"Because Japanese ministries had long been self-governing on personnel, so officials answered upward within their own institution. Once promotions ran through the prime minister's office, their incentives pointed at him instead."},{"q":"Why is pragmatism the quality most often credited to him?","a":"Because his first premiership collapsed when he pursued nationalist aims directly. The second advanced the same goals in increments small enough that each one individually was hard to mobilise against."}]},
    "pea-047": {"abstract":"<b>Womenomics</b> was the strand of Abe Shinzō's economic programme concerned with raising the participation and promotion of women, promoted under the slogan of a society where women shine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The term was coined by Kathy Matsui, an analyst at Goldman Sachs, whose work estimated that closing the gender employment gap would raise Japanese output substantially.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That framing explains both the appeal and the limits of the policy, because the case put to government was economic rather than one of rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With the workforce shrinking and the birthrate falling, women represented the largest reserve of underused labour in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Targets were duly announced for the share of leadership positions held by women, and a law of 2015 required large employers to publish their own figures and set out action plans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The results fell well short of the rhetoric, and the episode is now widely cited as a case of tokenism rather than reform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Female employment did rise substantially, but overwhelmingly into part-time and non-regular positions rather than into careers, so the headline participation figure improved while the pay gap and the promotion gap did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The government quietly abandoned its target of thirty percent of leadership positions by 2020, pushing the date back by a full decade once it was clear the figure would not be met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Japanese feminists have remained sceptical throughout, arguing that a policy justified by growth can be dropped the moment growth no longer requires it, and that it left the structures producing inequality untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those structures are the real obstacle, and none of them was seriously addressed: punishing working hours, a shortage of childcare places, and the maternity harassment known as matahara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Helen Macnaughtan, “Womenomics for Japan: is the Abe policy for gendered employment viable in an era of precarity?,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 13, no. 50 (2015), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466015017489. [Open access]","Ayako Kano, “Womenomics and Acrobatics: Why Japanese Feminists Remain Skeptical about Feminist State Policy,” <i>Feminist Encounters</i> 2, no. 1 (2018), https://doi.org/10.20897/femenc.201806. [Open access]","Mark Crawford, “Abe's Womenomics Policy, 2013–2020: Tokenism, Gradualism, or Failed Strategy?,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 19, no. 7 (2021): 1–16, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466021030849. [Open access]","Nobuko Nagase, “Has Abe's Womanomics Worked?,” <i>Asian Economic Policy Review</i> 13, no. 1 (2018): 68–101, https://doi.org/10.1111/aepr.12202. [Open access]","Stephanie Assmann, “Gender Equality in Japan: The Equal Employment Opportunity Law Revisited,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 12, no. 45 (2014), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466014028113. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does an economic justification limit what the policy can achieve?","a":"Because it measures success by labour supplied rather than by equality gained. Getting more women into any paid work satisfies the stated aim, so nothing in the argument requires those jobs to be secure or promotable."},{"q":"Why did participation rise while the pay gap did not?","a":"Because the new employment was concentrated in part-time and non-regular contracts, which sit outside the career track. More women working for lower pay raises participation and widens the average gap at the same time."},{"q":"Why do feminists treat a supportive state policy with suspicion?","a":"Because it was granted on grounds that have nothing to do with rights, and what is granted for economic reasons can be withdrawn for them. A government that later needs women to have children rather than careers can reverse it."}]},
    "pea-048": {"abstract":"The <b>demographic timebomb</b> is the phrase used for the combination of very few births and very long lives that is reshaping Japanese society and politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fertility rate has been below the replacement level since the mid-1970s and now sits near 1.2 children per woman, while life expectancy is among the highest in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population peaked at roughly 128 million in 2008 and has declined every year since, with deaths now exceeding births by several hundred thousand annually. More than a tenth of the population is now over eighty, and rural districts in particular are emptying as the young leave for the cities, leaving closed schools and abandoned houses behind them. The projections are severe enough that governments have spoken of the survival of the nation being at stake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The political consequences run through almost every other issue in this lecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A shrinking workforce supporting a growing retired population strains the pension and health systems and puts a ceiling on growth that no amount of monetary stimulus can lift.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is the reason the position of women became an economic question for government rather than only a social one, and the reason immigration has moved onto the agenda in a country long resistant to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The causes are themselves political, since the cost of raising children, insecure non-regular employment among the young, punishing working hours and the difficulty of combining a career with motherhood all bear directly on the decision to have children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Comparative work suggests policy can raise fertility somewhat, but only where it genuinely reduces the conflict between work and family, which is precisely what Japan has not managed to do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["James Feyrer, Bruce Sacerdote, and Ariel Dora Stern, “Will the Stork Return to Europe and Japan? Understanding Fertility within Developed Nations,” <i>The Journal of Economic Perspectives</i> 22, no. 3 (2008): 3–22, https://doi.org/10.1257/jep.22.3.3. [Open access]","Peter McDonald, “Low Fertility and the State: The Efficacy of Policy,” <i>Population and Development Review</i> 32, no. 3 (2006): 485–510, https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1728-4457.2006.00134.x. [Open access]","Melinda Mills et al., “Why do people postpone parenthood? Reasons and social policy incentives,” <i>Human Reproduction Update</i> 17, no. 6 (2011): 848–860, https://doi.org/10.1093/humupd/dmr026. [Open access]","Nobuko Nagase, “Has Abe's Womanomics Worked?,” <i>Asian Economic Policy Review</i> 13, no. 1 (2018): 68–101, https://doi.org/10.1111/aepr.12202. [Open access]","Helen Macnaughtan, “Womenomics for Japan: is the Abe policy for gendered employment viable in an era of precarity?,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 13, no. 50 (2015), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466015017489. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do cash payments for births achieve so little?","a":"Because the obstacle is rarely the one-off cost. What deters people is the long-run conflict between raising a child and holding a career, and a payment that does nothing about hours, childcare places or job security leaves that conflict intact."},{"q":"Why does the decline put a ceiling on economic growth?","a":"Because output depends on how many people work and how productive they are. With the workforce shrinking every year, productivity has to rise just to keep output level, and stimulus cannot conjure workers who were never born."},{"q":"Why is immigration both the quickest fix and the hardest one?","a":"Because it adds working-age people immediately, without waiting twenty years for a birth to become a worker. It is hardest because it asks a society with little experience of large-scale immigration to change what it takes itself to be."}]},
    "pea-049": {"abstract":"The <b>History Issue</b> is the name given to the continuing dispute between Japan and its neighbours over how its empire and its conduct in the Second World War are officially acknowledged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its recurring flashpoints are well defined: visits by prime ministers to the Yasukuni Shrine, where the war dead are enshrined together with men convicted as war criminals; the approval of school textbooks judged to soften the record; the treatment of the comfort women coerced into military brothels; and compensation for forced wartime labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Japanese governments have issued apologies, most fully in the Murayama statement of 1995, and have made compensation arrangements, notably the agreement with South Korea in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The difficulty is that apology has never been allowed to settle, because subsequent statements by politicians repeatedly appear to qualify what was said before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each such remark reopens the wound and confirms neighbouring publics in the belief that the contrition was never sincere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The dispute is genuinely political rather than merely historical, and it serves domestic purposes on every side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In China the memory of the war is central to the patriotic education campaign, which gives Beijing both an interest in the grievance and a public that can be mobilised by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In South Korea the issue divides conservative governments willing to compromise from progressive ones that reopen settlements, so an agreement rarely survives a change of administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Japan the conservative wing treats endless apology as a humiliation and an obstacle to becoming a normal country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The practical cost is considerable, since Japan and South Korea, two democracies with closely aligned security interests and deeply intertwined economies, find cooperation repeatedly hostage to a quarrel about the past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Hiro Saito, “The History Problem: The Politics of War Commemoration in East Asia,” <i>University of Hawai‘i Press</i> (2016), https://doi.org/10.26530/oapen_625901. [Open access]","Karl Gustafsson, “Memory Politics and Ontological Security in Sino-Japanese Relations,” <i>Asian Studies Review</i> 38, no. 1 (2013): 71–86, https://doi.org/10.1080/10357823.2013.852156. [Open access]","Eun A Jo, “Memory, Institutions, and the Domestic Politics of South Korean–Japanese Relations,” <i>International Organization</i> 76, no. 4 (2022): 767–798, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0020818322000194. [Open access]","Chris Deacon, “(Re)producing the ‘history problem’: memory, identity and the Japan-South Korea trade dispute,” <i>The Pacific Review</i> 35, no. 5 (2021): 789–820, https://doi.org/10.1080/09512748.2021.1897652. [Open access]","Mark Selden, “Japan, the United States and Yasukuni Nationalism: War, Historical Memory and the Future of the Asia Pacific,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 6, no. 9 (2008), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466008007419. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do repeated apologies fail to close the question?","a":"Because an apology is judged by what follows it. When ministers afterwards visit the shrine or dispute the historical record, neighbouring publics read the apology as tactical, so each statement resets the argument rather than ending it."},{"q":"Why does the dispute serve domestic purposes in all three countries?","a":"Because each side gains something at home: Beijing gets a grievance that legitimises the party, Seoul gets an issue that divides its own parties, and Japanese conservatives get a humiliation to campaign against."},{"q":"Why does a settlement rarely survive a change of government in Seoul?","a":"Because a deal made by a conservative administration was contested domestically from the start, so an incoming progressive government can reopen it at low political cost and considerable political benefit."}]},
    "pea-050": {"abstract":"The <b>Gender Parity Law</b> of 2018 asked Japanese political parties to field roughly equal numbers of male and female candidates, and attached no penalty of any kind to ignoring it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was passed against a background that makes Japan an outlier among wealthy democracies, since women hold about 14.6 percent of the seats in the House of Representatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Inter-Parliamentary Union places the country around 147th of 186 on this measure, far below the global average of roughly 27 percent and behind a number of states with no democratic credentials at all. The regional comparisons are the ones that matter most for a course on East Asia, since Taiwan reaches roughly 41 percent, which is 23rd in the world, while South Korea stands near 21 percent and China near 26. Japan therefore sits below every other industrial democracy and below several of its immediate neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Several features of the political system explain the figure rather than culture alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Candidate selection in the dominant party rewards long service in local organisations and the inheritance of a constituency from a relative, both of which favour men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Campaigning demands personal funds and networks that women are less likely to command, and the working culture of the Diet assumes a member with a spouse at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Public hostility is not the obstacle it is often assumed to be, since survey work finds substantial support for state intervention once respondents are asked about quotas directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The opposition parties comply far more than the governing party does, which suggests the imbalance is partly a consequence of one-party dominance rather than of Japanese society as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Mari Miura, Kenneth Mori McElwain, and Tomoki Kaneko, “Explaining Public Support for Gender Quotas: Sexism, Representational Quality, and State Intervention in Japan,” <i>Politics & Gender</i> 19, no. 3 (2022): 781–805, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1743923x22000617. [Open access]","Ayako Kano, “Womenomics and Acrobatics: Why Japanese Feminists Remain Skeptical about Feminist State Policy,” <i>Feminist Encounters</i> 2, no. 1 (2018), https://doi.org/10.20897/femenc.201806. [Open access]","Mark Crawford, “Abe's Womenomics Policy, 2013–2020: Tokenism, Gradualism, or Failed Strategy?,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 19, no. 7 (2021): 1–16, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466021030849. [Open access]","Stephanie Assmann, “Gender Equality in Japan: The Equal Employment Opportunity Law Revisited,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 12, no. 45 (2014), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466014028113. [Open access]","Helen Macnaughtan, “Womenomics for Japan: is the Abe policy for gendered employment viable in an era of precarity?,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 13, no. 50 (2015), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466015017489. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why pass a law with no penalty attached?","a":"Because it lets a legislature record a commitment without any party having to give up winnable seats to fulfil it. The measure satisfies the demand for action while leaving candidate selection exactly where it was."},{"q":"Why does inheriting a constituency work against women?","a":"Because the seats handed down within families have overwhelmingly gone to sons, so a selection route that rewards inheritance reproduces the existing balance rather than opening the field to newcomers of either sex."},{"q":"Why does public opinion matter less here than is usually assumed?","a":"Because survey evidence finds real support for quotas once people are asked, which shifts the explanation from voters to the parties. The constraint is who gets selected to stand, not who the electorate is willing to vote for."}]},
    "pea-051": {"abstract":"The <b>Japan Socialist Party</b> was the great opposition force of the postwar era and the other half of the 1955 System.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its influence on Japanese politics was considerable despite its permanent exclusion from power, because it set the terms of debate on the constitution, the American alliance and nuclear weapons, and offered a genuinely different vision of the country's future.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its parliamentary strength was also strategically vital, since holding more than a third of the lower house denied the Liberal Democrats the two-thirds majority needed to revise the constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That arithmetic, rather than any settled national consensus, is the reason Article 9 was never amended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was nonetheless perpetually divided between a doctrinaire Marxist left and a pragmatic right, and in 1960 the moderates broke away to found the Democratic Socialist Party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its failure has several intertwined causes worth understanding together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A strongly Marxist programme was a bridge too far for most voters and looked positively risky in the Cold War, especially while living standards rose year after year and appeared to vindicate the capitalist model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sustained growth from 1950 to 1990 steadily eroded its natural constituency, and it proved unable to build a common front with the other opposition parties while remaining so divided internally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The flexibility of the Liberal Democrats compounded the problem, since any popular socialist policy was liable to be adopted by the government and thereby neutralised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its isolation was underlined by the murder of its leader Asanuma Inejirō, stabbed on stage by a young ultranationalist in 1960 in front of television cameras, and the party disintegrated through the 1990s after finally joining a coalition on terms that abandoned its historic positions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Steven R. Reed, “Japanese Electoral Politics,” (2004), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203458501. [Open access]","Jonathan Boyd and Richard J. Samuels, “Nine Lives?: The Politics of Constitutional Reform in Japan,” <i>East-West Center Policy Studies</i> (2005), https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/bitstreams/c1e9a383-a58e-4a2a-9999-57a51898b129/download. [Open access]","Christopher W. Hughes, “Japan’s Strategic Trajectory and Collective Self-Defense: Essential Continuity or Radical Shift?,” <i>Journal of Japanese Studies</i> 43, no. 1 (2017): 93–126, https://doi.org/10.1353/jjs.2017.0005. [Open access]","Kenneth Mori McElwain, “The Nationalization of Japanese Elections,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 12, no. 3 (2012): 323–350, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800008055. [Open access]","Amy Catalinac, “Positioning under Alternative Electoral Systems: Evidence from Japanese Candidate Election Manifestos,” <i>American Political Science Review</i> 112, no. 1 (2017): 31–48, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0003055417000399. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a party that never governed still shape the constitution?","a":"Because blocking requires far fewer seats than governing. A third of one chamber was enough to deny the two-thirds that revision needs, so the socialists determined the outcome on the issue they cared about most."},{"q":"Why did rising living standards hurt a socialist party specifically?","a":"Because its case rested on the failures of capitalism, and voters were experiencing the opposite every year. An argument about exploitation is hard to sustain while wages and security are visibly improving."},{"q":"Why was the governing party's flexibility so damaging to it?","a":"Because any socialist proposal that became popular was simply adopted by the government. The opposition could originate policy but never claim credit for it, which left it permanently without a distinctive offer."}]},
    "pea-052": {"abstract":"The <b>Cabinet</b> holds executive power in Japan and is collectively responsible to the Diet, in the standard parliamentary pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The prime minister is designated by the Diet from among its own members and then formally appointed by the emperor, so the appointment is ceremonial and the designation is what counts. He selects and may dismiss ministers at will, which on paper makes him a commanding figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A majority of those ministers must themselves sit in the Diet, and the constitution requires that all of them be civilians, a clause written directly against the prewar practice by which serving officers held the army and navy portfolios and could bring down a government by refusing to serve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The cabinet is collectively responsible, so a successful vote of no confidence in the lower house obliges it either to resign as a body or to dissolve that chamber within ten days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The reality of prime ministerial power was for decades far weaker than the formal position suggests, for two reasons that run through the postwar period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first is factional politics inside the Liberal Democratic Party, where posts were distributed to balance factions rather than to assemble the most capable team, and ministers were often rotated after barely a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The second is the permanent bureaucracy, whose career officials command the expertise, draft most legislation and have frequently set the terms on which ministers operate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Breaking that second constraint was the central promise of the Democratic Party government in 2009, and its failure did much to destroy that government's reputation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Abe Shinzō came closer than any predecessor to genuine prime ministerial dominance, largely by strengthening the cabinet secretariat and taking control of senior bureaucratic appointments in 2014.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Tina Burrett, “Explaining Japan's Revolving Door Premiership: Applying the Leadership Capital Index,” <i>Politics and Governance</i> 4, no. 2 (2016): 36–53, https://doi.org/10.17645/pag.v4i2.575. [Open access]","Giulio Pugliese, “Kantei diplomacy? Japan's hybrid leadership in foreign and security policy,” <i>The Pacific Review</i> 30, no. 2 (2016): 152–168, https://doi.org/10.1080/09512748.2016.1201131. [Open access]","Steven R. Reed, “Japanese Electoral Politics,” (2004), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203458501. [Open access]","Adam P. Liff and Ko Maeda, “Electoral incentives, policy compromise, and coalition durability: Japan's LDP–Komeito Government in a mixed electoral system,” <i>Japanese Journal of Political Science</i> 20, no. 1 (2018): 53–73, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1468109918000415. [Open access]","Craig Martin, “The Legitimacy of Informal Constitutional Amendment and the ‘Reinterpretation’ of Japan's War Powers,” <i>Fordham International Law Journal</i> 40, no. 2 (2016): 427, https://ir.lawnet.fordham.edu/ilj/vol40/iss2/3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why insist in the constitution that ministers be civilians?","a":"Because before the war the army and navy supplied their own ministers, and could topple a cabinet simply by withdrawing him and refusing a replacement. Requiring civilians removes that veto and makes the military answerable to elected politicians."},{"q":"Why did rotating ministers every year weaken the cabinet?","a":"Because a minister needs time to learn a ministry well enough to direct it. Someone who will be gone within a year depends entirely on the officials briefing him, which hands the initiative back to the bureaucracy."},{"q":"Why did controlling appointments strengthen the prime minister so much?","a":"Because officials had previously been promoted within their own ministries, so their loyalty ran to the institution. Once the prime minister's office decided senior postings, ambition pointed towards him instead."}]},
    "pea-053": {"abstract":"<b>Komeito</b> is the party that grew out of Sōka Gakkai, a large lay Buddhist organisation in the Nichiren tradition, and its name is usually rendered as the clean government party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its founding concern was exactly that, an attack on the corruption of postwar politics, combined with welfare policies aimed at the urban working population among whom its parent movement recruited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The formal link with Sōka Gakkai was severed in the 1970s under constitutional pressure regarding the separation of religion and state, but the movement remains the source of its votes and its formidable capacity to mobilise them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That reliable bloc of several million disciplined voters is the whole basis of its influence, since it can supply the margin of victory in closely fought single-member districts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its combination of social conservatism with a deep commitment to pacifism gives it an unusual position in the Japanese spectrum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Since 1999 it has governed in coalition with the Liberal Democratic Party, an alliance that has lasted far longer than most observers expected of two such different partners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The arrangement is transactional and mutually useful, since the larger party gains the votes it needs in marginal seats and the smaller one gains ministerial office and a seat at the table where policy is made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mixed electoral system is what makes the bargain work, because a party whose support is spread thinly across the country converts it into seats far better through an ally than alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its most significant role has been as a brake on the security ambitions of the conservative right, repeatedly forcing the dilution of proposals on collective self-defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Critics argue it has legitimised policies it was founded to oppose while extracting only modest concessions in return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Adam P. Liff and Ko Maeda, “Electoral incentives, policy compromise, and coalition durability: Japan's LDP–Komeito Government in a mixed electoral system,” <i>Japanese Journal of Political Science</i> 20, no. 1 (2018): 53–73, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1468109918000415. [Open access]","Nathan Beaulieu Batto et al., “Mixed-Member Electoral Systems in Constitutional Context,” <i>University of Michigan Press eBooks</i> (2016), https://doi.org/10.3998/mpub.8084028. [Open access]","Christopher W. Hughes, “Japan’s Strategic Trajectory and Collective Self-Defense: Essential Continuity or Radical Shift?,” <i>Journal of Japanese Studies</i> 43, no. 1 (2017): 93–126, https://doi.org/10.1353/jjs.2017.0005. [Open access]","Craig Martin, “The Legitimacy of Informal Constitutional Amendment and the ‘Reinterpretation’ of Japan's War Powers,” <i>Fordham International Law Journal</i> 40, no. 2 (2016): 427, https://ir.lawnet.fordham.edu/ilj/vol40/iss2/3. [Open access]","Steven R. Reed, “Japanese Electoral Politics,” (2004), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203458501. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a small party with thinly spread support so valuable to a large one?","a":"Because single-member districts are won at the margin. A few thousand disciplined votes that can be directed to an ally decide close seats, which is worth far more to the larger party than the smaller one's national vote share suggests."},{"q":"Why has an alliance between such different parties lasted so long?","a":"Because each gets something it cannot obtain alone: the larger party gets marginal seats, the smaller gets office and influence over legislation. Neither has found a better available partner, so the bargain renews itself."},{"q":"Why does its pacifism constrain a government it belongs to?","a":"Because leaving the coalition would cost the senior partner its majority in close districts. That threat need never be made explicit for security proposals to be softened before they reach the Diet."}]},
    "pea-054": {"abstract":"The <b>Lost Decades</b> are the long period of low growth, deflation and political drift that followed the collapse of Japan's asset bubble at the start of the 1990s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The contrast with what came before is what gives the term its force, because the years from about 1975 to 1989 had combined rapid growth, political stability and a rising cultural confidence that produced talk of Japan as number one. Land and share prices had reached levels that in retrospect were plainly unsustainable, and when they fell the banking system was left carrying enormous bad debts that took a decade to resolve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What followed was not catastrophe but stasis: weak growth, falling prices, stagnant wages and a politics that changed prime ministers frequently while changing very little else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Deflation proved unexpectedly hard to escape, since once people expect prices to fall, spending is deferred and the expectation fulfils itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The consequences for politics are the subject of much of this lecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Economic failure destroyed the performance argument that had sustained Liberal Democratic dominance, opened the way for the collapse of the 1955 System in 1993 and gave the Democratic Party its opening in 2009. Repeated attempts at monetary and fiscal remedy, culminating in Abenomics, lifted asset prices and ended outright deflation without restoring the growth of the earlier era, and the gains went disproportionately to those who held assets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Policy uncertainty itself became a drag, since firms facing an unpredictable policy environment postpone the investment that would lift productivity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One correction is worth holding in mind, since Japan remains a wealthy, safe and broadly comfortable society with excellent public services, and today's pessimism can be overdone exactly as the triumphalism of the 1980s was.","sources":["Joshua K. Hausman and Johannes Wieland, “Overcoming the Lost Decades? Abenomics after Three Years,” <i>Brookings Papers on Economic Activity</i> 2015, no. 2 (2015): 385–431, https://doi.org/10.1353/eca.2015.0008. [Open access]","Ayako Saiki and Jon Frost, “Does unconventional monetary policy affect inequality? Evidence from Japan,” <i>Applied Economics</i> 46, no. 36 (2014): 4445–4454, https://doi.org/10.1080/00036846.2014.962229. [Open access]","Elif C. Arbatli Saxegaard et al., “Policy uncertainty in Japan,” <i>Journal of the Japanese and International Economies</i> 64 (2022): 101192, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jjie.2022.101192. [Open access]","Tina Burrett, “Explaining Japan's Revolving Door Premiership: Applying the Leadership Capital Index,” <i>Politics and Governance</i> 4, no. 2 (2016): 36–53, https://doi.org/10.17645/pag.v4i2.575. [Open access]","Lars E. O. Svensson, “Escaping from a Liquidity Trap and Deflation: The Foolproof Way and Others,” <i>The Journal of Economic Perspectives</i> 17, no. 4 (2003): 145–166, https://doi.org/10.1257/089533003772034934. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is deflation so much harder to escape than inflation?","a":"Because expectations become self-fulfilling. If prices are expected to fall, buyers wait, demand drops and prices fall further, and a central bank cannot cut interest rates below roughly zero to counter it."},{"q":"Why did stagnation matter politically rather than only economically?","a":"Because the governing party's claim rested on delivering prosperity. Once growth stopped, the reason to keep voting for it weakened, which is what made 1993 and 2009 possible at all."},{"q":"Why can the pessimistic view be as overdone as the 1980s hype?","a":"Because headline growth understates how people live. With a shrinking population, output per person has held up far better than the totals suggest, and public services, safety and life expectancy remain among the world's best."}]},
    "pea-055": {"abstract":"<b>Matahara</b>, a contraction of maternity harassment, is the Japanese term for the pressure placed on women to leave work when they become pregnant, and for the demotion or marginalisation that can follow their return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It names one part of a larger structural problem in which the headline employment figures look entirely respectable while the underlying reality is considerably worse than they suggest. A large majority of Japanese women are in employment of some kind, but a far smaller share work full-time, and the gap between those two numbers is where the difficulty lies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Women make up roughly 77 percent of all part-time and non-regular workers, a category that carries lower pay, negligible job security, little training and almost no prospect of promotion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The average woman consequently earns far less than the average man, and the gap widens rather than narrows over a career.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The division between regular and non-regular employment is the key to understanding this.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Japanese labour market is built around a permanent core of employees who receive lifetime security in return for total availability, and that bargain assumes very long hours and a willingness to accept transfers at short notice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Combining it with primary responsibility for children is close to impossible in a country where domestic work remains heavily unequal, and a shortage of licensed daycare places compounds the problem directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Supreme Court ruled in 2014 that demotion on account of pregnancy was unlawful, and legislation has since obliged employers to take preventive measures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The practice persists nonetheless, because enforcement is weak and few women are willing to litigate against an employer they may need a reference from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Helen Macnaughtan, “Womenomics for Japan: is the Abe policy for gendered employment viable in an era of precarity?,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 13, no. 50 (2015), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466015017489. [Open access]","Nobuko Nagase, “Has Abe's Womanomics Worked?,” <i>Asian Economic Policy Review</i> 13, no. 1 (2018): 68–101, https://doi.org/10.1111/aepr.12202. [Open access]","Stephanie Assmann, “Gender Equality in Japan: The Equal Employment Opportunity Law Revisited,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 12, no. 45 (2014), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466014028113. [Open access]","Mark Crawford, “Abe's Womenomics Policy, 2013–2020: Tokenism, Gradualism, or Failed Strategy?,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 19, no. 7 (2021): 1–16, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466021030849. [Open access]","Ayako Kano, “Womenomics and Acrobatics: Why Japanese Feminists Remain Skeptical about Feminist State Policy,” <i>Feminist Encounters</i> 2, no. 1 (2018), https://doi.org/10.20897/femenc.201806. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a high female employment rate conceal the problem?","a":"Because it counts any paid work equally. A shift from unemployed to part-time and insecure raises the rate while leaving pay, security and promotion prospects untouched, so the headline improves as the substance does not."},{"q":"Why does lifetime employment make motherhood so costly?","a":"Because the security is exchanged for total availability: long hours and transfers at short notice. Anyone with primary care of a child cannot meet that condition, so leaving the core track is often the only option."},{"q":"Why does a favourable court ruling change so little in practice?","a":"Because a right has to be claimed to work. Suing an employer is slow, public and career-ending in a labour market where references matter, so most women absorb the treatment rather than test the law."}]},
    "pea-056": {"abstract":"The <b>Constitutional Democratic Party</b> is the largest opposition party in the Japanese Diet and the principal successor to the Democratic Party of Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name announces its central commitment, since it was founded by politicians who objected to the erosion of constitutional government, and above all to the reinterpretation of Article 9 by cabinet decision rather than by amendment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It occupies the centre to centre-left and is clearly the more progressive of the two successors to the old party, the other being the centre-right Democratic Party for the People.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its positions include defence of Article 9 against revision, greater gender equality, the phasing out of nuclear power, public investment in renewable energy and support for same-sex marriage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Opposition to nuclear power in particular became a defining commitment for the parties of the centre-left after the Fukushima accident, and it still separates them sharply from the governing party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Its difficulty is the one that has faced every Japanese opposition since 1955, namely how to become a plausible alternative government rather than a permanent critic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The memory of the DPJ years hangs heavily over it, since many voters unenthusiastic about the Liberal Democrats remain unconvinced that the opposition can govern competently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fragmentation of the non-communist opposition compounds the problem, because the anti-government vote is divided among four parties and split constituencies are exactly what a first-past-the-post district rewards least.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cooperation with the communists brings the arithmetic closer but costs moderate support, which is the bind every opposition leader has faced since the old party collapsed. The electoral system therefore works against it twice over, once through rural weighting and again through the division of its own side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Steven R. Reed, “Japanese Electoral Politics,” (2004), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203458501. [Open access]","Phillip Y. Lipscy and Ethan Scheiner, “Japan Under the DPJ: The Paradox of Political Change Without Policy Change,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 12, no. 3 (2012): 311–322, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800008043. [Open access]","Craig Martin, “The Legitimacy of Informal Constitutional Amendment and the ‘Reinterpretation’ of Japan's War Powers,” <i>Fordham International Law Journal</i> 40, no. 2 (2016): 427, https://ir.lawnet.fordham.edu/ilj/vol40/iss2/3. [Open access]","Hiroshi Ohta, “The Analysis of Japan's Energy and Climate Policy from the Aspect of Anticipatory Governance,” <i>Energies</i> 13, no. 19 (2020): 5153, https://doi.org/10.3390/en13195153. [Open access]","Kenneth Mori McElwain, “The Nationalization of Japanese Elections,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 12, no. 3 (2012): 323–350, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800008055. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why name a party after the constitution rather than a policy?","a":"Because the founders' objection was procedural rather than substantive: that a government had changed what the constitution means without amending it. Naming the party for the document makes defending the process its identity."},{"q":"Why does a divided opposition suffer so badly under this system?","a":"Because single-member districts reward the largest single party in each seat. Four parties splitting the anti-government vote can together outpoll the government and still lose almost every district to it."},{"q":"Why is cooperating with the communists a genuine dilemma?","a":"Because standing aside for one another would win seats the divided opposition loses, but association with the communists drives away the moderate voters the party needs to look like a government in waiting."}]},
    "pea-057": {"abstract":"The <b>Supreme Court</b> stands at the head of the Japanese judiciary and was given the power to determine the constitutionality of any law, order or official act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Chief Justice is designated by the cabinet and appointed by the emperor, the other fourteen justices are appointed by the cabinet, and the court controls appointments and assignments throughout the lower judiciary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Justices face a popular review at the first general election after their appointment and every ten years thereafter, a mechanism that has never once removed anyone. Despite these formal powers the court has struck down statutes on only a handful of occasions in nearly eighty years, a record of restraint with few parallels among established democracies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has been markedly more willing to rule against administrative acts than against the Diet itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Several explanations are offered, and they matter for understanding how the Japanese constitution actually works.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The court has developed a doctrine of political questions under which the most sensitive issues, above all the constitutionality of the Self-Defence Forces, are treated as matters for the elected branches rather than for judges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its control over judicial careers also gives it powerful means to discourage adventurousness among lower-court judges, since assignments and promotions run through the same secretariat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The consequence is that Article 9 has been reshaped by cabinet reinterpretation rather than by judicial ruling, and constitutional argument in Japan is conducted politically rather than in court. The area where the court has been least deferential is electoral malapportionment, repeatedly declaring the weight given to rural votes to be in a state of unconstitutionality while stopping short of voiding the elections concerned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["David S. Law, “Why Has Judicial Review Failed in Japan?,” <i>Washington University Law Review</i> 88, no. 6 (2011): 1425–1466, https://openscholarship.wustl.edu/law_lawreview/vol88/iss6/3. [Open access]","John O. Haley, “Constitutional Adjudication in Japan: Context, Structures, and Values,” <i>Washington University Law Review</i> 88, no. 6 (2011): 1467–1491, https://openscholarship.wustl.edu/law_lawreview/vol88/iss6/4. [Open access]","Frank K. Upham, “Stealth Activism: Norm Formation by Japanese Courts,” <i>Washington University Law Review</i> 88, no. 6 (2011): 1493–1505, https://openscholarship.wustl.edu/law_lawreview/vol88/iss6/5. [Open access]","Tokiyasu Fujita, “The Supreme Court of Japan: Commentary on the Recent Work of Scholars in the United States,” <i>Washington University Law Review</i> 88, no. 6 (2011): 1507–1526, https://journals.library.wustl.edu/lawreview/article/id/7112/download/pdf/. [Open access]","Hiroshi Itoh, “The Role of Precedent at Japan's Supreme Court,” <i>Washington University Law Review</i> 88, no. 6 (2011): 1631–1667, https://openscholarship.wustl.edu/law_lawreview/vol88/iss6/11. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does controlling careers make lower-court judges cautious?","a":"Because the same secretariat decides postings and promotions, and a judge who rules against the government knows it. Nothing needs to be threatened for an ambitious judge to avoid the decision that would mark him out."},{"q":"Why does calling something a political question amount to a decision?","a":"Because declining to rule leaves the government's position standing. Treating the constitutionality of the armed forces as a matter for the elected branches settles it in their favour without the court ever saying so."},{"q":"Why declare malapportionment unconstitutional and then let the election stand?","a":"Because voiding a national election would throw the country into a crisis the court has no means to manage. Naming the breach while withholding the remedy pressures the Diet to act without the judges taking responsibility for the consequences."}]},
    "pea-058": {"abstract":"<b>Kishida Fumio</b> led Japan from 2021 to 2024, having previously served five years as foreign minister under Abe Shinzō.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He belonged to the party's more moderate tradition rather than to its nationalist wing, and came to office promising a new form of capitalism that would redistribute more of the country's wealth towards the middle class. His family comes from Hiroshima and he represents a constituency there, which gave a personal character to his long engagement with nuclear disarmament and non-proliferation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He brought the G7 summit to the city in May 2023 and took the assembled leaders to the Peace Memorial, a piece of political theatre that carried real diplomatic weight because the setting made the argument for him. He showed markedly little interest in revising Article 9, the cause that had animated his predecessor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> His foreign policy was nonetheless consequential and in several respects a clear break with the past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He committed Japan to doubling defence spending towards two percent of national output and to acquiring counterstrike capabilities, a shift justified by the threat from China and North Korea and accomplished with far less controversy than Abe's smaller steps had provoked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He gave strong and early support to Ukraine, framing the invasion as a challenge to the international order with direct implications for East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also worked with the South Korean president to settle the wartime labour dispute that had poisoned relations between the two countries, which is the rare case of the history issue being managed rather than inflamed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Domestically he fared much less well, as inflation eroded living standards and a party funding scandal involving unreported factional income destroyed his poll ratings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Christopher W. Hughes, “Japan as a Global Military Power,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108975025. [Open access]","Erik French, “The US-Japan Alliance and China's Rise: Alliance Strategy and Reassurance, Deterrence, and Compellence,” <i>Syracuse University Libraries</i> (2018), https://surface.syr.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1918&context=etd. [Open access]","Craig Martin, “The Legitimacy of Informal Constitutional Amendment and the ‘Reinterpretation’ of Japan's War Powers,” <i>Fordham International Law Journal</i> 40, no. 2 (2016): 427, https://ir.lawnet.fordham.edu/ilj/vol40/iss2/3. [Open access]","Tina Burrett, “Explaining Japan's Revolving Door Premiership: Applying the Leadership Capital Index,” <i>Politics and Governance</i> 4, no. 2 (2016): 36–53, https://doi.org/10.17645/pag.v4i2.575. [Open access]","Eun A Jo, “Memory, Institutions, and the Domestic Politics of South Korean–Japanese Relations,” <i>International Organization</i> 76, no. 4 (2022): 767–798, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0020818322000194. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a far larger defence increase provoke less resistance than Abe's?","a":"Because public opinion had already moved. North Korean missiles and Chinese pressure near the southern islands made the case that no government argument had managed, so the policy followed the perceived threat rather than leading it."},{"q":"Why do Hiroshima roots carry diplomatic weight?","a":"Because they make a disarmament position hard to dismiss as posturing. A prime minister from the city can host the G7 at the Peace Memorial and have the setting itself do the arguing."},{"q":"Why did a successful foreign policy not save him at home?","a":"Because voters judge a government on prices and on honesty. Inflation was eroding real incomes while a funding scandal ran through his own party, and diplomatic achievements abroad do not answer either complaint."}]},
    "pea-059": {"abstract":"<b>Takaichi Sanae</b> became prime minister of Japan in October 2025, the first woman to hold the office in the country's history. The circumstance is instructive, because the barrier fell not to a campaigner for gender equality but to a politician of the party's right-nationalist wing and a protégée of Abe Shinzō. She is socially conservative, opposing separate surnames for married couples and same-sex marriage, positions that place her at odds with much of the movement for women's advancement in Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Her economic programme is a return to the expansionary approach of Abenomics, favouring fiscal stimulus and loose money over the consolidation her predecessors had drifted towards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She is critical of immigration at a moment when the shrinking workforce makes that question unavoidable, which is the sharpest tension in her whole programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In foreign policy she stands on the hard edge of the Japanese debate, pressing for higher defence spending, closer cooperation with the United States and partners in the region, and a notably firm line towards China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That firmness carries risks the material on the history issue makes plain, since nationalist gestures have repeatedly damaged relations with Beijing and Seoul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Her arrival is a significant fact about Japanese politics in its own right, in a country ranked near the bottom of the industrial world for women in parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether it changes anything for women more broadly is a separate question, and the experience of Womenomics counsels caution about reading symbolism as substance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Her tenure began too recently for any settled assessment, and the account given here rests on the record as it stood at the start of it, so everything above should be read as provisional rather than final.","sources":["Joshua K. Hausman and Johannes Wieland, “Overcoming the Lost Decades? Abenomics after Three Years,” <i>Brookings Papers on Economic Activity</i> 2015, no. 2 (2015): 385–431, https://doi.org/10.1353/eca.2015.0008. [Open access]","Mari Miura, Kenneth Mori McElwain, and Tomoki Kaneko, “Explaining Public Support for Gender Quotas: Sexism, Representational Quality, and State Intervention in Japan,” <i>Politics & Gender</i> 19, no. 3 (2022): 781–805, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1743923x22000617. [Open access]","Christopher W. Hughes, “Japan as a Global Military Power,” <i>Cambridge University Press eBooks</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108975025. [Open access]","Hiro Saito, “The History Problem: The Politics of War Commemoration in East Asia,” <i>University of Hawai‘i Press</i> (2016), https://doi.org/10.26530/oapen_625901. [Open access]","James Feyrer, Bruce Sacerdote, and Ariel Dora Stern, “Will the Stork Return to Europe and Japan? Understanding Fertility within Developed Nations,” <i>The Journal of Economic Perspectives</i> 22, no. 3 (2008): 3–22, https://doi.org/10.1257/jep.22.3.3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the first female prime minister come from the nationalist right?","a":"Because reaching the top requires winning a party leadership, and the governing party's selectorate is conservative. A candidate who reassures that electorate on everything else can be a woman without that being the thing at issue."},{"q":"Why might a female prime minister change little for women generally?","a":"Because representation at the top and conditions at the bottom are separate questions. Womenomics raised participation without touching pay, security or promotion, and one appointment does not alter candidate selection or the labour market."},{"q":"Why is criticising immigration difficult to sustain in this position?","a":"Because the workforce shrinks every year and no domestic measure has reversed it. A government committed to growth and to restricting immigration has to find its workers somewhere, and the arithmetic does not yield."}]},
    "pea-060": {"abstract":"<b>Koike Yuriko</b> has governed Tokyo since 2016 and is the most electorally successful woman in Japanese politics, re-elected for a third term in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She built her career inside the Liberal Democratic Party, serving as environment minister and briefly as defence minister, before leaving to win the metropolitan governorship in a contest against her own party's official candidate. The office matters because Tokyo is larger in population and economic weight than most countries, and because it provides a national platform entirely independent of the Diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> She has used it to campaign on the position of women and on environmental policy, which distinguishes her sharply from most senior figures on the Japanese right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She is nonetheless a conservative nationalist on questions of history and security, which is the combination that makes her hard to place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Her career illustrates something general about how women reach the top in Japanese politics, and about where the real obstacles sit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The routes that work are those that bypass the party candidate-selection machinery, since a directly elected executive office is won from the electorate rather than granted by a local organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is why the country can produce prominent individual women while its parliament remains near the bottom of the industrial world for female representation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She has more than once tried to convert metropolitan success into a national vehicle, founding parties that performed well in opinion polls and then poorly at the general election that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pattern is a familiar one in Japanese politics, where new parties built around a single popular figure rise very quickly in the polls and rarely survive their first contact with the electoral system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Mari Miura, Kenneth Mori McElwain, and Tomoki Kaneko, “Explaining Public Support for Gender Quotas: Sexism, Representational Quality, and State Intervention in Japan,” <i>Politics & Gender</i> 19, no. 3 (2022): 781–805, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1743923x22000617. [Open access]","Kenneth Mori McElwain, “The Nationalization of Japanese Elections,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 12, no. 3 (2012): 323–350, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800008055. [Open access]","Steven R. Reed, “Japanese Electoral Politics,” (2004), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203458501. [Open access]","Nathan Beaulieu Batto et al., “Mixed-Member Electoral Systems in Constitutional Context,” <i>University of Michigan Press eBooks</i> (2016), https://doi.org/10.3998/mpub.8084028. [Open access]","Mark Crawford, “Abe's Womenomics Policy, 2013–2020: Tokenism, Gradualism, or Failed Strategy?,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 19, no. 7 (2021): 1–16, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466021030849. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a directly elected governorship easier for a woman to win?","a":"Because it is decided by voters rather than by a local party organisation. The selection stage that filters out female candidates in district contests simply does not exist, so the obstacle is public opinion, which is far less hostile."},{"q":"Why do parties built around one popular figure keep failing?","a":"Because single-member districts reward organisation on the ground, and a new party has none. Enthusiasm measured in national polls does not convert into the local machinery needed to win seats one district at a time."},{"q":"Why can Japan produce prominent women and still rank near the bottom?","a":"Because the two are measured at different points. Individual success in executive offices says nothing about how many women are selected to contest parliamentary seats, which is what the international ranking counts."}]},
    "pea-061": {"abstract":"The <b>Candlelight Revolution</b> was the wave of peaceful mass protest that removed President Park Geun-hye from office between November 2016 and March 2017.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began with revelations that the president had allowed a private confidante holding no official position to see classified papers and to extract money from the conglomerates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Protesters gathered week after week in central Seoul carrying candles, in what became the largest sustained demonstrations in the country since democratisation, with cumulative participation usually put at more than 17 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Not one incident of physical violence was recorded across those four months, which has become central to how the episode is remembered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The National Assembly voted to impeach Park in December 2016, the Constitutional Court unanimously upheld that impeachment on 10 March 2017, and she was later tried, convicted and imprisoned before being pardoned. <br><br> The protests drew directly on the grief and anger left by the Sewol ferry disaster, and the yellow ribbons of the bereaved families were carried through the crowds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Interpretations of what the whole episode actually proved about South Korean democracy differ from one another in instructive ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some read it as evidence that Korean democracy had frayed badly, since a president had privatised public power and the institutions meant to check her noticed very late.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Others emphasise that the same episode showed a mature democracy at work, removing a leader through constitutional means under overwhelming but entirely peaceful public pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A third reading places the whole episode within an older Korean tradition of righteous remonstrance, in which the people call a ruler back to the proper name of the office rather than seeking to overthrow the office itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Jamie Doucette, “The Occult of Personality: Korea's Candlelight Protests and the Impeachment of Park Geun-hye,” <i>The Journal of Asian Studies</i> 76, no. 4 (2017): 851–860, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0021911817000821. [Open access]","Nan Kim, “Candlelight and the Yellow Ribbon: Catalyzing Re-Democratization in South Korea,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 15, no. 17 (2017), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466017013717. [Open access]","Sungmoon Kim, “Candlelight for Our Country's Right Name: A Confucian Interpretation of South Korea's Candlelight Revolution,” <i>Religions</i> 9, no. 11 (2018): 330, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel9110330. [Open access]","Hyejin Kim, “‘Spoon Theory’ and the Fall of a Populist Princess in Seoul,” <i>The Journal of Asian Studies</i> 76, no. 4 (2017): 839–849, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0021911817000778. [Open access]","Paul Y. Chang, “Candlelight Protests in South Korea: The Legacies of Authoritarianism and Democratization,” <i>Ewha Journal of Social Sciences</i> 34, no. 1 (2018): 5–18, https://doi.org/10.16935/ejss.2018.34.1.001. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the absence of violence matter so much to how this is remembered?","a":"Because it denied the government the justification it needed. A protest that turns violent can be answered with force and dismissed as disorder, so millions gathering peacefully left the authorities with no answer but to let the constitutional process run."},{"q":"Why can the same events show democracy both frayed and mature?","a":"Because they measure different things. That a president could privatise public power for years shows the checks failing; that she was removed lawfully under public pressure and without violence shows them eventually working."},{"q":"Why did a ferry disaster feed a protest movement two years later?","a":"Because the bereaved families had spent those years being dismissed, and their yellow ribbons had already become a symbol of a government that protected itself rather than its citizens. The corruption scandal confirmed what they had been alleging."}]},
    "pea-062": {"abstract":"The <b>chaebol</b> are the large family-controlled conglomerates that dominate the South Korean economy, among them Samsung, Hyundai and LG.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They grew out of the state-led industrialisation of the postwar decades, when governments directed credit, import licences and scarce foreign exchange towards a handful of firms chosen to lead particular industries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That origin explains the relationship that followed, since the groups depend on government for favourable legislation, subsidies, cheap loans and lenient tax treatment, while politicians depend on them for funding and for the growth by which they are judged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Family control is typically maintained through circular shareholdings among affiliates rather than through outright majority ownership, so a modest direct stake can command the whole structure and pass down a generation intact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The financial crisis of 1997 exposed how much debt the model had accumulated, and the collapse of Daewoo, then the second largest group in the country, became its emblem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Corruption scandals have recurred so regularly that they have tarnished the reputation of business and politics together, and several chairmen have been convicted, sentenced and then pardoned on the argument that the national economy needed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Resentment has grown for a further reason, since the groups employ only around a tenth of Korean workers while smaller and medium-sized firms employ the great majority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are now widely seen as contributing to inequality rather than to shared prosperity, which reverses how they were regarded during the growth decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Successive progressive governments have promised economic democratisation, and each has found itself dependent on the same groups for investment and employment before its term was out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The result is a pattern in which reform is announced, partially implemented and quietly abandoned, which is why chaebol power has survived every change of government since democratisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Sang-young Rhyu, “The Origins of Korean Chaebols and their Roots in the Korean War,” <i>Korean Journal of International Relations</i> 45, no. 5 (2005): 203–230, https://doi.org/10.14731/kjis.2005.12.45.5.203. [Open access]","Kyung-Pil Kim, “The Neoliberal Strategy of South Korean Conglomerates,” <i>World Review of Political Economy</i> 13, no. 1 (2022), https://doi.org/10.13169/worlrevipoliecon.13.1.0097. [Open access]","Jamie Doucette, “Debating Economic Democracy in South Korea: The Costs of Commensurability,” <i>Critical Asian Studies</i> 47, no. 3 (2015): 388–413, https://doi.org/10.1080/14672715.2015.1057025. [Open access]","Joongi Kim, “A Forensic Study of Daewoo's Corporate Governance: Does Responsibility for the Meltdown Solely Lie with the Chaebol and Korea?,” <i>Northwestern Journal of International Law and Business</i> 28, no. 2 (2008): 273, https://scholarlycommons.law.northwestern.edu/njilb/vol28/iss2/12. [Open access]","Jongwoo Han and L. H. M. Ling, “Authoritarianism in the Hypermasculinized State: Hybridity, Patriarchy, and Capitalism in Korea,” <i>International Studies Quarterly</i> 42, no. 1 (1998): 53–78, https://doi.org/10.1111/0020-8833.00069. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a family control a huge group without owning most of it?","a":"Because affiliates hold shares in one another in a circle, so a modest direct stake commands the votes of the whole chain. Control travels through the structure rather than through the size of the holding."},{"q":"Why does every reforming government end up dependent on them?","a":"Because they supply the investment, exports and jobs the government will be judged on. A president who genuinely weakened them would be blamed for the slowdown that followed, well before any benefit appeared."},{"q":"Why did they stop being seen as the engine of prosperity?","a":"Because growth stopped being widely shared. When the groups employ about a tenth of workers while dominating profits and political access, they look less like the source of a national miracle and more like the reason it stalled for everyone else."}]},
    "pea-063": {"abstract":"The <b>Gwangju Uprising</b> was the ten-day revolt in the south-western city of Gwangju in May 1980 that became the founding event of the South Korean democracy movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It followed the seizure of power by General Chun Doo-hwan after the assassination of Park Chung-hee, and began when the new regime extended martial law across the country, closed the universities and arrested opposition politicians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Special forces units sent to suppress a student demonstration attacked bystanders as well as protesters with such severity that the whole city turned against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Residents broke into police and reserve armouries, armed themselves, and drove the troops out, then governed the city through citizens' committees for roughly a week before the army returned in force and retook it on 27 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Official figures put the dead at around two hundred, while local and bereaved-family estimates have always been considerably higher, and the exact toll remains disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What made the episode politically decisive was the account the regime then insisted upon, which was that Gwangju had been a rebellion stirred up by communists and by the opposition leader Kim Dae-jung.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because the press was censored and that version was taught as fact, recovering what had actually happened became itself the central work of the opposition through the 1980s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Photographs and testimony circulated underground, and each new student cohort was radicalised by learning that the state had lied about killing its own citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After democratisation the verdict was formally reversed, a national cemetery and memorial day were established, and Chun and his successor were tried and convicted in 1996 for mutiny and for the deaths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The episode also hardened the regional grievance of the Honam provinces, which remains visible in how Korea votes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Soon Seok Park, “Remembering Gwangju: Memory Work in the South Korean Democracy Movement, 1980–1987,” <i>Research in Social Movements, Conflicts and Change</i> 45 (2021), https://doi.org/10.1108/s0163-786x20210000045011. [Open access]","Thomas Kern, “Cultural Performance and Political Regime Change,” <i>Sociological Theory</i> 27, no. 3 (2009): 291–316, https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9558.2009.01349.x. [Open access]","Dong-Choon Kim, “The Social Grounds of Anticommunism in South Korea: Crisis of the Ruling Class and Anticommunist Reaction,” <i>Asian Journal of German and European Studies</i> 2, no. 1 (2017), https://doi.org/10.1186/s40856-017-0018-1. [Open access]","Aurel Croissant, David Kuehn, and Tanja Eschenauer, “Mass Protests and the Military,” <i>Journal of Democracy</i> 29, no. 3 (2018): 141–155, https://doi.org/10.1353/jod.2018.0051. [Open access]","Nan Kim, “The Color of Dissent and a Vital Politics of Fragility in South Korea,” <i>The Journal of Asian Studies</i> 77, no. 4 (2018): 971–990, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0021911818000980. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did an ordinary protest escalate into an armed revolt?","a":"Because the troops attacked people who were not protesting. Once bystanders were being beaten and shot, the dispute stopped being about the students and became about whether the city would defend itself."},{"q":"Why was remembering it treated as political work rather than history?","a":"Because the official version was a lie the regime needed. Every photograph that circulated and every testimony passed on was a direct contradiction of the state's account, which made accurate memory an act of opposition."},{"q":"Why does one city in 1980 still shape how Korea votes?","a":"Because the violence fell on one region and the regime that ordered it drew its leadership from another. Grievance of that kind attaches to place, and the parties that formed afterwards were built on top of it."}]},
    "pea-064": {"abstract":"<b>Park Chung-hee</b> took power in a military coup in May 1961 and governed South Korea until he was shot dead by his own intelligence chief in October 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is the central figure of the country's transformation from one of the poorest states in the world into an industrial economy, achieved through five-year plans, export targets set for individual firms and state-directed credit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 1973 the strategy shifted deliberately towards heavy and chemical industry, which is why steel, shipbuilding, chemicals and later electronics rather than light manufacturing became the country's specialisms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He normalised relations with Japan in 1965 against furious domestic opposition, taking a settlement package that helped fund the industrial drive while leaving the colonial-era grievances formally closed and politically alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He also sent Korean troops to Vietnam, which bought American money and security guarantees at a moment when both were in question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> His rule grew harsher as it went on, culminating in the Yushin Constitution of 1972, which ended direct presidential elections, removed any limit on his terms, gave him the power to appoint a third of the legislature and allowed rule by emergency decree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Labour organisation was suppressed as a matter of industrial policy, since low and controlled wages were part of what made the export drive work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dissidents were imprisoned and in some cases executed, and the intelligence service operated with very few constraints.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He remains the most divisive figure in modern Korean politics, remembered by conservatives as the architect of national prosperity and by the democratic movement as the man who built that prosperity on repression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His daughter Park Geun-hye was elected president in 2012, which made that argument a live electoral question rather than a historical one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Seungsook Moon, “The Cultural Politics of Remembering Park Chung Hee,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 7, no. 19 (2009), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466009023894. [Open access]","Minji Jeong and Youseop Shin, “Post-War Korean Conservatism, Japanese Statism, and the Legacy of President Park Chung-hee in South Korea,” <i>The Korean Journal of International Studies</i> 16, no. 1 (2018): 57–76, https://doi.org/10.14731/kjis.2018.04.16.1.57. [Open access]","Frederic C. Deyo, Stephan Haggard, and Hagen Koo, “Labor in the Political Economy of East Asian Industrialization,” <i>Bulletin of Concerned Asian Scholars</i> 19, no. 2 (1987): 42–53, https://doi.org/10.1080/14672715.1987.10409890. [Open access]","Ito Peng, “Postindustrial Pressures, Political Regime Shifts, and Social Policy Reform in Japan and South Korea,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 4, no. 3 (2004): 389–425, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800006020. [Open access]","Jamie Doucette, “The Postdevelopmental State,” <i>University of Michigan Press eBooks</i> (2024), https://doi.org/10.3998/mpub.12875326. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the state pick industries rather than leave it to the market?","a":"Because the capital needed for steel or shipbuilding was far beyond what any Korean firm could raise, and the returns were too distant for a lender to accept. Only the state could direct credit on that scale and absorb the risk of being wrong."},{"q":"Why was suppressing unions part of the economic strategy rather than separate from it?","a":"Because the exports competed on price. Wages rising with productivity would have eaten the advantage, so keeping labour unorganised was not incidental to the model but one of its inputs."},{"q":"Why is the argument about him still electorally live?","a":"Because both claims about him are true at once, and Koreans differ on which one settles the matter. His daughter's presidency turned that disagreement into a choice on a ballot paper rather than a debate among historians."}]},
    "pea-065": {"abstract":"<b>Park Chung-hee Syndrome</b> is the name Korean commentators give to the recurring popular nostalgia for the dictator who ruled the country from 1961 to 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its clearest measure is that opinion polls have repeatedly named him the best president in the country's history, decades after a democratic movement fought to end the system he built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The nostalgia is strongest among older voters who lived through the growth years, but it is not confined to them, and it rises reliably whenever the economy disappoints.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The financial crisis of 1997 produced the sharpest surge, because a generation that had been told hardship bought prosperity watched the prosperity disappear under an elected government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What is remembered is decisiveness, personal frugality and a state that visibly delivered roads, factories and rising wages, rather than the emergency decrees, the torture cells and the executions of political opponents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The phenomenon matters in Korean politics because it functions as an organising resource rather than as a private sentiment held by individual voters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Korean conservatism has drawn much of its identity from the claim to be the party of competence and national strength, and the memory of his rule supplies the evidence for that claim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It also helps explain why corruption allegations damage conservative and progressive politicians unevenly, since voters who already attribute past prosperity to strong leadership discount misconduct by leaders they read that way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The clearest demonstration came in 2012, when his daughter Park Geun-hye won the presidency on a campaign that invoked her father's era directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Her impeachment five years later damaged that inheritance badly and split the conservative party, though the underlying nostalgia has proved considerably more durable than the political vehicle that carried it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Seungsook Moon, “The Cultural Politics of Remembering Park Chung Hee,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 7, no. 19 (2009), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466009023894. [Open access]","Namhee Lee, “Memory Construction and the Politics of Time in Neoliberal South Korea,” <i>Duke University Press</i> (2022), https://doi.org/10.1215/9781478023616. [Open access]","Sanghoon Kim-Leffingwell, “Authoritarian Legacies and Partisan Bias in Corruption Voting,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 23, no. 2 (2023): 241–262, https://doi.org/10.1017/jea.2023.5. [Open access]","Jamie Doucette, “The Occult of Personality: Korea's Candlelight Protests and the Impeachment of Park Geun-hye,” <i>The Journal of Asian Studies</i> 76, no. 4 (2017): 851–860, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0021911817000821. [Open access]","Minji Jeong and Youseop Shin, “Post-War Korean Conservatism, Japanese Statism, and the Legacy of President Park Chung-hee in South Korea,” <i>The Korean Journal of International Studies</i> 16, no. 1 (2018): 57–76, https://doi.org/10.14731/kjis.2018.04.16.1.57. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does nostalgia for a dictatorship rise when the economy falters?","a":"Because the bargain his rule offered was hardship now for prosperity later. When prosperity stops arriving under an elected government, the comparison people reach for is the period when the bargain seemed to be honoured."},{"q":"Why is the repression so much less remembered than the growth?","a":"Because the growth touched everyone and the repression touched a minority directly. Factories and roads were visible to the whole country, while the emergency decrees fell on activists, journalists and unionists."},{"q":"Why would this make corruption allegations land unevenly?","a":"Because voters interpret misconduct through what they already believe a politician is for. Someone read as a strong leader delivering results has their misconduct filed as a cost of getting things done, which is not available to a leader without that reputation."}]},
    "pea-066": {"abstract":"<b>Chojoongdong</b> is the collective name for the three conservative newspapers that dominate the South Korean press, the <i>Chosun Ilbo</i>, the <i>JoongAng Ilbo</i> and the <i>Dong-A Ilbo</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word is a compound of their first syllables and was coined by critics on the left, so using it at all signals a view about the power those papers hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Between them they have long accounted for the large majority of paid newspaper circulation in the country, which gives them an agenda-setting position with no real counterpart in most democracies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two of the three date from the colonial period and all three prospered under the authoritarian governments, when the press operated under censorship and the surviving papers were those that accommodated it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That history is why the complaint against them is not simply about bias but about a conservative common sense that shapes what counts as a reasonable political position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The accusation runs that they frame progressive policy, and engagement with North Korea in particular, as naive or dangerous before an argument has been had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their influence grew further after a 2009 media law allowed newspaper companies to own broadcasters, and in 2011 each launched a general-programming cable channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The internet is the main reason the picture is no longer one-sided, since online outlets and citizen journalism gave progressive opinion a distribution network that did not depend on print.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The irony of the broadcast expansion is that one of those new channels, JTBC, broke the tablet computer story that destroyed the conservative president Park Geun-hye in 2016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ownership and editorial line, that episode showed, do not determine what a newsroom will do with a genuine story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Eun Suk Sa, “Development of Press Freedom in South Korea since Japanese Colonial Rule,” <i>Asian Culture and History</i> 1, no. 2 (2009): 3–17, https://doi.org/10.5539/ach.v1n2p3. [Open access]","Eun Suk Sa, “Factors Influencing Freedom of the Press in South Korea: A Survey of Print Journalists' Opinions,” <i>Asian Social Science</i> 5, no. 3 (2009): 3–24, https://doi.org/10.5539/ass.v5n3p3. [Open access]","Joan Cho, Jae Seung Lee, and B. K. Song, “Media Exposure and Regime Support under Competitive Authoritarianism: Evidence from South Korea,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 17, no. 2 (2017): 145–166, https://doi.org/10.1017/jea.2016.41. [Open access]","Ronda Hauben, “Online Grassroots Journalism and Participatory Democracy in South Korea,” <i>Korea Yearbook</i> (2007), https://doi.org/10.1163/ej.9789004164406.i-306.26. [Open access]","Dong-Choon Kim, “The Social Grounds of Anticommunism in South Korea: Crisis of the Ruling Class and Anticommunist Reaction,” <i>Asian Journal of German and European Studies</i> 2, no. 1 (2017), https://doi.org/10.1186/s40856-017-0018-1. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does using the word itself take a side?","a":"Because it was invented to name a problem. Treating three separately owned papers as one actor is already the claim that they function as a single conservative bloc, which is exactly what their defenders deny."},{"q":"Why did surviving the dictatorship leave a mark on these papers?","a":"Because a censored press selects for accommodation. Papers that fought the regime were shut or had their journalists purged, so the ones that reached democratisation large and profitable had reached an understanding with power."},{"q":"Why did the internet change the balance when new newspapers could not?","a":"Because the barrier was distribution, not opinion. Printing presses, delivery networks and advertising relationships take decades and capital to build, while an online outlet needs none of them to reach a national audience."}]},
    "pea-067": {"abstract":"<b>Regionalism</b> is the deepest cleavage in South Korean electoral politics, dividing the south-western Honam provinces from the south-eastern Yeongnam provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Honam, centred on Gwangju and the two Jeolla provinces, votes overwhelmingly for the progressive party, while Yeongnam, centred on Busan and Daegu, votes as reliably for the conservative one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The margins involved are extreme by comparative standards, with single constituencies regularly returning a party with more than eighty percent of the vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pattern is not ancient, but grew out of the authoritarian decades, when presidents drawn from the south-east directed industrial investment towards their own region and left the south-west comparatively undeveloped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The killings at Gwangju in 1980 then gave that economic grievance a far more serious grounding, and the opposition leader Kim Dae-jung, himself from Honam, became its political vehicle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The cleavage matters most for what it compensates for, which is the striking organisational weakness of Korean parties themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They split, merge and rename themselves so often that few voters can trace a continuous organisation across two decades, and party identification in the European sense is correspondingly weak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Regional loyalty is the one attachment that survives those reorganisations intact, which makes it the structure holding the whole system together rather than a distortion of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also concentrates power, since a leader who controls a regional bloc arrives at any coalition negotiation with seats already secured, and this was the basis of the three regional bosses who dominated politics through the 1990s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The hold of region has weakened somewhat among younger and metropolitan voters, for whom inequality, housing and gender have become sharper dividing lines, though it still shapes the map at every general election.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Olli Hellmann, “Party System Institutionalization Without Parties: Evidence from Korea,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2014): 53–84, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800009589. [Open access]","Youngmi Kim, “The Politics of Coalition in Korea,” <i>Routledge</i> (2011), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203821138. [Open access]","Hyun-Chool Lee and Alexandre Repkine, “A Spatial Analysis of the Voting Patterns in the South Korean General Elections of 2016,” <i>Social Sciences</i> 11, no. 9 (2022): 389, https://doi.org/10.3390/socsci11090389. [Open access]","Jörg Michael Dostal, “South Korea: The Lasting Pitfalls of the ‘Imperial Presidency’,” <i>The Political Quarterly</i> 94, no. 1 (2023): 57–68, https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-923x.13232. [Open access]","Seungwoo Han, “Between the Ladder and the Mirror: Subjective Class Consciousness and Voting under Inequality in South Korea,” <i>Japanese Journal of Political Science</i> 27, no. 1 (2026): 79–98, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1468109925100224. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did regional loyalty become the system's backbone rather than party loyalty?","a":"Because there was nothing else stable to attach to. Parties dissolved and reformed under new names every few years, so a voter who wanted a durable political identity could only find one in place and in the leaders a place produced."},{"q":"Why are the margins so much larger than regional differences elsewhere?","a":"Because the division rests on a grievance about violence and neglect, not on differing interests. Where the disagreement is about whether the state killed your neighbours, the losing party has nothing to offer that would move a tenth of the vote."},{"q":"Why does controlling a region translate into national power?","a":"Because it converts into seats without campaigning. A leader who can deliver a bloc of constituencies before negotiations begin is indispensable to any coalition, which is how three regional figures dominated a country of many more politicians."}]},
    "pea-068": {"abstract":"The <b>National Security Law</b> is the South Korean statute, enacted in December 1948, that criminalises activity judged to benefit an anti-state organisation, which in practice means North Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was passed within months of the republic's founding, in a period of insurgency and imminent war, and it drew on the colonial-era security legislation the Japanese had used against Korean nationalists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its central provision punishes praising, encouraging or propagating the positions of such an organisation, which makes expression rather than conduct the offence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under the dictatorships it was the principal instrument for imprisoning opposition politicians, journalists, students and trade unionists, since almost any criticism of the government could be presented as serving the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Confessions were extracted under torture in the worst cases, and a number of those convicted on such evidence were executed years before posthumous retrials cleared their names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What makes the law remarkable is that democratisation, which rewrote almost every other part of the constitutional order, did not remove it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It remains in force, prosecutions continue at a low but real level, and the material that draws them now includes North Korean websites and songs shared online.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Constitutional Court has narrowed its application, requiring a clear danger to national security rather than mere sympathy, but has repeatedly declined to strike it down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> United Nations human rights bodies and international monitoring groups have criticised it for years as incompatible with freedom of expression in a democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every progressive administration has proposed repeal and every one has retreated, because conservatives can frame the attempt as sympathy for the regime across the border, which is an accusation Korean politics still takes seriously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["György Széll, “Anticommunism in Korea and Germany in Times of Cold War,” <i>Asian Journal of German and European Studies</i> 2, no. 1 (2017), https://doi.org/10.1186/s40856-017-0014-5. [Open access]","Meredith Shaw, “Godzilla vs Pulgasari: Anti-Japanism and Anti-Communism as Dueling Antagonisms in South Korean Politics,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 22, no. 2 (2022): 201–230, https://doi.org/10.1017/jea.2022.2. [Open access]","Timothy Gitzen, “Banal Security: Queer Korea in the Time of Viruses,” <i>Helsinki University Press</i> (2023), https://doi.org/10.33134/ahead-3. [Open access]","Scott L. Cummings, “The Internationalization of Public Interest Law,” <i>Duke Law Journal</i> 57, no. 4 (2006): 891–1036, https://scholarship.law.duke.edu/dlj/vol57/iss4/2. [Open access]","Dong-Choon Kim, “The Social Grounds of Anticommunism in South Korea: Crisis of the Ruling Class and Anticommunist Reaction,” <i>Asian Journal of German and European Studies</i> 2, no. 1 (2017), https://doi.org/10.1186/s40856-017-0018-1. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a law written for wartime survive in a consolidated democracy?","a":"Because the state it was written against still exists and is still armed. That makes repeal arguable as a security decision rather than a civil liberties one, and no government wants to own the risk."},{"q":"Why is punishing expression rather than conduct the core problem?","a":"Because the prosecutor never has to show that anything was done. Sympathy, a book, a shared link or an argument can each be read as benefiting the other side, which leaves the boundary of the offence wherever the state chooses to put it."},{"q":"Why did the courts narrow it instead of striking it down?","a":"Because narrowing lets a court fix the abuse without taking the security judgement away from the legislature. Requiring a demonstrable danger removed most casual prosecutions while leaving the statute for the government to repeal if it wished."}]},
    "pea-069": {"abstract":"<b>Minjung</b> is the Korean word for the common people, and it names the intellectual and political movement that drove the struggle against dictatorship from the 1970s until democratisation in 1987.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its central claim was that the ordinary people, rather than rulers, generals or great men, are the real subject of Korean history, and that the suffering they had accumulated under colonialism, war and industrialisation gave them the right to speak for the nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The movement drew together university students, factory workers, farmers, writers and dissident Christian clergy, an alliance that would have been unlikely in most other countries at the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its immediate origin is usually traced to the self-immolation of the young garment worker Chun Tae-il in 1970, who burned himself to death demanding that the labour laws already on the books be enforced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Students responded by taking factory jobs under false identities in order to organise the workers they had read about, which became a defining practice of the movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It was as much a cultural project as a political one, reviving mask dances, peasant drumming and oral storytelling as forms that belonged to the people rather than to the elite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Protest songs carried its arguments furthest, and <i>March for the Beloved</i>, written for two victims of the Gwangju killings, is still sung at Korean demonstrations today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A distinctively Korean liberation theology grew alongside it, placing God among the suffering and giving church networks a reason to shelter activists the state was hunting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The movement dissolved as a coherent force after 1987, when its objective was achieved and the collapse of the socialist bloc discredited part of its analysis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its veterans nonetheless staffed the progressive parties, the unions and the civic organisations that have shaped Korean politics ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Eungi Kim, “Minjung Theology in Contemporary Korea: Liberation Theology and a Reconsideration of Secularization Theory,” <i>Religions</i> 9, no. 12 (2018): 415, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel9120415. [Open access]","Jim Glassman, Bae-Gyoon Park, and Young-Jin Choi, “Failed Internationalism and Social Movement Decline,” <i>Critical Asian Studies</i> 40, no. 3 (2008): 339–372, https://doi.org/10.1080/14672710802274110. [Open access]","Thomas Kern and Sang-hui Nam, “The Making of a Social Movement,” <i>Current Sociology</i> 57, no. 5 (2009): 637–660, https://doi.org/10.1177/0011392109337649. [Open access]","Frederic C. Deyo, Stephan Haggard, and Hagen Koo, “Labor in the Political Economy of East Asian Industrialization,” <i>Bulletin of Concerned Asian Scholars</i> 19, no. 2 (1987): 42–53, https://doi.org/10.1080/14672715.1987.10409890. [Open access]","Jung-Min Mina Lee, “Minjung Kayo: Imagining Democracy through Song in South Korea,” <i>Twentieth-Century Music</i> 20, no. 1 (2023): 49–69, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1478572222000470. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did students go to work in factories?","a":"Because the workers were where the movement's theory said history was being made, and because organising was illegal. A student could not reach a factory floor as a visitor, so reaching it meant becoming a worker."},{"q":"Why were churches so involved in an opposition movement?","a":"Because they had protection the activists did not. A regime that imprisoned students was more cautious about raiding a church with foreign connections, which made congregations useful as meeting places and as witnesses."},{"q":"Why did the movement fade once democracy arrived?","a":"Because it was held together by a single enemy. With the dictatorship gone, workers, students and churches wanted different things, and the ordinary disagreements of democratic politics had no common target to suppress them."}]},
    "pea-070": {"abstract":"The South Korean <b>presidency</b> is the dominant institution of the country's politics, held for a single non-renewable term of five years under the constitution agreed in 1987.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The single term was the democratic movement's central demand, because every previous strongman had extended his own tenure by amending or replacing the constitution, and one term removes the temptation at its source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The office that remained is nonetheless extraordinarily powerful, controlling senior appointments across the government, the prosecution service and the state-linked corporations, along with the budget and the direction of foreign policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Korean political scientists commonly call it an imperial presidency, meaning that the 1987 settlement constrained how long a president may rule without much constraining what a president may do while ruling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The prime minister exists but is appointed rather than elected and carries nothing like the authority of the head of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The single term also has a cost that is now widely discussed, since a president becomes a lame duck from roughly the third year, when the party begins positioning for the succession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Presidential and legislative elections run on different cycles, so a president frequently faces a National Assembly controlled by the opposition and can govern only by decree, veto and appointment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The resulting deadlock is a standing feature of Korean government rather than an occasional accident of the calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The most striking pattern is what happens to the holders, since nearly every president of the democratic era has left office to face criminal investigation, and several have been imprisoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether that record shows a justice system that finally works or an office that invites abuse is one of the genuine open arguments in Korean politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Jörg Michael Dostal, “South Korea: The Lasting Pitfalls of the ‘Imperial Presidency’,” <i>The Political Quarterly</i> 94, no. 1 (2023): 57–68, https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-923x.13232. [Open access]","Youngmi Kim, “The Politics of Coalition in Korea,” <i>Routledge</i> (2011), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203821138. [Open access]","Tom Ginsburg, James Melton, and Zachary Elkins, “On the Evasion of Executive Term Limits,” <i>William and Mary Law Review</i> 52, no. 6 (2011): 1807–1872, https://doi.org/10.26153/tsw/2241. [Open access]","Olli Hellmann, “Party System Institutionalization Without Parties: Evidence from Korea,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2014): 53–84, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800009589. [Open access]","Jin Wook Kim, “Korean Constitutional Court and Constitutionalism in Political Dynamics: Focusing on Presidential Impeachment,” <i>Constitutional Review</i> 4, no. 2 (2018): 222–254, https://doi.org/10.31078/consrev423. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a single term the movement's central demand?","a":"Because every dictator had stayed by rewriting the rules on his own tenure. A term that cannot be renewed removes the thing worth rewriting the constitution for, which no other safeguard had managed to prevent."},{"q":"Why does a president become powerless before the term ends?","a":"Because everyone knows the date of departure. Once the party must choose a successor, its members owe their future to the candidate rather than to the incumbent, and a leader with nothing left to give is not obeyed."},{"q":"Why do the president and the assembly so often belong to different parties?","a":"Because the two elections run on separate cycles of different lengths. Voters are choosing at different moments with different moods, and nothing in the system pushes the two results into alignment."}]},
    "pea-071": {"abstract":"The <b>Constitutional Court</b> of South Korea was created by the 1987 constitution and began work in 1988, and it has become one of the most consequential institutions in the country's politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has nine justices serving six-year terms, three chosen by the president, three elected by the National Assembly and three nominated by the chief justice of the Supreme Court, an arrangement designed so that no single actor controls the bench.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Six votes are required to strike down a statute, dissolve a political party or remove a president, a supermajority that forces genuine agreement across justices appointed by different hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Earlier Korean constitutions had provided for judicial review on paper, but the bodies responsible almost never used it, so an effective court was one of the real innovations of democratisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ordinary citizens may petition it directly when a public authority has violated their rights, which is why it hears far more cases than comparable courts elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Its most visible power is over impeachment, since an assembly vote only suspends a president and the court decides whether the removal stands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reinstated Roh Moo-hyun in 2004, judging his election-law breach too minor to justify removal, and it removed Park Geun-hye unanimously in March 2017.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has also ruled on questions most courts never touch, blocking the transfer of the capital to Sejong in 2004 on the reasoning that the status of Seoul was a matter of customary constitutional law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 2014 it dissolved a left-wing party for violating the basic democratic order, the first such dissolution in Korean history and a decision still argued about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The criticism most often made of it is that its composition mirrors the political balance too closely, so that its authority depends on justices resisting the expectations of those who appointed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Jin Wook Kim, “Korean Constitutional Court and Constitutionalism in Political Dynamics: Focusing on Presidential Impeachment,” <i>Constitutional Review</i> 4, no. 2 (2018): 222–254, https://doi.org/10.31078/consrev423. [Open access]","J.-r. Yeh, “Presidential Politics and the Judicial Facilitation of Dialogue between Political Actors in New Asian Democracies: Comparing the South Korean and Taiwanese Experiences,” <i>International Journal of Constitutional Law</i> 8, no. 4 (2010): 911–949, https://doi.org/10.1093/icon/mor006. [Open access]","Sang-Hyeon Jeon, “A Critical Review on Composition of the Constitutional Court of Korea,” <i>Constitutional Review</i> 10, no. 1 (2024): 1–32, https://doi.org/10.31078/consrev1011. [Open access]","Tom Ginsburg and Zachary Elkins, “Ancillary Powers of Constitutional Courts,” <i>Texas Law Review</i> 87, no. 7 (2009): 1431–1461, https://doi.org/10.26153/tsw/2245. [Open access]","Hannes B. Mosler, “Judicialization of Politics and the Korean Constitutional Court: The Party Chapter Abolition Case,” <i>Verfassung in Recht und Übersee</i> 47, no. 3 (2014): 293–318, https://doi.org/10.5771/0506-7286-2014-3-293. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why split the appointments three ways?","a":"Because a court that one office staffs is that office's court. Dividing the nine seats between the president, the assembly and the judiciary means no single political force can assemble a bench in its own image."},{"q":"Why require six votes rather than a simple majority?","a":"Because the decisions are irreversible in practice. Removing an elected president or dissolving a party on five votes to four would leave half the country reading the outcome as a factional win rather than a legal judgement."},{"q":"Why did the same court reinstate one president and remove another?","a":"Because it applied a threshold rather than a preference. A breach of election law was judged too small to overturn an election, while running state power through an unaccountable private person was not."}]},
    "pea-072": {"abstract":"The <b>Sunshine Policy</b> was the strategy of engagement with North Korea adopted by President Kim Dae-jung in 1998 and continued by his successor Roh Moo-hyun until 2008.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name comes from the fable in which the sun persuades a traveller to remove his coat after the wind has failed to tear it off, and the analogy carried the whole argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It rested on three commitments, which were that armed provocation would not be tolerated, that the South had no intention of absorbing the North, and that cooperation should proceed regardless of political disputes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The most important of these was the separation of economics from politics, which meant that trade and aid were meant to continue through crises rather than being suspended as punishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its high point was the first summit between the two Koreas, held in Pyongyang in June 2000, for which Kim Dae-jung received the Nobel Peace Prize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The policy produced results that could be seen and visited, including reunions for families separated since the war, a tourist resort at Mount Kumgang and a joint industrial park at Kaesong where Southern firms employed Northern workers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kaesong was the most ambitious of these, running from 2004 until it was closed in 2016, and it remains the only sustained example of the two economies operating together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The criticism was constant and serious, since the North conducted its first nuclear test in 2006 and conservatives concluded that Southern money had helped pay for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It later emerged that a large payment had been channelled to Pyongyang shortly before the summit, which damaged the policy's moral standing considerably.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Engagement and pressure have alternated with each change of government ever since, and unification has become an increasingly abstract prospect for younger Koreans who feel little connection to the North.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Jin Hee Park, “Discourse Construction of Inter-Korean Summits in South Korean Newspapers: A Diachronic Study,” <i>Language & Communication</i> 78 (2021): 19–34, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.langcom.2021.02.001. [Open access]","Ralph Wrobel, “Ten Years of Kaesong Industrial Complex: A Brief History of the Last Economic Cooperation Project of the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Ordnungspolitische Diskurse</i> (2014), http://hdl.handle.net/10419/178853. [Open access]","Soon-Won Kang, “The Limit and Possibilities of Unification Education as Peace Education beyond Division in South Korea,” <i>Asian Journal of Peacebuilding</i> 6, no. 1 (2018): 133–156, https://doi.org/10.18588/201805.00a053. [Open access]","Miftah Farid Darussalam, “The Influence of Domestic Politics of South Korea on its Relations with North Korea and the United States,” <i>Jurnal Ilmu Sosial dan Ilmu Politik</i> 23, no. 3 (2020): 250–264, https://doi.org/10.22146/jsp.48652. [Open access]","Udo Merkel, “Sport, Politics and Reunification: A Comparative Analysis of Korea and Germany,” <i>The International Journal of the History of Sport</i> 26, no. 3 (2009): 406–428, https://doi.org/10.1080/09523360802602273. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why insist on separating economics from politics?","a":"Because suspending cooperation after every incident had never changed Northern behaviour and always ended the contact. Keeping the projects running through crises was the only way anything could accumulate long enough to matter."},{"q":"Why did the nuclear test damage the policy so badly?","a":"Because it answered the question the policy had been staked on. Engagement was supposed to make the North less dangerous over time, and a weapon test is the plainest possible evidence in the other direction."},{"q":"Why does unification interest younger Koreans less?","a":"Because they have no memory of a single country and can see the cost of joining a collapsed economy to theirs. For their grandparents the North held relatives, while for them it is a foreign state that happens to speak Korean."}]},
    "pea-073": {"abstract":"<b>Kim Dae-jung</b> was the leading figure of the Korean opposition for three decades and served as president from 1998 to 2003, having survived a kidnapping, a death sentence and years of house arrest and exile along the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He came from the south-western Honam region, which made him the political voice of an area that the authoritarian governments had neglected and that had suffered the killings at Gwangju.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He came close to defeating Park Chung-hee in the presidential election of 1971, after which the intelligence service seized him from a Tokyo hotel in 1973 and very nearly killed him at sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1980 the incoming Chun Doo-hwan regime blamed him for the Gwangju uprising and sentenced him to death, a sentence commuted only after sustained international pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His election in December 1997 was the first time in Korean history that power passed from a governing party to an opposition one through the ballot box.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He took office in the middle of the financial crisis, which forced him to implement the severe restructuring programme demanded by the International Monetary Fund.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The irony was considerable, as a lifelong champion of workers and the poor presided over mass redundancies and the breaking up of indebted conglomerates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He paired that with the country's first serious welfare state, establishing a guaranteed minimum livelihood and extending health and pension coverage, so that the social protections and the market reforms arrived together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He also invested heavily in broadband and information technology, a decision usually credited with making Korea one of the most connected societies in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He is best known internationally for the Sunshine Policy of engagement with North Korea and the Nobel Peace Prize he received for it in 2000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Jin Hee Park, “Discourse Construction of Inter-Korean Summits in South Korean Newspapers: A Diachronic Study,” <i>Language & Communication</i> 78 (2021): 19–34, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.langcom.2021.02.001. [Open access]","Jamie Doucette, “Debating Economic Democracy in South Korea: The Costs of Commensurability,” <i>Critical Asian Studies</i> 47, no. 3 (2015): 388–413, https://doi.org/10.1080/14672715.2015.1057025. [Open access]","Youngmi Kim, “The Politics of Coalition in Korea,” <i>Routledge</i> (2011), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203821138. [Open access]","Ito Peng, “Postindustrial Pressures, Political Regime Shifts, and Social Policy Reform in Japan and South Korea,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 4, no. 3 (2004): 389–425, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800006020. [Open access]","Soon Seok Park, “Remembering Gwangju: Memory Work in the South Korean Democracy Movement, 1980–1987,” <i>Research in Social Movements, Conflicts and Change</i> 45 (2021), https://doi.org/10.1108/s0163-786x20210000045011. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the regime blame him for an uprising he was already in custody for?","a":"Because a conspiracy is easier to answer than a city. Attributing Gwangju to a single opposition politician meant the killings could be framed as the suppression of a plot rather than as a response to ordinary citizens."},{"q":"Why did a champion of workers carry out mass redundancies?","a":"Because the alternative was national insolvency. The rescue money came with conditions attached, and a president who refused them in his first weeks would have had no economy left to protect anyone in."},{"q":"Why did welfare expand at the same moment as market reform?","a":"Because the reforms created the casualties the welfare system was built to catch. Once lifetime employment ended, something had to stand between a dismissed worker and destitution, and nothing had previously needed to."}]},
    "pea-074": {"abstract":"The <b>June Democratic Uprising</b> of 1987 ended military rule in South Korea and produced the constitution the country still uses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its immediate cause was the death under police torture of a Seoul National University student in January of that year, and the government's attempt to conceal how he had died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Anger hardened in April when President Chun Doo-hwan suspended all debate on constitutional reform, making clear that he intended to hand the presidency to a chosen successor through the existing indirect system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second student died in June after being struck by a tear gas grenade, and the photograph of him collapsing into a friend's arms became the image of the whole movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What made these protests different from every earlier round was that office workers in suits joined the students in the commercial districts, so the government faced the middle class it had claimed to govern for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On 29 June the ruling party's candidate, Roh Tae-woo, announced that direct presidential elections, an amnesty for Kim Dae-jung and restored press freedoms would all be granted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A new constitution was approved by referendum in October, and it remains in force today with its single five-year presidential term.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The transition was therefore a negotiated one rather than a collapse, which matters because the old governing elite survived it intact and entered democratic politics with its money, its networks and its newspapers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That character was confirmed in December, when Kim Young-sam and Kim Dae-jung both insisted on standing and split the opposition vote, handing the presidency to Roh with only a little over a third of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many Koreans regard this as the founding disappointment of their democracy, since the protests won the rules while the men they had protested against won the first election held under them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Grażyna Strnad, “The Sixth Republic under Roh Tae Woo: The Genesis of South Korean Democracy,” <i>Polish Political Science Yearbook</i> 39 (2010): 204–225, https://doi.org/10.15804/ppsy2010011. [Open access]","Youngmi Kim, “The Politics of Coalition in Korea,” <i>Routledge</i> (2011), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203821138. [Open access]","Aurel Croissant, David Kuehn, and Tanja Eschenauer, “Mass Protests and the Military,” <i>Journal of Democracy</i> 29, no. 3 (2018): 141–155, https://doi.org/10.1353/jod.2018.0051. [Open access]","Thomas Kern and Sang-hui Nam, “The Making of a Social Movement,” <i>Current Sociology</i> 57, no. 5 (2009): 637–660, https://doi.org/10.1177/0011392109337649. [Open access]","Stephen Zunes, “People-Powered and Non-Violent Social Movements: Forcing Gradualist Democratic Reforms in Authoritarian Societies,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 3 (2022), https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2021.721055. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did office workers joining change the government's options?","a":"Because students could be dismissed as radicals and dispersed with force, while salaried professionals could not. Shooting at the middle class would have destroyed the regime's claim to be the guardian of the prosperity it had built."},{"q":"Why did the regime concede rather than declare martial law?","a":"Because the costs had risen too far. The Olympics were a year away, the Americans were pressing against another Gwangju, and the protests were too broad for the army to suppress without killing people the country would not forgive."},{"q":"Why did the opposition lose an election it had just won the right to hold?","a":"Because both of its leaders believed the presidency was theirs, and neither would stand aside. With one ruling-party candidate against two opposition ones, a third of the vote was enough to win under the rules they had just secured."}]},
    "pea-075": {"abstract":"<b>Christianity</b> is professed by roughly three in ten South Koreans, split between a large Protestant majority and a smaller Catholic minority, which makes the country unlike any of its neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It arrived in an unusual way, beginning in the late 18th century when Korean scholars encountered Catholic texts in China and organised congregations before any missionary had reached them, a start followed by severe persecution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Protestant missionaries arrived from 1884 and concentrated on schools, hospitals and translation into the Korean alphabet, so conversion came bound up with modern education and with literacy in the vernacular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decisive turn came under Japanese colonial rule, when churches became centres of nationalist organisation and Christians were prominent among those who signed the 1919 declaration of independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That association is the main reason the religion was received as a vehicle of Korean identity rather than as an imposition from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Its politics have never run in one direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Protestant refugees who fled the communist North after 1945 carried a fierce anticommunism into Southern churches, which aligned much of the denominational leadership with the authoritarian governments and later with the conservative parties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At the same time, dissident clergy developed minjung theology, which read the gospel from the position of the suffering poor, and church premises sheltered activists the police were hunting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Catholic cathedral in central Seoul served as a refuge for protesters during the decisive confrontation of 1987, and the cardinal's refusal to let the police enter carried real weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Today the churches remain an organised force in public life, mobilising against anti-discrimination legislation and turning out large conservative rallies, even as attendance falls among Koreans under thirty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Donald N. Clark, “Christianity in Modern Korea,” <i>Education About Asia</i> (2006), https://doi.org/10.65959/eaa.722. [Open access]","Saehwan Lee and Seil Oh, “Religion and Public Conflict in the Post-COVID Era: The Case of Protestant Churches in South Korea,” <i>Religions</i> 12, no. 10 (2021): 851, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel12100851. [Open access]","Andrew Eungi Kim, “Minjung Theology in Contemporary Korea: Liberation Theology and a Reconsideration of Secularization Theory,” <i>Religions</i> 9, no. 12 (2018): 415, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel9120415. [Open access]","Thomas Kern and Sang-hui Nam, “The Making of a Social Movement,” <i>Current Sociology</i> 57, no. 5 (2009): 637–660, https://doi.org/10.1177/0011392109337649. [Open access]","Dong-Choon Kim, “The Social Grounds of Anticommunism in South Korea: Crisis of the Ruling Class and Anticommunist Reaction,” <i>Asian Journal of German and European Studies</i> 2, no. 1 (2017), https://doi.org/10.1186/s40856-017-0018-1. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did it grow here and not in China or Japan?","a":"Because it arrived while Korea was losing its independence rather than while the state was strong. A foreign religion tied to national survival is received very differently from one arriving alongside foreign power."},{"q":"Why did northern refugees push the churches rightwards?","a":"Because they had lost everything to a communist state and often seen congregations destroyed. Anticommunism was not an abstract position for them, and they arrived in numbers large enough to shape the denominations they joined."},{"q":"Why were church buildings useful to the democracy movement?","a":"Because entering one carried a political cost the police preferred to avoid, and foreign denominations were watching. That hesitation bought activists the hours and the meeting space that no other institution could offer."}]},
    "pea-076": {"abstract":"The <b>Martial Law Crisis</b> began late on 3 December 2024, when President Yoon Suk-yeol appeared on television to declare emergency martial law across South Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He justified it by accusing the opposition-controlled National Assembly of paralysing the government and of sheltering anti-state forces sympathetic to North Korea, language drawn directly from the vocabulary of the dictatorships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The proximate quarrel was ordinary parliamentary conflict, since the opposition had cut his budget and impeached a succession of his appointees, none of which is a recognised ground for suspending constitutional government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Troops and police were dispatched to the assembly building within the hour, and helicopters landed soldiers on its grounds while staff barricaded the doors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Legislators climbed over fences and pushed past soldiers to reach the chamber, and the 190 members who got inside voted unanimously to lift the decree, which the constitution obliged the president to obey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The whole episode lasted about six hours, and Yoon rescinded the declaration before dawn on 4 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Assembly impeached him on 14 December, the Constitutional Court removed him from office in April 2025, and he was subsequently prosecuted over the attempt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two readings of the crisis are possible and both are held seriously, the first being that the institutions worked exactly as designed, since the legislature, the courts and the public all refused to comply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The second is more troubling, because a president was able to order soldiers into the legislature at all, and a substantial minority of citizens supported him when he did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That combination is why the episode is now studied as a case of attempted democratic backsliding in a country long treated as a consolidated democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Jamie Doucette, “Converging Histories: South Korea's Martial Law Crisis in a Global Conjunctural Frame,” <i>Critical Asian Studies</i> 57, no. 3 (2025): 357–374, https://doi.org/10.1080/14672715.2025.2528101. [Open access]","Benjamin A. Engel, “Making Sense of South Korea's Senseless Martial Law Declaration,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 22, no. 10 (2024), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466024038221. [Open access]","Tom Gerald Daly, “Designing Resilient Institutions: Countering Democratic Backsliding in Asia,” <i>International IDEA</i> (2025), https://doi.org/10.31752/idea.2025.15. [Open access]","Brandon Beomseob Park and Yujin J. Jung, “Populism, Ideology, and the Endorsement of Martial Law: A Conditional Relationship,” <i>Government and Opposition</i> 61 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1017/gov.2026.10032. [Open access]","Hyungjoon Jun and Hyun Jung Lee, “The Meritocratic Paradox: Confucian Democracy and the Crisis of Procedural Legitimacy in Contemporary South Korea,” <i>Asian Journal of Comparative Politics</i> 11, no. 1 (2025): 18–33, https://doi.org/10.1177/20578911251391390. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did reaching the chamber matter so much?","a":"Because the constitution requires the president to lift martial law if a majority of the assembly demands it. The decree could not be argued away, only voted away, which made physical access to the chamber the whole contest."},{"q":"Why accuse the opposition of serving North Korea over a budget dispute?","a":"Because that accusation is the one that has historically justified emergency powers in Korea. Framing a parliamentary majority as an enemy force converts a political defeat into a security threat, which is the only thing martial law can be claimed for."},{"q":"Why do people disagree about whether this proves democracy is strong or weak?","a":"Because it shows both at once. The attempt failed in hours, which is the strength, and the attempt was made at all by an elected president with troops at his disposal, which is the weakness."}]},
    "pea-077": {"abstract":"<b>Yoon Suk-yeol</b> was president of South Korea from May 2022 until his removal in April 2025, and he had never held elected office before winning the post.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He spent his career as a prosecutor, and he made his name leading the investigations that convicted the former presidents Park Geun-hye and Lee Myung-bak, which gave him a reputation for pursuing the powerful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The progressive president Moon Jae-in appointed him prosecutor-general in 2019, after which the two fell out badly over proposals to curb the prosecution service, and he resigned, joined the conservative party and won its nomination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His victory in March 2022 came by less than one percentage point, the closest presidential result the country has recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A central plank of that campaign was the pledge to abolish the ministry of gender equality and family, aimed at young men who believed that feminism had left them disadvantaged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In foreign policy he moved decisively, settling the wartime forced-labour dispute with Japan on terms many Koreans found too generous, and deepening trilateral security cooperation with Japan and the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At home he governed against a National Assembly the opposition controlled throughout his term, which left him legislating by decree and veto while his budgets were cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Scandals involving his wife, a crowd crush that killed more than 150 people in Seoul, and his combative style with the press kept his approval ratings low for most of the presidency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The crisis came on 3 December 2024, when he declared martial law and sent troops to the legislature, a decision the Assembly overturned before dawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was impeached within two weeks, removed unanimously by the Constitutional Court in April 2025, and prosecuted over the attempt, which made him the third Korean president to face criminal trial after leaving office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Jamie Doucette, “Converging Histories: South Korea's Martial Law Crisis in a Global Conjunctural Frame,” <i>Critical Asian Studies</i> 57, no. 3 (2025): 357–374, https://doi.org/10.1080/14672715.2025.2528101. [Open access]","Benjamin A. Engel, “Making Sense of South Korea's Senseless Martial Law Declaration,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 22, no. 10 (2024), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466024038221. [Open access]","Eunbin Chung, Youn Ki, and Yuree Noh, “Gendered Backsliding: Anti-Gender Politics as a Mechanism of Democratic Erosion in South Korea,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 8 (2026), https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2026.1749416. [Open access]","Brandon Beomseob Park and Yujin J. Jung, “Populism, Ideology, and the Endorsement of Martial Law: A Conditional Relationship,” <i>Government and Opposition</i> 61 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1017/gov.2026.10032. [Open access]","Hyungjoon Jun and Hyun Jung Lee, “The Meritocratic Paradox: Confucian Democracy and the Crisis of Procedural Legitimacy in Contemporary South Korea,” <i>Asian Journal of Comparative Politics</i> 11, no. 1 (2025): 18–33, https://doi.org/10.1177/20578911251391390. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could a prosecutor go straight to the presidency?","a":"Because Korean parties are weak enough that a nomination can be won by a famous outsider. His reputation for jailing presidents supplied the anti-establishment credential that years of party service would otherwise have to buy."},{"q":"Why campaign against a small government ministry?","a":"Because it was a symbol rather than a policy. Abolishing it promised nothing material but told young men that their grievance was recognised, which in a race decided by a fraction of a point was worth more than a programme."},{"q":"Why did a president who had prosecuted presidents end up prosecuted?","a":"Because the office and the exposure come together in Korea. The same single term that limits a president also guarantees a successor who inherits the prosecution service and no reason to protect the person who just left."}]},
    "pea-078": {"abstract":"<b>Park Geun-hye</b> was president of South Korea from 2013 until March 2017, the first woman to hold the office and the first head of state the country has removed by impeachment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> She is the daughter of the dictator Park Chung-hee, and she served as acting first lady as a young woman after her mother was killed by a bullet intended for her father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her political career rested heavily on that inheritance, since older conservative voters associated her with the growth decades and treated a vote for her as a verdict on her father's record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She was a formidable campaigner in her own right, rebuilding the conservative party after several defeats and winning the presidency in December 2012 with an outright majority of the vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In office she promised an expansion of welfare and a creative economy of new industries, though most of the welfare pledges were quietly scaled back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Her presidency accumulated damage well before the scandal that ended it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The sinking of the Sewol ferry in 2014 left her looking remote and unaccountable, and her government was later found to have maintained a blacklist of thousands of writers, film-makers and artists judged politically unfriendly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Her 2015 agreement with Japan on the wartime comfort women was presented as final, and was rejected by many of the surviving women and by much of the public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In late 2016 it emerged that a private confidante with no government position had been editing her speeches, seeing classified material and extracting large sums from the conglomerates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Months of candlelight protest followed, the Assembly impeached her, the Constitutional Court removed her unanimously, and she was convicted and imprisoned before receiving a pardon at the end of 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Seungsook Moon, “The Cultural Politics of Remembering Park Chung Hee,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 7, no. 19 (2009), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466009023894. [Open access]","Hyejin Kim, “‘Spoon Theory' and the Fall of a Populist Princess in Seoul,” <i>The Journal of Asian Studies</i> 76, no. 4 (2017): 839–849, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0021911817000778. [Open access]","Nan Kim, “Candlelight and the Yellow Ribbon: Catalyzing Re-Democratization in South Korea,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 15, no. 17 (2017), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466017013717. [Open access]","Jamie Doucette, “The Occult of Personality: Korea's Candlelight Protests and the Impeachment of Park Geun-hye,” <i>The Journal of Asian Studies</i> 76, no. 4 (2017): 851–860, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0021911817000821. [Open access]","Sanghoon Kim-Leffingwell, “Authoritarian Legacies and Partisan Bias in Corruption Voting,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 23, no. 2 (2023): 241–262, https://doi.org/10.1017/jea.2023.5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did her father's record work as a campaign asset?","a":"Because for many older voters the growth decades were the best years of their lives. Her candidacy let them vote for that period rather than for a programme, which no ordinary politician could offer."},{"q":"Why did a ferry sinking damage a president so badly?","a":"Because the failure was one of response rather than cause. Her whereabouts during the crucial hours went unexplained, and a government that cannot account for itself while children drown loses the benefit of the doubt permanently."},{"q":"Why was a private confidante's involvement worse than ordinary corruption?","a":"Because it was not about money going astray but about who was governing. Korean voters had elected a president, and the revelation suggested that state authority was being exercised by someone nobody had chosen."}]},
    "pea-079": {"abstract":"The <b>Sewol</b> was a passenger ferry that capsized and sank off the south-western coast of South Korea on 16 April 2014, killing 304 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the dead were second-year pupils from a single high school near Seoul who were travelling to Jeju island on a school trip, so the loss fell on one community with terrible concentration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ship had been illegally modified to carry more passengers, was loaded far beyond its limit with improperly secured cargo, and was running with too little ballast water to stay stable in a turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As it listed, the crew repeatedly instructed passengers over the public address system to remain in their cabins, and then abandoned the vessel themselves while those instructions were still being broadcast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The coastguard reached the scene but failed to enter the ship or organise an evacuation, and the whole sinking was carried live on national television over several hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The political consequences were as large as the human ones, because the disaster was understood as the product of a system rather than as an accident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Regulators had approved the modifications, inspectors had passed the overloading, and the pattern of business and officialdom accommodating one another was familiar to everyone watching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bereaved families became a durable political movement, campaigning for an independent investigation with real powers, holding hunger strikes and taking the government to court for years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were attacked in the conservative press as opportunists, while the yellow ribbon adopted in their support became a general symbol of distrust in the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the corruption scandal broke in 2016, those ribbons were carried through the candlelight crowds, which is why the disaster is treated as the beginning of the sequence that removed President Park Geun-hye.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Sun Mi Cho et al., “Social Relationship Changes in Victim Families due to a Social Disaster: Experiences of Student Victims' Families in the South Korean Sewol Ferry Disaster,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 12 (2017): e0188699, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0188699. [Open access]","Sera Yeong Seo Park, “Sewŏl Activism and the Changing Tenors and Tactics of Political Action in South Korea,” <i>The Journal of Korean Studies</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 219–239, https://doi.org/10.1215/07311613-12178320. [Open access]","Minyoung Kim, “Evolving Emotion, Situated Context, and Movement Activism: The Case of Bereaved Families in South Korea,” <i>Sociological Perspectives</i> 68, no. 1–3 (2025): 102–122, https://doi.org/10.1177/07311214251321445. [Open access]","Nan Kim, “Candlelight and the Yellow Ribbon: Catalyzing Re-Democratization in South Korea,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 15, no. 17 (2017), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466017013717. [Open access]","Miriam Bartolozzi, “Grief and Democracy: Mourning, Mobilisation, and Public Distrust after Recent Tragedies in South Korea,” <i>Heteroglossia</i> 22 (2026): 149–180, https://doi.org/10.13138/h.vi22.5371. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the instructions to stay in the cabins so deadly?","a":"Because the ship was rolling further every minute. Passengers who moved early could still reach the decks, and those who obeyed were trapped once the corridors turned on their side."},{"q":"Why did this become a political question rather than a maritime one?","a":"Because every failure had a licence behind it. The modifications were approved and the overloading was passed by inspection, so the ship was a product of the regulatory system rather than an exception to it."},{"q":"Why did the bereaved families stay organised for years?","a":"Because what they wanted was not compensation but a finding of fact. An investigation with the power to compel evidence was repeatedly obstructed, and until it happened there was nothing for them to accept and stop."}]},
    "pea-080": {"abstract":"<b>Choi Soon-sil gate</b> was the corruption scandal that broke in October 2016 and destroyed the presidency of Park Geun-hye within five months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Choi Soon-sil held no government position of any kind, but she was the president's oldest confidante, the daughter of a religious figure who had attached himself to Park after her mother's assassination in 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A broadcaster obtained a discarded tablet computer containing drafts of presidential speeches, which showed that she had been editing them before delivery and had seen classified material she had no right to see.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Investigators established that she and presidential aides had pressed the conglomerates to pay tens of billions of won into two foundations she controlled, money the firms later described as extorted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Samsung's support for her daughter's equestrian career became the most damaging strand, because it ran alongside a corporate merger the national pension fund had backed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What made the scandal explosive was less the money than the question of who had been governing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Koreans had voted for a president and were told that an unelected private person had been shaping decisions from outside the state altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The detail that mobilised students first was her daughter's admission to a prestigious university on terms other applicants had not enjoyed, an offence against fairness in a society where examinations are treated as the one honest ladder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Weekly candlelight demonstrations grew through the autumn until they were the largest in Korean history, and the National Assembly impeached the president on 9 December 2016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Choi was convicted and imprisoned, the president was removed and later convicted, and the head of Samsung was convicted of bribery, which is a rare outcome for a Korean scandal of this scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jamie Doucette, “The Occult of Personality: Korea's Candlelight Protests and the Impeachment of Park Geun-hye,” <i>The Journal of Asian Studies</i> 76, no. 4 (2017): 851–860, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0021911817000821. [Open access]","Hyejin Kim, “‘Spoon Theory' and the Fall of a Populist Princess in Seoul,” <i>The Journal of Asian Studies</i> 76, no. 4 (2017): 839–849, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0021911817000778. [Open access]","Nan Kim, “Candlelight and the Yellow Ribbon: Catalyzing Re-Democratization in South Korea,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 15, no. 17 (2017), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466017013717. [Open access]","Paul Y. Chang, “Candlelight Protests in South Korea: The Legacies of Authoritarianism and Democratization,” <i>Ewha Journal of Social Sciences</i> 34, no. 1 (2018): 5–18, https://doi.org/10.16935/ejss.2018.34.1.001. [Open access]","Sanghoon Kim-Leffingwell, “Authoritarian Legacies and Partisan Bias in Corruption Voting,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 23, no. 2 (2023): 241–262, https://doi.org/10.1017/jea.2023.5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a university admission more inflammatory than millions in payments?","a":"Because students could measure it against their own lives. Corporate money is abstract, while a place at a good university is the thing an entire generation has spent its adolescence competing for on supposedly equal terms."},{"q":"Why did the firms call it extortion rather than bribery?","a":"Because the legal consequences differ entirely. A victim of extortion has paid under compulsion, while a briber has bought something, and the same transfer can be described either way depending on who is asked."},{"q":"Why did a tablet computer settle what years of rumour had not?","a":"Because it moved the claim from allegation to document. Speech drafts held before delivery cannot be explained as gossip, and they dated the access precisely enough that denial stopped being available."}]},
    "pea-081": {"abstract":"<b>Moon Jae-in</b> was president of South Korea from May 2017 to May 2022, elected in the snap contest held after Park Geun-hye's removal from office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was born to parents who had fled the North during the Korean War, and he worked as a human rights lawyer in Busan before entering government as chief of staff to President Roh Moo-hyun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His economic programme was built on what his government called income-led growth, raising the minimum wage steeply and capping the working week at 52 hours on the argument that higher wages would generate the demand to pay for themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Economists divided sharply over the results, with critics pointing to job losses among small employers and supporters to a genuine narrowing of wage inequality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also began a phase-out of nuclear power, expanded public health coverage and won broad approval for the government's handling of the coronavirus pandemic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> His presidency is remembered above all for the diplomacy of 2018, when he met Kim Jong-un three times and brokered the first meeting between a North Korean leader and a sitting American president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Winter Olympics at Pyeongchang provided the opening, with a joint team and a Northern delegation, and the two leaders met at the truce village on the border in April of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The process stalled completely after the American and North Korean leaders failed to reach agreement at their second summit in 2019, and nothing durable survived it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At home his standing was undermined by a surge in housing costs that put property in Seoul beyond young buyers, which struck directly at his claim to be governing for fairness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That grievance, together with a scandal over a close ally's family, is why the conservative candidate took the presidency back in 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Jin Hee Park, “Discourse Construction of Inter-Korean Summits in South Korean Newspapers: A Diachronic Study,” <i>Language & Communication</i> 78 (2021): 19–34, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.langcom.2021.02.001. [Open access]","Jamie Doucette, “The Postdevelopmental State,” <i>University of Michigan Press eBooks</i> (2024), https://doi.org/10.3998/mpub.12875326. [Open access]","Udo Merkel, “Sport, Politics and Reunification: A Comparative Analysis of Korea and Germany,” <i>The International Journal of the History of Sport</i> 26, no. 3 (2009): 406–428, https://doi.org/10.1080/09523360802602273. [Open access]","Seungwoo Han, “Between the Ladder and the Mirror: Subjective Class Consciousness and Voting under Inequality in South Korea,” <i>Japanese Journal of Political Science</i> 27, no. 1 (2026): 79–98, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1468109925100224. [Open access]","Miftah Farid Darussalam, “The Influence of Domestic Politics of South Korea on its Relations with North Korea and the United States,” <i>Jurnal Ilmu Sosial dan Ilmu Politik</i> 23, no. 3 (2020): 250–264, https://doi.org/10.22146/jsp.48652. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why would raising wages be expected to raise growth?","a":"Because the argument treats consumption as the constraint. If ordinary households have too little to spend, firms have no reason to invest, so putting money into wages is supposed to create the demand that justifies the cost."},{"q":"Why did the Olympics open a diplomatic channel?","a":"Because a sporting event provides a reason to talk that commits neither side to anything. Sending a delegation to a games is deniable as a political concession, which makes it the cheapest possible first step."},{"q":"Why were house prices so politically lethal for him?","a":"Because his whole appeal was fairness. A young Korean watching the cost of an apartment double while wages crept up does not hear an argument about interest rates, they hear that the ladder was pulled up."}]},
    "pea-082": {"abstract":"<b>Roh Moo-hyun</b> was president of South Korea from 2003 to 2008, and he reached the office by a route that no previous holder had taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He came from a poor farming family, never attended university, and qualified as a lawyer by passing the bar examination on his own, after which he defended students and workers charged under the security laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As a member of parliament he repeatedly gave up safe seats to contest hopeless ones in the conservative south-east, a deliberate protest against regional voting that cost him elections and made his reputation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His presidential campaign in 2002 was carried by an online supporters' movement of young voters, the first Korean election in which internet organising decided the outcome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He entered office promising to break the hold of the prosecution service, the big newspapers and the regional machines on Korean political life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In March 2004 the opposition-controlled National Assembly impeached him over a minor breach of election law, suspending him from office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Voters treated the move as an abuse of the procedure, gave his party a parliamentary majority in the election held weeks later, and the Constitutional Court restored him in May on the grounds that the offence was too small to justify removal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His record in office frustrated many of his own supporters, since he signed a free trade agreement with the United States, sent troops to Iraq and failed to loosen the grip of the conglomerates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He held a second inter-Korean summit in 2007 and pushed to move much of the government to a new administrative city, a plan the Constitutional Court partly blocked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After leaving office he was investigated over payments to his family, and he took his own life in May 2009, an event that reshaped how Korean progressives remember him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Jin Wook Kim, “Korean Constitutional Court and Constitutionalism in Political Dynamics: Focusing on Presidential Impeachment,” <i>Constitutional Review</i> 4, no. 2 (2018): 222–254, https://doi.org/10.31078/consrev423. [Open access]","J.-r. Yeh, “Presidential Politics and the Judicial Facilitation of Dialogue between Political Actors in New Asian Democracies: Comparing the South Korean and Taiwanese Experiences,” <i>International Journal of Constitutional Law</i> 8, no. 4 (2010): 911–949, https://doi.org/10.1093/icon/mor006. [Open access]","Ronda Hauben, “Online Grassroots Journalism and Participatory Democracy in South Korea,” <i>Korea Yearbook</i> (2007), https://doi.org/10.1163/ej.9789004164406.i-306.26. [Open access]","Olli Hellmann, “Party System Institutionalization Without Parties: Evidence from Korea,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2014): 53–84, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800009589. [Open access]","Jamie Doucette, “Debating Economic Democracy in South Korea: The Costs of Commensurability,” <i>Critical Asian Studies</i> 47, no. 3 (2015): 388–413, https://doi.org/10.1080/14672715.2015.1057025. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why contest seats he was certain to lose?","a":"Because the point was the attempt rather than the seat. Standing where his party could not win said that a politician should not be owed a constituency by his birthplace, and losing publicly made the argument better than winning elsewhere would have."},{"q":"Why did impeaching him strengthen him?","a":"Because voters judged the punishment against the offence. A technical breach of campaign rules did not look to most people like grounds for undoing an election, so the attempt read as the assembly overturning their choice."},{"q":"Why did his own supporters end up disappointed?","a":"Because an outsider still inherits the constraints of the office. A trade agreement, an alliance commitment and the weight of the conglomerates do not yield to a president's biography, whatever his supporters had read into it."}]},
    "pea-083": {"abstract":"<b>Lee Myung-bak</b> was president of South Korea from 2008 to 2013, and he came to the office from business rather than from the democracy movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He rose through Hyundai Construction from a poor background to run the company while still in his thirties, earning the nickname bulldozer for the way he drove projects through, and he later served a term as mayor of Seoul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He won the presidency by a wide margin on the promise of seven percent annual growth, after a decade of progressive government that many voters felt had delivered neither prosperity nor security.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His first months were nonetheless consumed by mass candlelight protests against the resumption of American beef imports, which began over food safety and broadened into a verdict on his style of governing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He abandoned the Sunshine Policy, making aid to the North conditional on steps towards denuclearisation, and relations deteriorated through the sinking of a Southern warship and the shelling of an island in 2010.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> His domestic legacy is dominated by the four rivers project, a vast dredging and damming programme presented as flood control and river restoration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was the most expensive public works scheme the country had undertaken, and subsequent audits and water quality studies left it widely regarded as an environmental failure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also promoted green growth as a national brand and hosted the first G20 summit held in Asia, which suited his view of Korea as a global middle power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Investigations later established that the national intelligence service had run online campaigns against opposition politicians during his term, including in the 2012 presidential election.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was convicted of bribery and embezzlement in 2018 and sentenced to a long prison term before being pardoned at the end of 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Jamie Doucette and Se-Woong Koo, “Distorting Democracy: Politics by Public Security in Contemporary South Korea,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 12, no. 8 (2014), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466014027430. [Open access]","Jamie Doucette, “The Postdevelopmental State,” <i>University of Michigan Press eBooks</i> (2024), https://doi.org/10.3998/mpub.12875326. [Open access]","Miftah Farid Darussalam, “The Influence of Domestic Politics of South Korea on its Relations with North Korea and the United States,” <i>Jurnal Ilmu Sosial dan Ilmu Politik</i> 23, no. 3 (2020): 250–264, https://doi.org/10.22146/jsp.48652. [Open access]","Sanghoon Kim-Leffingwell, “Authoritarian Legacies and Partisan Bias in Corruption Voting,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 23, no. 2 (2023): 241–262, https://doi.org/10.1017/jea.2023.5. [Open access]","Jojin V. John, “Globalization, National Identity and Foreign Policy: Understanding ‘Global Korea',” <i>The Copenhagen Journal of Asian Studies</i> 33, no. 2 (2016): 38–57, https://doi.org/10.22439/cjas.v33i2.4965. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a dispute about beef become a mass movement?","a":"Because it condensed a broader objection into something concrete. People who could not easily protest a governing style could protest a specific decision taken quickly and without consultation, and food safety gave families a reason to join."},{"q":"Why did he make aid to the North conditional?","a":"Because he judged that a decade of unconditional engagement had bought nothing verifiable. Tying assistance to disarmament steps was meant to restore leverage, at the accepted cost of the contact that engagement had built."},{"q":"Why is the rivers project remembered as a failure?","a":"Because the dams that were supposed to improve the water slowed it. Standing water in warm months produced algal blooms, so the programme is judged by an outcome that was measurable and went the wrong way."}]},
    "pea-084": {"abstract":"<b>Lee Jae-myung</b> became president of South Korea in June 2025, winning the snap election called after Yoon Suk-yeol was removed from office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His background is unlike that of almost any other senior Korean politician, since he worked in factories as a boy instead of attending secondary school and lost the use of part of an arm in an industrial press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He qualified through examinations taken outside the school system, passed the bar, and practised as a human rights and labour lawyer before entering local government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As mayor of a city near Seoul and then as governor of the surrounding province he built a reputation for direct redistribution, introducing youth payments, a local currency intended to keep spending with small traders and an experiment with unconditional basic income.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He lost the presidential election of 2022 to Yoon by less than a percentage point, then took the leadership of the Democratic Party and survived a knife attack in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> His decisive moment came on the night of 3 December 2024, when he broadcast himself climbing over the fence of the National Assembly to reach the chamber and vote down the martial law decree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The footage was watched by enormous numbers of people while the outcome was still uncertain, and it made him the visible leader of the resistance to the declaration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He led his party to a commanding parliamentary majority and then to the presidency, so a progressive government now holds both branches at once, which is unusual in Korean politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is a polarising figure even among progressives, and he has faced a series of criminal proceedings over election law and a property development that his opponents treat as disqualifying and his supporters as politically driven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His presidency is too recent for any settled assessment, and everything here should be read as an account of how he arrived rather than of what he has done in office.","sources":["Jamie Doucette, “Converging Histories: South Korea's Martial Law Crisis in a Global Conjunctural Frame,” <i>Critical Asian Studies</i> 57, no. 3 (2025): 357–374, https://doi.org/10.1080/14672715.2025.2528101. [Open access]","Benjamin A. Engel, “Making Sense of South Korea's Senseless Martial Law Declaration,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 22, no. 10 (2024), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466024038221. [Open access]","Hyungjoon Jun and Hyun Jung Lee, “The Meritocratic Paradox: Confucian Democracy and the Crisis of Procedural Legitimacy in Contemporary South Korea,” <i>Asian Journal of Comparative Politics</i> 11, no. 1 (2025): 18–33, https://doi.org/10.1177/20578911251391390. [Open access]","Seungwoo Han, “Between the Ladder and the Mirror: Subjective Class Consciousness and Voting under Inequality in South Korea,” <i>Japanese Journal of Political Science</i> 27, no. 1 (2026): 79–98, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1468109925100224. [Open access]","Brandon Beomseob Park and Yujin J. Jung, “Populism, Ideology, and the Endorsement of Martial Law: A Conditional Relationship,” <i>Government and Opposition</i> 61 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1017/gov.2026.10032. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does his background matter politically?","a":"Because Korean elite politics runs through a few universities and the professions they feed. A leader who reached the bar without schooling can make claims about inequality that a graduate of the usual route cannot make credibly."},{"q":"Why was the livestream so consequential?","a":"Because nobody knew what was happening and the broadcasters were uncertain. A live feed of an opposition leader climbing into the legislature told the country both that the decree was being resisted and that resisting it was possible."},{"q":"Why is holding both branches unusual enough to note?","a":"Because the two elections normally fall out of step and hand the assembly to the opposition. A government with a majority can legislate rather than govern by decree, which changes what a presidency can attempt."}]},
    "pea-085": {"abstract":"The <b>National Assembly</b> is the single chamber of the South Korean parliament, with 300 members serving four-year terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The large majority are elected in single-member districts on a first-past-the-post basis, and a smaller block is distributed by proportional representation, an arrangement that rewards the two big parties and squeezes everyone else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its powers are substantial, covering legislation, the budget, confirmation of the prime minister, an annual inspection of the entire administration, and impeachment, which requires a simple majority for a minister and two thirds of the house for a president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It also holds the authority that proved decisive in December 2024, since a majority vote obliges the president to lift a declaration of martial law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its speaker must resign from their party on taking the chair, a rule meant to make the office something other than an instrument of the majority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The assembly and the presidency are elected on separate cycles, so the two are frequently held by opposing parties, and divided government is the normal condition rather than the exception.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A president facing a hostile majority can be reduced to governing by decree and veto, which is one reason the office generates the confrontations it does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An attempt in 2020 to make the proportional seats compensate for distortions in the district results was immediately defeated when both major parties registered satellite parties to collect those seats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That episode is often cited as evidence of how little institutional reform constrains Korean parties, which are organisationally weak but tactically formidable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Proceedings are frequently disrupted by walkouts, boycotts and occasional physical obstruction, though the chamber's conduct during the martial law crisis considerably improved its standing with the public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Olli Hellmann, “Party System Institutionalization Without Parties: Evidence from Korea,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2014): 53–84, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800009589. [Open access]","Youngmi Kim, “The Politics of Coalition in Korea,” <i>Routledge</i> (2011), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203821138. [Open access]","Jörg Michael Dostal, “South Korea: The Lasting Pitfalls of the ‘Imperial Presidency’,” <i>The Political Quarterly</i> 94, no. 1 (2023): 57–68, https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-923x.13232. [Open access]","Benjamin A. Engel, “Making Sense of South Korea's Senseless Martial Law Declaration,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal: Japan Focus</i> 22, no. 10 (2024), https://doi.org/10.1017/s1557466024038221. [Open access]","Hyun-Chool Lee and Alexandre Repkine, “A Spatial Analysis of the Voting Patterns in the South Korean General Elections of 2016,” <i>Social Sciences</i> 11, no. 9 (2022): 389, https://doi.org/10.3390/socsci11090389. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the constitution let the assembly overturn martial law?","a":"Because the drafters had watched the power used to shut legislatures down. Giving the chamber a vote the president must obey puts the one check outside his control, which is exactly what it proved to be in 2024."},{"q":"Why did satellite parties defeat the 2020 reform so easily?","a":"Because the formula compensated parties that won few district seats, and a party with no districts by design qualifies perfectly. Registering a temporary affiliate turned the correction into an extra allocation."},{"q":"Why does divided government produce confrontation rather than negotiation?","a":"Because neither side needs the other to survive. A president serves a fixed term regardless of the assembly, and the assembly cannot be dissolved, so refusing to deal carries no cost until the next scheduled election."}]},
    "pea-086": {"abstract":"The <b>People Power Party</b> is the main conservative party of South Korea, and it has carried its present name only since 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its organisational line runs back through a long succession of renamings and mergers to the party Park Chung-hee built to support his rule, and then to the party of the generals who followed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That descent is disputed in its own ranks, since some members embrace the developmental record while others would prefer a founding story that begins at democratisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its electoral heartland is the south-eastern Yeongnam region, above all the city of Daegu and the province around it, where its margins are among the largest recorded in any democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its coalition combines older voters, business interests, much of the large Protestant church leadership and those who prioritise the American alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On policy it favours market solutions, lower corporate taxation and a firm line towards North Korea, treating engagement as leverage given away rather than as diplomacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is socially conservative on family law, gender policy and the security legislation inherited from the authoritarian period, and it has been the main obstacle to comprehensive anti-discrimination law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The party fractured badly after the impeachment of Park Geun-hye in 2017, reunited under a new name, and then took the presidency back in 2022 behind a candidate who had never been a member until shortly before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The martial law declaration of December 2024 divided it again, since most of its legislators did not join the vote to lift the decree and the party then split over impeaching its own president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It has since been out of office and is engaged in the argument that follows every such defeat, over whether to hold its base or to widen it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Olli Hellmann, “Party System Institutionalization Without Parties: Evidence from Korea,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2014): 53–84, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800009589. [Open access]","Minji Jeong and Youseop Shin, “Post-War Korean Conservatism, Japanese Statism, and the Legacy of President Park Chung-hee in South Korea,” <i>The Korean Journal of International Studies</i> 16, no. 1 (2018): 57–76, https://doi.org/10.14731/kjis.2018.04.16.1.57. [Open access]","Sanghoon Kim-Leffingwell, “Authoritarian Legacies and Partisan Bias in Corruption Voting,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 23, no. 2 (2023): 241–262, https://doi.org/10.1017/jea.2023.5. [Open access]","Jamie Doucette, “Converging Histories: South Korea's Martial Law Crisis in a Global Conjunctural Frame,” <i>Critical Asian Studies</i> 57, no. 3 (2025): 357–374, https://doi.org/10.1080/14672715.2025.2528101. [Open access]","Hyun-Chool Lee and Alexandre Repkine, “A Spatial Analysis of the Voting Patterns in the South Korean General Elections of 2016,” <i>Social Sciences</i> 11, no. 9 (2022): 389, https://doi.org/10.3390/socsci11090389. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do Korean parties change their names so often?","a":"Because a name carries the last scandal with it. Rebranding costs a party very little when voters attach themselves to regions and leaders rather than to organisations, so shedding the label is cheaper than rebuilding its reputation."},{"q":"Why is the party's own lineage contested inside it?","a":"Because the inheritance is both its greatest asset and its greatest liability. The developmental record wins older voters, while the dictatorship attached to it is what keeps younger ones away."},{"q":"Why did impeaching its own president split it rather than unite it?","a":"Because each course was fatal in a different direction. Defending him meant defending troops sent into the legislature, and removing him meant admitting the party had put him there, and no leadership could choose without losing part of the coalition."}]},
    "pea-087": {"abstract":"The <b>Democratic Party of Korea</b> is the main progressive party of South Korea and the direct descendant of the opposition movements that fought the dictatorships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its lineage runs through the parties built around Kim Dae-jung, and through them back to the civilian opposition of the 1950s, though like every Korean party it has changed its name repeatedly along the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its strongest region is the south-western Honam provinces, where the memory of Gwangju and of decades of neglect produces margins that the conservative party cannot approach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its second base is the capital region around Seoul, and this is the more consequential one electorally, since roughly half the national population lives there and the seats are genuinely competitive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It has produced four presidents, Kim Dae-jung, Roh Moo-hyun, Moon Jae-in and Lee Jae-myung, under one party name or another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its consistent commitments are engagement with North Korea, a more redistributive welfare state, reform of the conglomerates and limits on the powers of the prosecution service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The chaebol commitment has been the least honoured, since every one of its governments has arrived promising economic democratisation and left having reached an accommodation with the same firms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is also the party more willing to press Japan on the colonial period, which makes relations with Tokyo swing with each change of government in Seoul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Internally it is organised around factions loyal to particular leaders rather than around programmatic wings, which is why splits, expulsions and reunifications recur so often.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Since 2020 it has held large parliamentary majorities, and it now holds the presidency as well, which gives it more control of the state than any Korean party has enjoyed for some time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Olli Hellmann, “Party System Institutionalization Without Parties: Evidence from Korea,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 14, no. 1 (2014): 53–84, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800009589. [Open access]","Youngmi Kim, “The Politics of Coalition in Korea,” <i>Routledge</i> (2011), https://doi.org/10.4324/9780203821138. [Open access]","Jamie Doucette, “Debating Economic Democracy in South Korea: The Costs of Commensurability,” <i>Critical Asian Studies</i> 47, no. 3 (2015): 388–413, https://doi.org/10.1080/14672715.2015.1057025. [Open access]","Seungwoo Han, “Between the Ladder and the Mirror: Subjective Class Consciousness and Voting under Inequality in South Korea,” <i>Japanese Journal of Political Science</i> 27, no. 1 (2026): 79–98, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1468109925100224. [Open access]","Hyun-Chool Lee and Alexandre Repkine, “A Spatial Analysis of the Voting Patterns in the South Korean General Elections of 2016,” <i>Social Sciences</i> 11, no. 9 (2022): 389, https://doi.org/10.3390/socsci11090389. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the capital region more decisive than the party's own heartland?","a":"Because the heartland is already won. Seats that return the same party by enormous margins cannot be gained again, while the metropolitan seats change hands and therefore decide which party governs."},{"q":"Why does chaebol reform keep failing under its governments?","a":"Because the firms hold the investment and the jobs the government will be judged on. A president with five years and an economy to answer for finds the conglomerates indispensable long before the reform is complete."},{"q":"Why do factions form around people rather than policies?","a":"Because nominations flow through leaders. A politician's career depends on a patron's standing inside the party rather than on a shared programme, so the groupings that matter are personal ones."}]},
    "pea-088": {"abstract":"The <b>Miracle on the Han River</b> is the name given to South Korea's transformation from one of the poorest countries in the world into an advanced industrial economy within a single lifetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The phrase borrows the form of an earlier European expression and points to the river that runs through Seoul, past the districts that were rice fields when the process began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the early 1960s income a head was comparable to the poorest parts of Africa, the country had almost no natural resources, and the war had destroyed much of what industry existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mechanism was a state that chose industries and then disciplined the firms it backed, granting credit, licences and protection in exchange for export performance that could be measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two preconditions are often underrated, the first being a thorough land reform that removed the landlord class, and the second an early commitment to mass education that produced a literate and disciplined workforce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Foreign circumstances helped considerably, including American aid, payments received on normalising relations with Japan in 1965, and the contracts and wages earned by sending troops and firms to Vietnam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The costs were paid by workers, through some of the longest hours in the industrial world, suppressed unions, industrial accidents and wages deliberately held below productivity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Growth was also concentrated, favouring the south-east over the south-west and the conglomerates over everyone else, which left political grievances that outlasted the poverty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The financial crisis of 1997 ended the confidence of the period and forced a restructuring whose social consequences, in insecure employment and inequality, are still being argued about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The deepest political argument is over who deserves the credit, since conservatives read the record as the justification of Park Chung-hee and the democratic movement reads it as the achievement of the people who did the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Frederic C. Deyo, Stephan Haggard, and Hagen Koo, “Labor in the Political Economy of East Asian Industrialization,” <i>Bulletin of Concerned Asian Scholars</i> 19, no. 2 (1987): 42–53, https://doi.org/10.1080/14672715.1987.10409890. [Open access]","Ito Peng, “Postindustrial Pressures, Political Regime Shifts, and Social Policy Reform in Japan and South Korea,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 4, no. 3 (2004): 389–425, https://doi.org/10.1017/s1598240800006020. [Open access]","Martin Hart-Landsberg, Seongjin Jeong, and Richard Westra, “Marxist Perspectives on South Korea in the Global Economy,” <i>Routledge</i> (2017), https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315249612. [Open access]","Sang-young Rhyu, “The Origins of Korean Chaebols and their Roots in the Korean War,” <i>Korean Journal of International Relations</i> 45, no. 5 (2005): 203–230, https://doi.org/10.14731/kjis.2005.12.45.5.203. [Open access]","James M. West, “Review Essay: The Suboptimal ‘Miracle' of South Korean State Capitalism,” <i>Bulletin of Concerned Asian Scholars</i> 19, no. 3 (1987): 60–71, https://doi.org/10.1080/14672715.1987.10409882. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did land reform matter to industrial growth?","a":"Because it removed a class whose wealth depended on rent rather than production, and gave the state a free hand. It also spread purchasing power and made education worth buying for families who now owned what they farmed."},{"q":"Why tie state support to exports rather than to output?","a":"Because a foreign buyer is an honest test. Anyone can sell to a protected home market, so requiring firms to compete abroad gave the government a measure of success it could not be bribed into misreading."},{"q":"Why is the credit for it still politically contested?","a":"Because the answer settles what the dictatorship was worth. If the growth required the repression, it can be defended as a price; if the growth came from workers and teachers, the repression was an additional cost rather than the cause."}]},
    "pea-089": {"abstract":"<b>Gender conflict</b> is the term Korean commentators use for the unusually sharp political division between young men and young women, which has become one of the organising cleavages of national politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The underlying conditions are severe, since South Korea has the widest gender pay gap among the rich democracies, one of the lowest shares of women in parliament and in senior management, and the lowest fertility rate ever recorded in a peacetime country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> These are connected rather than separate, because a labour market that penalises motherhood heavily makes having children a career decision rather than a family one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A powerful feminist movement emerged in the late 2010s, driven by a wave of prosecutions and testimony about sexual harassment and by anger over digitally distributed sexual crime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against it formed an equally organised reaction among young men, who argue that structural discrimination has ended, that military conscription falls on them alone, and that feminism has become a hiring advantage for women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What makes this politics rather than culture is that parties have found it worth mobilising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The conservative campaign of 2022 courted young men directly, promising to abolish the ministry responsible for gender equality and denying that structural sexism existed, and the presidential result turned on a margin smaller than the gap it opened between the sexes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Surveys in that election found young Korean men and women diverging more sharply than in any comparable democracy, which reverses the usual expectation that generations move together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Governments have spent very large sums on childcare and parental leave without reversing the birth rate, which suggests the obstacle lies in workplaces and expectations rather than in subsidies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The argument matters beyond Korea, since a wealthy democracy whose young people cannot agree enough to form households is a problem other ageing societies are watching closely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Eunbin Chung, Youn Ki, and Yuree Noh, “Gendered Backsliding: Anti-Gender Politics as a Mechanism of Democratic Erosion in South Korea,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 8 (2026), https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2026.1749416. [Open access]","Min Hee Go, “The Political Roots of Gender Divergence: Democratic Consolidation and Gender Equality in Taiwan and South Korea,” <i>Journal of East Asian Studies</i> 25, no. 1 (2025): 98–120, https://doi.org/10.1017/jea.2025.11. [Open access]","Yenn Lee, “The Digital Misogyny Pipeline: Deepfake-Based Sexual Violence in South Korean Schools and Universities,” <i>Gender and Education</i> 38, no. 6 (2026): 699–715, https://doi.org/10.1080/09540253.2026.2617577. [Open access]","Ito Peng, “Social Investment Policies in Canada, Australia, Japan, and South Korea,” <i>International Journal of Child Care and Education Policy</i> 5, no. 1 (2011): 41–53, https://doi.org/10.1007/2288-6729-5-1-41. [Open access]","Peter McDonald, “Gender Equity, Social Institutions and the Future of Fertility,” <i>Journal of the Australian Population Association</i> 17, no. 1 (2000): 1–16, https://doi.org/10.1007/bf03029445. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why would unequal workplaces lower the birth rate?","a":"Because the cost of a child falls almost entirely on the mother's career. When education and early employment are equal but promotion after childbirth is not, postponing or forgoing children is the rational response to the choice offered."},{"q":"Why do young men experience equality as a loss?","a":"Because they compare themselves with women their own age rather than with the generation above. Their fathers' advantages are invisible to them, while conscription and a brutal job market are immediate, so parity feels like being overtaken."},{"q":"Why has spending on childcare not worked?","a":"Because money addresses the wrong constraint. Subsidies reduce the cost of a child without changing working hours, promotion norms or the assumption that a mother will step back, which is what the decision actually turns on."}]},
    "pea-090": {"abstract":"<b>Rhee Syngman</b> was the first president of the Republic of Korea, holding office from its founding in 1948 until a student uprising forced him out in April 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had spent most of his adult life abroad as an independence campaigner, earning a doctorate in the United States and leading the Korean government in exile, which gave him a standing no rival at home could match.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He returned in 1945 with American support and built his rule on uncompromising anticommunism, an outlook that suited the occupation authorities and the emerging division of the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His government suppressed rebellions on Jeju island and in the south-west with great brutality before the Korean War had even begun, and thousands of suspected leftists were killed in mass executions during the war itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also passed the National Security Law in 1948 and abandoned the investigation into Koreans who had collaborated with Japanese rule, choosing officials with colonial-era experience over untested opponents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> He kept power by rewriting the rules whenever they threatened him, forcing through a constitutional amendment under martial law in 1952 and another in 1954 that removed the term limit for himself alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The press was censored, opposition leaders were prosecuted, and one was executed on a charge of espionage that a later court found baseless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The end came after the election of March 1960 was rigged so blatantly that protests broke out across the country, and the discovery of a schoolboy's body in a southern port turned them into a national rising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Police fired on demonstrators in Seoul, the army declined to continue the suppression, and he resigned on 26 April and left for exile in Hawaii.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The precedent he set, of a president amending the constitution to extend his own tenure, was followed by every authoritarian ruler who came after him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Dong-Choon Kim, “The Social Grounds of Anticommunism in South Korea: Crisis of the Ruling Class and Anticommunist Reaction,” <i>Asian Journal of German and European Studies</i> 2, no. 1 (2017), https://doi.org/10.1186/s40856-017-0018-1. [Open access]","Tom Ginsburg, James Melton, and Zachary Elkins, “On the Evasion of Executive Term Limits,” <i>William and Mary Law Review</i> 52, no. 6 (2011): 1807–1872, https://doi.org/10.26153/tsw/2241. [Open access]","Eun Suk Sa, “Development of Press Freedom in South Korea since Japanese Colonial Rule,” <i>Asian Culture and History</i> 1, no. 2 (2009): 3–17, https://doi.org/10.5539/ach.v1n2p3. [Open access]","Aurel Croissant, David Kuehn, and Tanja Eschenauer, “Mass Protests and the Military,” <i>Journal of Democracy</i> 29, no. 3 (2018): 141–155, https://doi.org/10.1353/jod.2018.0051. [Open access]","György Széll, “Anticommunism in Korea and Germany in Times of Cold War,” <i>Asian Journal of German and European Studies</i> 2, no. 1 (2017), https://doi.org/10.1186/s40856-017-0014-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did exile abroad make him the strongest candidate at home?","a":"Because it left no record to hold against him. Koreans who had stayed had made accommodations with the colonial government in order to live, while a man absent for decades arrived with his nationalist credentials unspent."},{"q":"Why keep officials who had served the Japanese?","a":"Because they were the people who knew how to run a state, and their compromised past made them dependent on him. Purging them would have handed the administration to the nationalist left he regarded as the greater threat."},{"q":"Why did the army's refusal end his rule?","a":"Because the police alone could not hold the streets. Once the soldiers declined to fire on students, the government had no instrument left between concession and a massacre it could not survive."}]},
    "pea-091": {"abstract":"<b>Leninism</b> is the organisational doctrine that explains how communist parties take power and, more importantly, how they keep it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Marx described the revolution he expected but said very little about how it would be carried out or what the resulting government would look like, and Lenin supplied exactly that missing part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His answer was a vanguard party of disciplined professionals who would organise the workers, lead the revolution and then run the state that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Marx had expected the state to wither away once class rule ended, and Lenin ensured that it did nothing of the kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a Leninist system the party holds a monopoly on political organisation, competing with no one and suppressing any attempt to build associations independent of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It makes the significant policy decisions and appoints its own members to every leading post in the government, the courts, the military and the security services.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It also plants cells throughout government offices, workplaces, schools and neighbourhoods, which both monitor what happens there and provide ideological instruction to members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A ruling Leninist party stays a vanguard rather than becoming a mass movement, so only a small fraction of the population is admitted and fewer than one applicant in ten is accepted in China today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Bruce Dickson argues that this inheritance, rather than economic performance alone, is the reason the Chinese Communist Party has survived seven decades that included famine, factional war and the collapse of every comparable regime elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The prominence of the party in daily life has risen and fallen since 1949, but under Xi Jinping it has been pushed forward again, in his formulation that the party leads everything in every direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Dickson, <i>The Party and the People: Chinese Politics in the 21st Century</i> (Princeton University Press, 2021), https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691216966.","Patricia M. Thornton, “Party All the Time: The CCP in Comparative and Historical Perspective,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 248, no. S1 (2021): 1–15, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741021000965. [Open access]","Alexander Dukalskis and Johannes Gerschewski, “Adapting or Freezing? Ideological Reactions of Communist Regimes to a Post-Communist World,” <i>Government and Opposition</i> 55, no. 3 (2018): 511–532, https://doi.org/10.1017/gov.2018.40. [Open access]","Jérôme Doyon and Long Yang, “Shades of Red: Changing Understandings of Political Loyalty in the Chinese Communist Party, 1921–2021,” <i>Journal of Current Chinese Affairs</i> 51, no. 3 (2022): 386–410, https://doi.org/10.1177/18681026221119220. [Open access]","Émilie Frenkiel and Anna Shpakovskaya, “The Evolution of Representative Claim-Making by the Chinese Communist Party: From Mao to Xi, 1949–2019,” <i>Politics and Governance</i> 7, no. 3 (2019): 208–219, https://doi.org/10.17645/pag.v7i3.2151. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did communism need an organisational doctrine at all?","a":"Because Marx had written about the destination rather than the journey. A movement that expects history to arrive still has to decide who acts, in what order and under whose command, and none of that was in the theory."},{"q":"Why stay a vanguard once the party already governs?","a":"Because admitting everyone would dissolve the thing that makes it a party rather than a population. Selectivity keeps membership valuable, discipline enforceable and the leadership's instructions reliably carried out."},{"q":"Why put cells in schools and neighbourhoods rather than only in government?","a":"Because the party governs through information as much as through orders. A cell in a factory or a residential block reports what people are doing where no ministry could see, which is what makes the monopoly enforceable."}]},
    "pea-092": {"abstract":"<b>Party cells</b> are the small committees of Communist Party members embedded inside other organisations, and they are the hallmark of a Leninist system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> China has several million of them, in government offices, state-owned enterprises, private firms, universities, hospitals, villages and residential blocks, so that wherever party members work or live they belong to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their function is twofold, since they take the significant decisions inside the body that hosts them and they report on whether its activities match the party's directives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They also convene the ordinary members working in that organisation to study new policy documents and leadership speeches, which is how instructions reach the base without passing through the state bureaucracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They are deliberately obscure, and the identity of a cell's members and the content of its deliberations are usually not made public even inside the organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Their reach contracted sharply during the reform era, when private employment and the freedom to move between cities made the party far less present in ordinary life than it had been under Mao.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under Xi Jinping that retreat has been deliberately reversed, and cells have been pushed into private companies, joint ventures, foreign-invested enterprises and non-governmental organisations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Firms often accept them because a cell can be genuinely useful, smoothing regulatory approvals and personnel matters, so the arrangement is not simply imposed against resistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The effect nonetheless is that an enterprise contains a body loyal to an outside organisation and answerable to it, which is what distinguishes this from ordinary business regulation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Understanding these cells explains how a party of well under a tenth of the population can direct a country of 1.4 billion people without ruling it directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Dickson, <i>The Party and the People: Chinese Politics in the 21st Century</i> (Princeton University Press, 2021), https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691216966.","Patricia M. Thornton, “Party All the Time: The CCP in Comparative and Historical Perspective,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 248, no. S1 (2021): 1–15, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741021000965. [Open access]","Daniel Koss, “Party Building as Institutional Bricolage: Asserting Authority at the Business Frontier,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 248, no. S1 (2021): 222–243, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741021000692. [Open access]","Margaret M. Pearson, Meg Rithmire, and Kellee S. Tsai, <i>The State and Capitalism in China</i> (Cambridge University Press, 2023), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009356732. [Open access]","Zhongyuan Wang, “Remaking Bonds: Adaptive Party Linkage-Building in Contemporary China,” <i>China: An International Journal</i> 21, no. 2 (2023): 114–135, https://doi.org/10.1353/chn.2023.a898344. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why place a decision-making body inside an organisation that already has managers?","a":"Because it separates authority from the job title. A manager answers for the firm's performance, while the cell answers to the party, so the two lines of accountability run in parallel and the second one wins."},{"q":"Why would a private company welcome one?","a":"Because it functions as a channel to the state. Approvals, land, credit and labour disputes all pass through officials, and a cell inside the firm is a relationship with those officials that competitors may lack."},{"q":"Why were they weakened during the reform era?","a":"Because people stopped depending on the party for work and housing. A cell has influence over those who need something from it, and a private labour market meant that most people no longer did."}]},
    "pea-093": {"abstract":"The <b>hukou</b> is the Chinese household registration system, which records every citizen at a particular place and classifies that registration as rural or urban.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Formalised at the end of the 1950s, it was one of the instruments that made the command economy governable, because it fixed the population in place and tied entitlements to location rather than to citizenship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under Mao it determined where a person lived, where they worked and what social services they could claim, and moving required an application to the party that was usually refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That immobility was central to political control, since people who cannot leave can be watched, and the neighbourhood and workplace organisations knew exactly who belonged where.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bruce Dickson treats this as one of three mechanisms of Maoist control, alongside dependence on the party for the necessities of life and its power to reward and punish individuals directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Economic reform did not abolish the system, and the result is the defining social division of contemporary China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hundreds of millions of people have moved from the countryside to the cities for work, but their registration stays where it was, so they remain outsiders in the places they have spent their working lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A migrant may be excluded from local health insurance, from subsidised housing and, most consequentially, from enrolling their children in urban public schools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Many families therefore leave children behind with grandparents, producing a population of tens of millions growing up apart from working parents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Successive governments have loosened the rules in smaller cities while keeping them tight in the largest ones, so the system has been reformed repeatedly without being dismantled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Dickson, <i>The Party and the People: Chinese Politics in the 21st Century</i> (Princeton University Press, 2021), https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691216966.","Xiaogang Wu and Donald J. Treiman, “The Household Registration System and Social Stratification in China, 1955–1996,” <i>Demography</i> 41, no. 2 (2004): 363–384, https://doi.org/10.1353/dem.2004.0010. [Open access]","Lei Kuang and Li Liu, “Discrimination against Rural-to-Urban Migrants: The Role of the Hukou System in China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 11 (2012): e46932, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0046932. [Open access]","Jason Hung, “Hukou System Influencing the Structural, Institutional Inequalities in China: The Multifaceted Disadvantages Rural Hukou Holders Face,” <i>Social Sciences</i> 11, no. 5 (2022): 194, https://doi.org/10.3390/socsci11050194. [Open access]","Dandan Zhang, Xin Li, and Jinjun Xue, “Education Inequality between Rural and Urban Areas of the People's Republic of China, Migrants' Children Education, and Some Implications,” <i>Asian Development Review</i> 32, no. 1 (2015): 196–224, https://doi.org/10.1162/adev_a_00042. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a planned economy need people to stay put?","a":"Because the plan allocated grain, housing and jobs by place. A population that moved freely would arrive where nothing had been allocated for it, so the registration was the accounting system as much as a police measure."},{"q":"Why keep it once the economy was opened?","a":"Because cities want the labour without the obligations. Migrant workers supply the workforce while their schools, hospitals and pensions remain, on paper, the responsibility of the villages they left."},{"q":"Why is exclusion from school the most damaging part?","a":"Because it decides the next generation rather than this one. A worker can tolerate poor housing for a decade, but a child sent home to be raised by grandparents inherits the rural registration and its disadvantages in turn."}]},
    "pea-094": {"abstract":"The <b>Great Leap Forward</b> was Mao Zedong's attempt from 1958 to reach industrial modernity in a few years by mobilising the rural population rather than by planning and investment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Villages were merged into enormous communes that were to run agriculture and industry together, with peasants smelting steel in backyard furnaces alongside the harvest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Mao launched it against the doubts of most of the party leadership, and it belongs to the leftist phase of a pattern that Bruce Dickson describes as alternating between ideological campaigns and periods of pragmatic development.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Enthusiasm was to substitute for expertise, so agricultural and industrial specialists were pushed aside in favour of politically reliable activists, and propaganda replaced material reward as the reason to work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Officials competed to report ever larger harvests, and because promotion depended on those figures rather than on the grain itself, the reporting became systematically false.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The consequence was famine on a scale without parallel in the modern world, lasting from 1959 to 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state continued to requisition grain against the inflated figures, and in some provinces it exported food while villages starved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Estimates of the dead range from roughly 15 to 45 million, with about 30 million the figure most often cited, and the variation reflects how contested the underlying population statistics remain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Survival varied enormously by locality, and research has found that villages with strong lineage organisations lost fewer people, because established networks of obligation moved food where the state's did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The party retreated to incentives and expert management in the early 1960s, and the resentment Mao felt towards the colleagues who organised that retreat became one of the motives for the Cultural Revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Dickson, <i>The Party and the People: Chinese Politics in the 21st Century</i> (Princeton University Press, 2021), https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691216966.","Jiarui Cao, Yiqing Xu, and Chuanchuan Zhang, “Clans and Calamity: How Social Capital Saved Lives during China's Great Famine,” <i>Journal of Development Economics</i> 157 (2022): 102865, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jdeveco.2022.102865. [Open access]","Chris Bramall, “Agency and Famine in China's Sichuan Province, 1958–1962,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 208 (2011): 990–1008, https://doi.org/10.1017/s030574101100110x. [Open access]","Robert Ash, “Squeezing the Peasants: Grain Extraction, Food Consumption and Rural Living Standards in Mao's China,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 188 (2006): 959–998, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741006000518. [Open access]","Cheng Huang et al., “Early Life Exposure to the 1959–1961 Chinese Famine Has Long-Term Health Consequences,” <i>The Journal of Nutrition</i> 140, no. 10 (2010): 1874–1878, https://doi.org/10.3945/jn.110.121293. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the reported harvests keep rising while people starved?","a":"Because officials were rewarded for the number rather than the grain. Once every level had an incentive to exceed the level below, the figures climbed on their own and nobody in the chain could safely correct them."},{"q":"Why did inflated figures turn a bad harvest into a catastrophe?","a":"Because the state took its share of a harvest that did not exist. Requisition was calculated from the reports, so the shortfall was extracted from what the villages actually needed to eat."},{"q":"Why would enthusiasm have been expected to substitute for expertise?","a":"Because Mao believed that political will was the decisive productive force. If human commitment rather than technique set the limit, then mobilising hundreds of millions of people should beat any planned target."}]},
    "pea-095": {"abstract":"The <b>Cultural Revolution</b> was the political upheaval Mao Zedong launched in 1966 against his own party, and it dominated Chinese life for a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its immediate cause was his dissatisfaction with the recovery policies adopted after the famine, which had restored material incentives and put technical experts back in charge, and which he regarded as a drift towards capitalism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the slogan that it is right to rebel, he encouraged students organised as Red Guards to criticise and remove officials accused of taking the capitalist road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Party leaders were paraded, beaten and imprisoned, universities closed, and enormous numbers of young people were later sent to the countryside to labour among the peasants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Deng Xiaoping, the architect of the policies that had provoked Mao, was among those purged, and many of the disgraced later returned to run the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The movement escaped its author almost at once, as rival Red Guard factions each claiming to be the true defenders of Mao fought one another with weapons seized from arsenals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By 1968 the country was close to civil war in places, and Mao called in the People's Liberation Army to restore order and disband the groups he had created.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Provincial and local campaigns took on lives of their own, in border regions producing persecutions of ethnic minorities that had little to do with the arguments in Beijing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What followed revealed the limits of the whole exercise, since Mao had no alternative design for organising power and simply rebuilt the party committees, frequently with the same people he had removed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By his death in 1976 the state and the population were exhausted by the swings between radical and developmental policy, which is the condition that made the reform era possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Dickson, <i>The Party and the People: Chinese Politics in the 21st Century</i> (Princeton University Press, 2021), https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691216966.","Jérôme Doyon and Long Yang, “Shades of Red: Changing Understandings of Political Loyalty in the Chinese Communist Party, 1921–2021,” <i>Journal of Current Chinese Affairs</i> 51, no. 3 (2022): 386–410, https://doi.org/10.1177/18681026221119220. [Open access]","Michael Schoenhals, “Cultural Revolution on the Border: Yunnan's Political Frontier Defence,” <i>The Copenhagen Journal of Asian Studies</i> 19 (2004): 27–54, https://doi.org/10.22439/cjas.v19i0.24. [Open access]","Martin Singer, <i>Educated Youth and the Cultural Revolution in China</i> (University of Michigan Press, 1971), https://doi.org/10.3998/mpub.19144. [Open access]","Alfreda Murck, “Mao's Golden Mangoes and the Cultural Revolution,” <i>eScholarship, University of California</i> (2013), https://escholarship.org/uc/item/51g6w28s. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why attack his own party rather than an external enemy?","a":"Because the party was where the resistance was. The officials running the recovery were the ones undoing his policies, and no outside opponent had the power to reverse a direction that his own colleagues had set."},{"q":"Why did factions all loyal to Mao end up fighting each other?","a":"Because loyalty was the only criterion and nobody could certify it. When any group can claim to be the true revolutionaries and the leader will not adjudicate, the question of which one is right can only be settled by force."},{"q":"Why did rebuilding the same committees expose the whole project?","a":"Because it showed there was no replacement. The movement could dismantle the apparatus but had nothing to put in its place, so power returned to the institution it had just spent years denouncing."}]},
    "pea-096": {"abstract":"<b>Reform and opening</b> is the name for the transformation of the Chinese economy that began when the Central Committee met in December 1978 and declared that class struggle was over and economic modernisation was now the party's central task.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the countryside the communes were dismantled in favour of family farming, and in the cities a private sector was permitted that grew from small shops and restaurants into some of the largest companies in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> China opened itself to foreign trade, investment and technology, reversing a generation of deliberate self-reliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The slogan that to get rich is glorious mattered more than it may sound, because it told people they would not be denounced as capitalist roaders for the behaviour the state was now requesting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deng's approach was a middle course between conservatives who wanted the orthodox Soviet plan restored and radicals who wanted planning abolished altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Instead of the rapid privatisation later attempted in post-Soviet Russia, markets and private ownership were tried in a few special economic zones while the rest of the economy stayed under the plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The market sector was then allowed to outgrow the planned one, supplying most new jobs, most foreign investment and most of the growth, so the plan shrank in relative terms rather than being abolished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The governing principle was economic liberalisation without political liberalisation, and no Chinese leader since has departed from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Bruce Dickson emphasises the cost to the party, since private employment, private housing and mass migration weakened the dependence, monitoring and punishment through which it had controlled society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those weakened controls are the background to the crisis of 1989 and to the party's long search since for a basis of legitimacy other than the command over daily life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Dickson, <i>The Party and the People: Chinese Politics in the 21st Century</i> (Princeton University Press, 2021), https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691216966.","Margaret M. Pearson, Meg Rithmire, and Kellee S. Tsai, <i>The State and Capitalism in China</i> (Cambridge University Press, 2023), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009356732. [Open access]","Alexander Dukalskis and Johannes Gerschewski, “Adapting or Freezing? Ideological Reactions of Communist Regimes to a Post-Communist World,” <i>Government and Opposition</i> 55, no. 3 (2018): 511–532, https://doi.org/10.1017/gov.2018.40. [Open access]","Heike Holbig and Bruce Gilley, “Reclaiming Legitimacy in China,” <i>Politics & Policy</i> 38, no. 3 (2010): 395–422, https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1747-1346.2010.00241.x. [Open access]","Patricia M. Thornton, “Party All the Time: The CCP in Comparative and Historical Perspective,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 248, no. S1 (2021): 1–15, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741021000965. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a slogan about getting rich need to be issued at all?","a":"Because the recent past made enrichment dangerous. People had watched neighbours punished for exactly the behaviour now being encouraged, and only an authoritative signal could make the new permission credible enough to act on."},{"q":"Why grow markets beside the plan instead of replacing it?","a":"Because nothing had to be destroyed for the experiment to proceed. If a zone failed it could be closed, and if it succeeded the results argued for themselves, which let reform advance without a decisive political fight."},{"q":"Why did economic reform weaken the party's grip on society?","a":"Because its authority had rested on being the only supplier. Once a person could find a job, a room and food without the party's permission, the levers of reward and punishment no longer reached them."}]},
    "pea-097": {"abstract":"The <b>Three Represents</b> is the doctrine associated with Jiang Zemin, which states that the Chinese Communist Party represents the advanced productive forces, advanced culture, and the fundamental interests of the overwhelming majority of the Chinese people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first of the three is the one that mattered, because advanced productive forces was understood by everyone as a euphemism for private entrepreneurs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party had traditionally claimed to represent three revolutionary classes, the workers, the farmers and the soldiers, and after the demonstrations of 1989 it had formally banned the recruitment of business owners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That ban had become untenable, since the private sector was producing most of the growth and most of the exports on which the party's own performance now rested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Local officials were in any case already admitting entrepreneurs, sometimes by classifying an obviously private firm as a collective enterprise so the paperwork would not contradict the rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The formula resolved that contradiction by changing the identity of the party rather than the behaviour of its officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was added to the party constitution in 2002 as Jiang retired, which placed it alongside the contributions of Marx, Lenin, Mao and Deng.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A party defined by the classes it represents cannot recruit capitalists, while a party defined by the interests of the overwhelming majority can recruit almost anyone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Bruce Dickson treats the episode as a demonstration that ideology in China is not the source of policy but the justification produced afterwards, and yet the party still considers that justification necessary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What was never on offer was the corresponding political change, since through all of this the party's monopoly on organisation was left exactly where it had always been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Dickson, <i>The Party and the People: Chinese Politics in the 21st Century</i> (Princeton University Press, 2021), https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691216966.","Émilie Frenkiel and Anna Shpakovskaya, “The Evolution of Representative Claim-Making by the Chinese Communist Party: From Mao to Xi, 1949–2019,” <i>Politics and Governance</i> 7, no. 3 (2019): 208–219, https://doi.org/10.17645/pag.v7i3.2151. [Open access]","Alexander Dukalskis and Johannes Gerschewski, “Adapting or Freezing? Ideological Reactions of Communist Regimes to a Post-Communist World,” <i>Government and Opposition</i> 55, no. 3 (2018): 511–532, https://doi.org/10.1017/gov.2018.40. [Open access]","Heike Holbig and Bruce Gilley, “Reclaiming Legitimacy in China,” <i>Politics & Policy</i> 38, no. 3 (2010): 395–422, https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1747-1346.2010.00241.x. [Open access]","Margaret M. Pearson, Meg Rithmire, and Kellee S. Tsai, <i>The State and Capitalism in China</i> (Cambridge University Press, 2023), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009356732. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a communist party need a formula to admit business owners?","a":"Because capitalists were, in its own doctrine, the class enemy. Admitting them without an explanation would have said that the doctrine no longer applied, which is a far larger admission than any single recruitment."},{"q":"Why were local officials already breaking the ban?","a":"Because their promotion depended on growth, and the entrepreneurs delivered it. A rule that obstructs the thing you are evaluated on gets worked around, and relabelling a firm was cheaper than losing it."},{"q":"Why keep producing ideology if it only follows the decision?","a":"Because the party's authority rests on being the correct interpreter of history rather than on an election. A policy with no doctrinal account of itself would concede that it governs by convenience alone."}]},
    "pea-098": {"abstract":"<b>Red capitalists</b> are private entrepreneurs who are also members of the Chinese Communist Party, and they include many former officials who left government for business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their existence answers a problem that any authoritarian regime faces, and a communist one especially, since independent wealth creates people who can act politically without needing the state's permission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After the demonstrations of 1989 the party responded by banning the recruitment of business owners altogether, which was orthodox but incompatible with its own commitment to growth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bruce Dickson describes the three techniques by which the party then brought the private sector under supervision instead, each of them recognisably Leninist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It required firms to join official business associations overseen by the department responsible for relations with non-party groups, it placed party cells inside private companies, and it co-opted the owners themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Co-optation is the part that produced the term, since entrepreneurs were brought into the party and given seats in local legislatures and advisory bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> These positions carry real commercial value, providing access to officials, to credit and to land, which is why they are sought rather than merely accepted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The result is a business class with a direct stake in the continuation of one-party rule, which is close to the opposite of what theories linking capitalism to democratisation would predict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether this represents the party capturing business or business capturing the party is genuinely debated, and the honest answer is that influence runs in both directions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under Xi Jinping the balance has tilted back towards the state, as prominent entrepreneurs have been investigated, disciplined or removed, and the political risk of great private wealth has become visible again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Dickson, <i>The Party and the People: Chinese Politics in the 21st Century</i> (Princeton University Press, 2021), https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691216966.","Margaret M. Pearson, Meg Rithmire, and Kellee S. Tsai, <i>The State and Capitalism in China</i> (Cambridge University Press, 2023), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009356732. [Open access]","Daniel Koss, “Party Building as Institutional Bricolage: Asserting Authority at the Business Frontier,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 248, no. S1 (2021): 222–243, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741021000692. [Open access]","Zhongyuan Wang, “Remaking Bonds: Adaptive Party Linkage-Building in Contemporary China,” <i>China: An International Journal</i> 21, no. 2 (2023): 114–135, https://doi.org/10.1353/chn.2023.a898344. [Open access]","Émilie Frenkiel and Anna Shpakovskaya, “The Evolution of Representative Claim-Making by the Chinese Communist Party: From Mao to Xi, 1949–2019,” <i>Politics and Governance</i> 7, no. 3 (2019): 208–219, https://doi.org/10.17645/pag.v7i3.2151. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is independent wealth a problem for an authoritarian state?","a":"Because money buys the things politics runs on without asking anyone. Someone who can fund lawyers, publications or organisations from their own resources does not need official approval to act, which is precisely what a monopoly on organisation forbids."},{"q":"Why did co-opting owners work better than excluding them?","a":"Because it changed what they wanted rather than what they could do. An entrepreneur whose licences, credit and political standing depend on the party has no interest in a transition that would put all three in doubt."},{"q":"Why has the balance shifted under Xi Jinping?","a":"Because co-optation always left the firms substantially in charge of themselves. Disciplining prominent owners restates which side of the arrangement is senior, at the cost of some of the confidence that made it attractive."}]},
    "pea-099": {"abstract":"<b>Advance of the state, retreat of the private</b> is the phrase Chinese commentators use for the period after 2008, when state-owned enterprises grew at the expense of private firms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It followed the global financial crisis, which China's leaders read as a failure of the Western model of lightly regulated markets rather than as an argument for adopting more of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Demand for Chinese exports fell sharply as its customers went into recession, and the government answered with a stimulus programme of a size no other country attempted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The great majority of that money reached state-owned enterprises, which could borrow from state banks on terms no private company was ever offered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many private firms went out of business as a result, in a sector that had been supplying most of the country's new jobs, most of its exports and most of its growth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The phrase matters because what it names is a reversal of direction rather than any single policy decision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For three decades the party had allowed the market sector to outgrow the planned one, and the whole post-Mao settlement rested on that direction of travel continuing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bruce Dickson sets the change beside a deadlock among the leadership, between those who wanted to go on stimulating the economy and those who wanted to restructure it, an argument conducted behind closed doors for most of a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under Xi Jinping the balance has tilted further still, with party organisations pushed into private companies and prominent entrepreneurs investigated or removed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether that is a retreat from reform or a correction of it is a live question, and it is asked inside China as sharply as anywhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Dickson, <i>The Party and the People: Chinese Politics in the 21st Century</i> (Princeton University Press, 2021), https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691216966.","Margaret M. Pearson, Meg Rithmire, and Kellee S. Tsai, <i>The State and Capitalism in China</i> (Cambridge University Press, 2023), https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009356732. [Open access]","Daniel Koss, “Party Building as Institutional Bricolage: Asserting Authority at the Business Frontier,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 248, no. S1 (2021): 222–243, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741021000692. [Open access]","Zhongyuan Wang, “Remaking Bonds: Adaptive Party Linkage-Building in Contemporary China,” <i>China: An International Journal</i> 21, no. 2 (2023): 114–135, https://doi.org/10.1353/chn.2023.a898344. [Open access]","Heike Holbig and Bruce Gilley, “Reclaiming Legitimacy in China,” <i>Politics & Policy</i> 38, no. 3 (2010): 395–422, https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1747-1346.2010.00241.x. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a stimulus favour state firms rather than the whole economy?","a":"Because the money went out through state banks to borrowers those banks already knew and were not blamed for lending to. A state enterprise carries an implicit guarantee that a private firm does not, so the safe loan and the political loan were the same loan."},{"q":"Why did the crisis strengthen the argument for state control?","a":"Because the countries that had urged China to liberalise were the ones in trouble. A model whose crash was visible on the news was a poor advertisement for itself, and China's slowdown without recession looked like evidence for the alternative."},{"q":"Why is a four-character phrase worth knowing as a term?","a":"Because it is how the reversal is discussed in China itself, including by people who dislike it. Naming a direction of travel makes it arguable, which is why the phrase spread well beyond the economists who coined it."}]},
    "pea-100": {"abstract":"<b>Reds and experts</b> names the recurring Chinese argument over whether officials should be appointed for political commitment or for technical skill, captured in the slogan that a cadre should be both red and expert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bruce Dickson uses the pair to explain the whole shape of the Mao era, which alternated between radical phases aimed at building a communist society and developmental phases aimed at economic growth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a radical phase the party appointed ideologues, who ran campaigns using propaganda and coercion, and in a developmental phase it appointed engineers, agronomists and managers, who used material incentives instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because personnel followed the line, each swing replaced the people the previous swing had promoted, and the losers of one campaign frequently became the victors of the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Great Leap Forward was the clearest case of enthusiasm displacing expertise, and the recovery that followed it the clearest case of the reverse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The deeper cost was not to any individual career but to the predictability of political life as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither officials nor ordinary people could know how long a policy would last, so supporting one enthusiastically today could be the evidence used against them tomorrow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reform era settled the argument decisively in favour of expertise, and the leadership that emerged under Jiang Zemin was dominated by technocrats trained in science and engineering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those leaders were problem solvers rather than believers, willing to adjust the system where it worked badly but not to reinvent it, and they supplied doctrinal justifications after the fact rather than deriving policy from doctrine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under Xi Jinping the political half of the formula has been revived, with compulsory study of the leader's speeches and renewed emphasis on loyalty, though nobody proposes returning to the alternation itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Dickson, <i>The Party and the People: Chinese Politics in the 21st Century</i> (Princeton University Press, 2021), https://doi.org/10.1515/9780691216966.","Jérôme Doyon and Long Yang, “Shades of Red: Changing Understandings of Political Loyalty in the Chinese Communist Party, 1921–2021,” <i>Journal of Current Chinese Affairs</i> 51, no. 3 (2022): 386–410, https://doi.org/10.1177/18681026221119220. [Open access]","Robert Ash, “Squeezing the Peasants: Grain Extraction, Food Consumption and Rural Living Standards in Mao's China,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 188 (2006): 959–998, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741006000518. [Open access]","Alexander Dukalskis and Johannes Gerschewski, “Adapting or Freezing? Ideological Reactions of Communist Regimes to a Post-Communist World,” <i>Government and Opposition</i> 55, no. 3 (2018): 511–532, https://doi.org/10.1017/gov.2018.40. [Open access]","Patricia M. Thornton, “Party All the Time: The CCP in Comparative and Historical Perspective,” <i>The China Quarterly</i> 248, no. S1 (2021): 1–15, https://doi.org/10.1017/s0305741021000965. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the party not simply want both qualities in the same person?","a":"Because it did, and that is what the slogan asks for. The difficulty is that the two are tested differently, and when a campaign has to choose quickly between a loyal amateur and a doubtful specialist, one of them must win."},{"q":"Why was the unpredictability worse than the wrong choice?","a":"Because it made every choice dangerous. An official who cannot tell whether zeal or caution will be rewarded next year has reason to do as little as possible, which is corrosive in a way that a single bad policy is not."},{"q":"Why did technocrats not simply abandon the ideology?","a":"Because the party's right to rule is stated in doctrinal terms. A leadership that governs pragmatically still has to explain its pragmatism as a development of the theory, or it forfeits the only account of why it should be obeyed."}]}
  };
  (window.CARD_EXTRA_IN = window.CARD_EXTRA_IN || []).push({ CARD_EXTRA: CARD_EXTRA });
})();
